window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("data-architecture", {"topics":[{"key":"data-architecture/ingestion-pipelines","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium","Official docs: Apache Airflow"],"why":"- **The first version is always a script.** List everything in Confluence or an S3 bucket, embed it,\n  upsert it, run it nightly. It works at 2,000 documents and fails in four predictable ways at 200,000.\n- **Cost scales with the corpus, not with change.** A nightly full re-crawl re-fetches, re-parses and\n  re-embeds documents that have not moved. The embedding bill and API rate limits become the bottleneck\n  long before the vector store does.\n- **Deletes are invisible, and crashes leave a mess.** A listing-based crawl only sees what exists now, so a\n  deleted document stays in the index and keeps being retrieved: a compliance bug for HR policies or\n  revoked contracts. A crash halfway leaves the index half new and half old, and the only recovery is\n  another full run.\n- **No raw copy means re-index equals re-crawl.** When the team changes the chunker or the embedding model,\n  there is nothing to re-transform from, so the work collides again with rate limits and with the source\n  team's goodwill.\n- **\"Make the script faster\" fixes none of this.** The fix is to separate *getting bytes out of sources*\n  (incremental, polite, durable) from *turning bytes into retrieval units* (cheap to re-run). This lesson\n  covers the first half; the ETL/ELT lesson in this module covers the second.","how":"### Classify each source by how it exposes change\nEvery connector design starts with one question: how does this source tell me what changed? Four answers,\nin rising order of fidelity and cost:\n\n| Mode | How | Catches deletes? | Cost and caveats |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Snapshot diff | List everything, hash each item, compare with the last snapshot | Yes | O(corpus) per run for the listing (not the content, if the source gives a cheap version or ETag); fine for a few hundred thousand items, wrong for a billion rows |\n| 2. Cursor / watermark | `WHERE updated_at > :last_seen`, or a provider change token (Microsoft Graph delta queries, Google Drive's changes feed) | Change tokens usually yes (Graph delta marks removed items); a timestamp cursor no | Cheap and incremental, but a plain cursor misses hard deletes and rows written with an earlier timestamp due to clock skew or long transactions |\n| 3. Change data capture (CDC) | Tail the transaction log (Postgres logical decoding, MySQL binlog) with a tool such as Debezium | Yes, every insert, update and delete as an ordered event | Highest fidelity; you operate a streaming system and need DBA cooperation |\n| 4. Push: webhooks, event streams | The source calls you | Only if it sends them | Lowest latency, but webhooks are at-least-once at best and often lossy, so treat them as a trigger to fetch, not the source of truth |\n\n- **Fix the cursor's blind spots.** Read with `>=` and a small overlap window, dedupe by id, and pair it with\n  a periodic snapshot diff that reconciles deletes.\n- **Choose from freshness needs and what the source offers, not taste.** A support knowledge base that\n  changes a few hundred times a day is fine on a 15-minute cursor poll with a nightly snapshot reconcile.\n  An orders table that drives agent decisions wants CDC.\n\n### Land raw bytes immutably\n- **What is written.** Raw bytes in object storage or a lakehouse table, plus a metadata record: `source`,\n  `source_id`, `source_version` (ETag, revision number or content hash), `fetched_at`, `acl` (who may read\n  it at the source) and the raw `uri`.\n- **Immutable.** A new version is a new row, not an overwrite.\n- **Why this is the most important choice.** Parsing, chunking and embedding are deterministic functions of\n  the raw bytes, so they can be replayed without touching the source again.\n\n### Make every stage idempotent\n1. **Key by natural identity.** The key is `(source, source_id)`.\n2. **Skip unchanged bytes by content hash,** even when the source claims an update, because many SaaS APIs\n   bump `updated_at` on permission changes or views.\n3. **Still compare and propagate the ACL** when bytes are identical, because a permission change with\n   identical bytes is exactly the update retrieval filtering depends on.\n4. **Write upserts keyed by identity; write deletes as tombstone rows** with the same key, so replaying any\n   window produces the same end state.\n5. **Commit the watermark *after* the batch's writes are durable.** Committing it first is the classic bug\n   that turns a crash into a silent gap.\n\n**The delivery guarantee, stated precisely:**\n- **No stage is exactly-once.** The overlap window, retries and redelivered webhooks mean the same change\n  can arrive twice.\n- **The pipeline is *at-least-once with idempotent effects*.** Re-applying a change keyed by\n  `(source, source_id, version)` leaves the store unchanged, so a duplicate does no harm.\n- **Commit order matters more than deduplication.** Duplicates are absorbed downstream; gaps are not, and a\n  gap is the only failure you must prevent.\n\n### Initial load, backfills and the handoff to incremental\n- **Two modes per connector:** the first full load and steady-state increments. The switch between them is\n  the dangerous moment.\n- **The gap.** Snapshot the source, then poll \"from now\", and changes made during the snapshot (which can\n  take hours for a large source) fall between the two.\n- **The fix.** Record the change position *before* the snapshot starts (cursor value, provider delta token,\n  or CDC log position). Start the incremental reader there; idempotent upserts absorb the overlap.\n- **Debezium works this way.** It takes a consistent initial snapshot, then streams from the log offset it\n  recorded at snapshot time.\n- **Run backfills in a separate lane** with their own rate-limit budget, such as re-ingesting a source after\n  a connector bug. Otherwise a multi-day backfill uses up the API quota and the incremental sync goes stale.\n\n### Treat source schemas as a contract that will break\n- **Sources drift.** SaaS APIs add fields, rename them and change pagination without notice; a database\n  team will eventually drop a column the connector reads.\n- **Validate at the landing boundary.** Check each fetched record against an expected schema; store unknown\n  fields in the raw record instead of dropping them.\n- **Quarantine failures** rather than letting them flow on with nulls. A connector that quietly writes\n  `title = null` for a whole source is worse than one that stops, because the index fills with untitled\n  chunks and nothing alarms. The data-contracts lesson covers making this explicit with the source owner.\n\n### Decouple fetch rate from processing rate\n- **A queue between connector and transforms** absorbs the mismatch between source rate limits and\n  parser/embedder throughput, and gives retries with backoff a place to live.\n- **A dead-letter queue** parks a poison document (a 2 GB PDF that crashes the parser) instead of stalling\n  the run.\n- **Match orchestration to the source.** Batch orchestration (Airflow, Dagster, Prefect) suits snapshot and\n  cursor sources; a streaming consumer suits CDC and webhooks. Micro-batches every few minutes are the\n  common middle ground.\n\n### Carry ACLs and lineage as data\n- **ACLs are a first-class field** travelling with the document, because retrieval must filter on them\n  later and a pipeline that drops them cannot be fixed at query time.\n- **Lineage** (which raw version a chunk came from, which pipeline version produced it) is what makes a\n  selective re-run possible.\n\n### Observe the pipeline as a pipeline\n- **Per stage:** items in, items out, items failed.\n- **Lag:** `now − last_committed_watermark`.\n- **Change ratio alarms:** zero means the connector is silently broken; near 100 % means the source migrated\n  and every id changed.\n- These three numbers answer \"is the index fresh?\" without reading the index.","example":"### Incremental connector loop for a cursor-based source\nIt reads with an overlap window, dedupes by id, skips re-embedding unchanged content by hash while still\npropagating ACL changes, writes tombstones for deletes reported by the source, and commits the watermark\nonly after the batch is durable.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, datetime as dt\n\nOVERLAP = dt.timedelta(minutes=5)          # tolerate clock skew / late commits at the source\nEPOCH = dt.datetime(1970, 1, 1, tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)\n\ndef sync_once(source, landing, state):\n    wm = state.get(\"watermark\")                              # tz-aware datetime, or None on first run\n    since = wm - OVERLAP if wm else EPOCH\n    seen, newest = set(), wm or EPOCH\n    for item in source.list_changes(since=since):          # yields {id, updated_at, deleted, acl, fetch}\n        if item[\"id\"] in seen:\n            continue                                         # same id twice in one listing (paged API)\n        seen.add(item[\"id\"])\n        newest = max(newest, item[\"updated_at\"])\n        key = (source.name, item[\"id\"])\n        if item[\"deleted\"]:\n            landing.write_tombstone(key, at=item[\"updated_at\"])   # idempotent if re-delivered\n            continue\n        raw = item[\"fetch\"]()                                # bytes; respects source rate limits\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()\n        if landing.latest_hash(key) == digest:               # overlap re-delivery or metadata-only bump\n            if landing.latest_acl(key) != item.get(\"acl\"):\n                landing.update_acl(key, item.get(\"acl\"))     # permissions changed: no re-embed needed\n                landing.emit_event(\"document.acl_changed\", key=key)\n            continue\n        landing.write_version(key, raw, hash=digest, acl=item.get(\"acl\"),\n                              fetched_at=dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc))\n        landing.emit_event(\"document.changed\", key=key, hash=digest)\n    landing.flush()                                          # make writes durable first ...\n    state[\"watermark\"] = newest                              # ... then advance the cursor\n    return len(seen)\n```\n\nA nightly snapshot-diff job runs alongside this loop to catch deletes the source never reported, which is\nthe standard pairing for sources that only expose `updated_at`.\n\n### The nightly snapshot diff that catches deletes\nRunnable as-is. It compares tonight's `{id: content_hash}` listing with last night's and reports the change\nratio you would alarm on.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\ndef snapshot_diff(previous, current):\n    \"\"\"previous/current: {source_id: content_hash}. Returns (created, updated, deleted).\"\"\"\n    prev_ids, cur_ids = set(previous), set(current)\n    created = sorted(cur_ids - prev_ids)\n    deleted = sorted(prev_ids - cur_ids)                       # the only way a listing reveals deletes\n    updated = sorted(i for i in cur_ids & prev_ids if previous[i] != current[i])\n    return created, updated, deleted\n\ndef h(text):\n    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()\n\nunchanged  = {f\"page-{i}\": h(f\"doc {i}\") for i in range(5, 15)}          # 10 pages nobody touched\nlast_night = {**unchanged, \"page-1\": h(\"Leave policy v1\"), \"page-2\": h(\"Expenses\"), \"page-3\": h(\"Old VPN guide\")}\ntonight    = {**unchanged, \"page-1\": h(\"Leave policy v2\"), \"page-2\": h(\"Expenses\"), \"page-4\": h(\"New VPN guide\")}\n\ncreated, updated, deleted = snapshot_diff(last_night, tonight)\nprint(\"created:\", created)    # ['page-4']\nprint(\"updated:\", updated)    # ['page-1']\nprint(\"deleted:\", deleted)    # ['page-3'] -> write a tombstone for it\nratio = (len(created) + len(updated) + len(deleted)) / max(len(last_night), 1)\nprint(f\"change ratio: {ratio:.0%}\")   # 23%; alarm if 0% for days or near 100%\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: full re-crawl vs incremental at 200,000 documents\nIllustrative orders of magnitude, not a benchmark: assume 200,000 documents averaging 2,000 tokens, and\n1% of documents changing per day.\n\n| Design | Documents re-embedded per day | Tokens embedded per day | Source API calls per day |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nightly full re-crawl | 200,000 | 400 million | every document fetched |\n| Cursor + content hash | about 2,000 | about 4 million | changed items plus a cheap listing |\n| Ratio | 100x fewer | 100x fewer | the full fetch disappears |\n\n- **The bill tracks the change rate, not the corpus.** At a 1% daily change rate the incremental design does\n  about 1% of the embedding work.\n- **Deletes still need the listing.** The nightly snapshot diff touches only ids and versions, not content.","patterns":["**Snapshot-then-stream for CDC sources.** When a database table drives decisions, run Debezium with an initial snapshot (its `snapshot.mode` setting, `initial` by default) so it records the log offset before streaming. Monitor replication-slot lag in `pg_replication_slots`, because a stalled consumer makes Postgres retain WAL and fill the disk.","**Cursor poll plus nightly reconcile for SaaS.** For wikis and ticketing tools, poll every 15 minutes with a 5-minute overlap and a content hash, and run a snapshot diff nightly for deletes. Monitor freshness lag and the tombstones emitted per reconcile; a reconcile that keeps finding deletes means the cursor is missing them.","**Dead-letter queue with a retry cap.** Put a queue between fetch and parse, retry with exponential backoff, and move a message to a DLQ after a fixed number of receives (for SQS, the redrive policy's `maxReceiveCount`). Alert on DLQ depth above zero for more than one run.","**Separate backfill lane.** Run backfills as their own job with a lower share of the source's rate limit, and with `max_active_runs=1` on an Airflow DAG so runs do not pile up. Monitor incremental lag while the backfill runs; it must stay inside the freshness SLA.","**Pipeline health dashboard.** Per connector, chart items in/out/failed per stage, `now − watermark`, and change ratio per run, with alarms at zero change for N runs and near 100 % change."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source system\"] -->|\"cursor read with overlap\"| C[\"Connector\"]\n    S -.->|\"nightly full listing\"| R[\"Snapshot diff reconcile\"]\n    C --> H{\"Content hash changed?\"}\n    H -->|\"no\"| A[\"Compare and propagate ACL\"]\n    H -->|\"yes\"| L[(\"Immutable raw landing\")]\n    R -->|\"tombstones for vanished ids\"| L\n    L --> Q[\"Queue to parse, chunk, embed\"]\n    Q -.->|\"poison file\"| D[\"Dead-letter queue\"]\n    L -->|\"flush durable\"| W[\"Commit watermark\"]","caption":"Changes flow from the source into an immutable landing store; the watermark advances only after writes are\ndurable, and a nightly snapshot diff catches deletes the cursor misses."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"at-least-once with idempotent effects\" mean for an ingestion pipeline, and why aim for it?","a":"- **Definition:** a change may be delivered more than once (overlap windows, retries, redelivered\n  webhooks), but applying it twice leaves the store unchanged because writes are keyed by\n  `(source, source_id, version)`.\n- **Why:** exactly-once across a SaaS API, a queue and a vector store is not achievable in practice, while\n  idempotent upserts make duplicates harmless.\n- **The consequence:** the only failure left to prevent is a gap, which is why the watermark commit order\n  matters more than deduplication."},{"q":"Design ingestion for SharePoint, Jira and a Postgres orders table, with deletes and permissions.","a":"- **Per-source change detection:** Graph delta queries for SharePoint, an `updated` cursor with overlap plus a\n  nightly snapshot diff for Jira, Debezium CDC for the orders table.\n- **One landing schema:** immutable raw bytes plus `source`, `source_id`, `source_version`, `fetched_at`,\n  `acl` and `uri`; deletes become tombstones.\n- **Shared plumbing:** a queue with a DLQ before parsing, watermark committed after flush, and per-source lag\n  and change-ratio alarms."},{"q":"Users say an edited policy page still shows the old text in answers. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the landing store first:** is there a new version for that `source_id`? If not, the fault is in\n  the connector (cursor gap, clock skew, watermark committed too early).\n- **If the version landed,** follow lineage downstream: was a `document.changed` event emitted, did parsing\n  fail into the DLQ, or did the index upsert miss?\n- **Then fix the class of bug:** add an overlap window, reorder the commit, or alarm on DLQ depth."},{"q":"When would you not use CDC?","a":"- **When the source changes slowly:** a wiki changing a few hundred times a day is well served by a cursor\n  poll plus a nightly reconcile.\n- **When you lack log access or DBA buy-in,** because CDC needs logical decoding or binlog access and a\n  streaming platform to run.\n- **Use it** when the table drives agent decisions and needs ordered, delete-inclusive changes in seconds."},{"q":"Your embedding bill doubled but the corpus barely changed. What do you look at?","a":"- **Change ratio per run:** a jump suggests re-embedding on `updated_at` bumps without a content-hash check,\n  often caused by permission edits or view counts.\n- **Id churn:** a change ratio near 100 % means the source migrated and every id changed, so everything looks\n  new.\n- **Backfills:** an unplanned re-ingest in the main lane re-embeds everything; move it to its own budget."}],"tradeoffs":["**Snapshot diff: simplicity vs listing cost.** It catches deletes with no source cooperation, but listing cost grows with the corpus. It flips to cursor-plus-reconcile once a full listing takes longer than the freshness window.","**CDC: exactness vs operating cost.** It gives exact, ordered, delete-inclusive change streams, but needs log access, DBA buy-in and a streaming platform. For a source changing a few hundred times a day, polling is cheaper to run.","**Every raw version: easy replays vs storage.** Storing all versions makes replays and audits trivial at the cost of storage. When documents are large binaries that change often, keep only the last N versions and rely on lineage for the rest.","**Streaming vs micro-batch.** Streaming cuts freshness lag to seconds but spreads work evenly, which is worse for embedding APIs that are cheaper and faster in large batches. Micro-batching usually wins unless the use case is truly real-time."],"pitfalls":["**Advancing the watermark before writes are durable.** Symptom: documents missing from the index after a crash, with no error in any log. Fix: flush, then commit the cursor.","**A timestamp cursor without an overlap window.** Symptom: sporadic missed updates traced to rows committed late or from a clock-skewed replica. Fix: read with `>=` and an overlap, dedupe by id.","**Never handling deletes.** Symptom: an answer citing a document that no longer exists at the source, often discovered by a customer rather than a dashboard. Fix: tombstones plus a snapshot reconcile.","**Dropping source ACLs \"to simplify\".** Symptom: a user retrieves a document they could not open in the source system, which is a security incident, not a bug. Fix: carry ACLs as a first-class field.","**Re-fetching on every `updated_at` bump without a content hash.** Symptom: a large embedding bill for a corpus that barely changes, because the SaaS source bumps timestamps on permission edits. The opposite error, skipping hash-identical items entirely, silently drops those permission edits.","**Starting the incremental cursor when the initial snapshot *finished*.** Symptom: documents edited during the first load stay at their old version until someone edits them again. Fix: record the position when the snapshot started.","**No dead-letter path.** Symptom: a nightly run fails repeatedly on the same oversized or malformed file while thousands of healthy documents go stale. Fix: a DLQ with a retry cap."],"when":"- **Use when** a corpus has more than one source, changes while the system is live, or must respect\n  deletions and permissions, which is to say anything beyond a demo.\n- **Avoid** CDC for a wiki that changes twice a day, and reject a \"full re-crawl nightly\" design for a corpus\n  whose embedding cost per full pass exceeds the monthly vector store bill.\n- **Measure** freshness lag, change ratio per run, per-stage failure counts and embedding spend per run;\n  those four numbers expose nearly every ingestion defect.\n- **Day to day,** this is the code that pages you at 3 a.m. when a source's API changes.\n- **Interviews:** \"design ingestion for SharePoint plus Jira plus a Postgres orders table, with deletes and\n  permissions\". Walk through change detection per source, immutable raw landing, idempotent upserts and\n  tombstones, and the watermark commit order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes connectors with cursors, overlap windows, content hashing and tombstones, and instruments lag and change ratio so a silent connector failure is visible within one polling interval.","architect":"Decides per source between snapshot diff, cursor polling, CDC and push, defines the landing-store schema including ACL and lineage fields, and sets the freshness SLA that justifies the cost.","fde":"Negotiates API access, rate limits and service accounts with the customer's source-system owners, and proves deletions and permission changes propagate before go-live, because that is the question their security team will ask."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG index is built by a nightly job that lists every page in their wiki and embeds\npages whose `updated_at` changed. Users report that deleted pages still appear in answers and that some\nedits never show up. Diagnose both symptoms and describe the minimal redesign.","a":"Deleted pages persist because a listing of current pages can only report what exists; nothing\never emits a delete for a page that is gone, so the index keeps its chunks. Missed edits come from the\ntimestamp cursor: a page saved just before the previous run's watermark but committed after the listing\nwas taken, or saved on a replica with skewed time, falls below the cursor and is never re-read. The\nminimal fix keeps the nightly job but makes it a snapshot diff: hash the current page list and content\nversions, compare with the stored snapshot, and emit tombstones for ids that disappeared and updates for\nchanged hashes. Add an overlap window to the cursor read and dedupe by id, commit the watermark only\nafter writes are durable, and store the raw page bytes so chunker changes can be replayed without\nre-crawling the wiki."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the ingestion watermark be committed only after the batch's writes are durable?","options":["Because the watermark must be the largest timestamp in the batch, which is unknown until the end","Because a crash between committing the watermark and persisting the writes would silently skip those items on the next run","Because committing earlier would cause duplicate writes in the landing store","Because the source system rejects queries with a watermark newer than its own clock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the cursor advances first and the process dies before the writes land, the next run starts after the lost items and nothing ever re-reads them. Duplicates are the safe failure; gaps are the silent one."},{"q":"A source exposes only an `updated_at` field and a list-all endpoint. Which design catches deletes?","options":["Polling `updated_at` with an overlap window and deduplicating by id","Subscribing to the source's webhooks and treating each event as authoritative","Running a periodic snapshot diff of the listed ids against the previously stored snapshot","Hashing document content and skipping items whose hash has not changed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timestamp cursor never sees a row that no longer exists, and webhooks are lossy. Only comparing the set of ids present now with the set stored last time reveals which ids vanished, so tombstones can be emitted for them."},{"q":"What is the main reason to store immutable raw bytes in a landing store rather than only the derived chunks?","options":["Raw bytes compress better than chunk text in object storage","Vector databases cannot store the original document","Parsing, chunking and embedding are re-run far more often than sources change, and replaying from raw avoids re-crawling","Regulators require a copy of every source document"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The transformation steps are where experimentation and model upgrades happen. With a raw copy, a new chunker is a local re-run; without it, every change becomes a full re-crawl against rate-limited sources."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/etl-elt-for-rag","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: dbt","Anthropic, 2024 — Introducing Contextual Retrieval"],"why":"- **The terms come from data warehousing.** ETL was the norm when warehouse storage and compute were\n  expensive: clean and shape data on a separate server, load only the final tables. ELT took over when\n  cloud warehouses and lakes made storage cheap and put elastic compute next to the data: load everything\n  raw, transform with SQL (the dbt workflow), keep every intermediate table.\n- **ELT won because transformations are where requirements change.** With raw data kept, a changed\n  transformation is a re-run, not a re-extraction.\n- **RAG rediscovers this the hard way.** Version one is one function: fetch, split into 512-token chunks,\n  embed, upsert. Then the team wants semantic chunking, section titles on chunks, a new embedding model, or\n  an LLM-written summary per chunk (contextual retrieval), and each means re-running from the source.\n- **Nothing in between was kept.** No way to A/B two chunkers on the same parsed text, no intermediate to\n  inspect when a failure traces to a garbled chunk, no embedding cache, so the second attempt costs as much\n  as the first.\n- **A vector database is a poor transformation engine.** It cannot join, deduplicate or re-chunk, so\n  warehouse-style \"transform inside the target\" is not an option. Treat the chunk and embedding tables as\n  the data product and the index as a projection of them.","how":"### The stage tables\nA chain of derived tables, each keyed so it can be recomputed incrementally:\n\n| # | Table | Key and contents | Why it is stored |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `documents` | raw bytes (or a pointer) plus `doc_id`, `doc_version` (content hash), source metadata including ACLs | produced by ingestion; immutable per version |\n| 2 | `parsed` | text plus structure (headings, tables, page numbers) per `(doc_id, doc_version, parser_version)` | parsing is the most expensive and most bug-prone step |\n| 3 | `chunks` | `chunk_id`, `doc_id`, `doc_version`, `chunker_version`, `position`, `text`, `section_path`, `metadata` | the retrieval unit; versioned ids make orphans findable |\n| 4 | `enriched` (optional) | chunk text plus title and section path, extracted entities, or a short LLM-written context sentence (contextual retrieval) | enrichment calls an LLM and is the step most likely to be re-tuned |\n| 5 | `embeddings` | `(chunk_id, embedding_model, vector)`, cached by `(embedding_model, text_hash)` | an unchanged chunk is never embedded twice; two models can coexist during a migration |\n| 6 | the index | upsert of `embeddings` joined to `chunks` metadata, keyed by `chunk_id`, then delete ids absent from the current `chunks` for that document | serving structure, rebuildable from 3 to 5 |\n\n- **Content-addressed chunk ids.** `chunk_id = hash(doc_id, doc_version, chunker_version, position)`, so a\n  new chunker produces new ids and old chunks are found and deleted as orphans rather than overwritten in\n  place.\n- **Each table is a deterministic function of the previous one plus a version parameter.** That is what\n  makes the pipeline incremental.\n- **A `chunker_version` change** recomputes only `chunks`, `enriched`, `embeddings` and the index: for all\n  documents, but from `parsed`, not from the source.\n- **One document change** flows only that document's rows through every stage.\n\n### Why materialize instead of streaming through\nStoring intermediates costs disk and adds a write per stage. It buys four things:\n- **Debuggability.** A bad answer traces to a chunk, the chunk to its parsed text, the parsed text to the raw\n  bytes, each inspectable.\n- **Experimentation.** Two chunkers or two embedding models run against the same `parsed` table and are\n  compared with the same retrieval eval set.\n- **Cost control.** Embedding and LLM enrichment, the expensive calls, are cached by content hash and never\n  repeated for unchanged text.\n- **Late binding.** The embedding model is a parameter of the last stage, so upgrading it re-runs one stage,\n  not the pipeline.\n\n### Where ETL still applies\n- **Some transforms must happen before anything is stored** (ETL in the strict sense): redacting PII that\n  may not be persisted, dropping documents whose classification forbids them here, and enforcing tenant\n  boundaries at the landing store.\n- **The rule:** compliance-driven transformations go before the load; quality-driven transformations go\n  after it.\n\n### Quality gates between stages\nEvery stage always produces *something*, so failures are silent unless measured. Standard gates:\n- **Characters per page** from the parser (a scanned PDF parsed as text yields near zero).\n- **Empty or near-duplicate chunk ratio.**\n- **Embedding failure rate.**\n- **Orphan count** after index reconciliation.\n\nA gate that trips stops promotion of that stage's output, which is cheaper than finding out from users.\n\n### Orchestration and incrementality\n- **A DAG scheduler** (Dagster, Airflow, Prefect, or dbt-style SQL models over a lakehouse) runs stages in\n  dependency order on partitions of changed documents.\n- **Index writes are idempotent upserts by `chunk_id`,** so a retried stage is harmless. The version columns\n  make \"re-run everything from `parsed` with chunker v3\" a one-line change rather than a project.\n- **Recompute like a build system.** Each row's key includes the hash of its inputs plus the version of the\n  function that produced it; a stage runs only for keys whose output row does not exist yet.\n\n| Change | New `doc_version`? | Where new keys appear |\n|---|---|---|\n| A document edit | yes | every stage, that document only |\n| A chunker change | no, new `chunker_version` | from `chunks` onward, every document |\n| An embedding-model change | no | only `embeddings` |\n\nNothing has to track \"what changed\"; the missing keys are the work list.\n\n### Not every change needs a re-embed\n- **Metadata-only changes** (an ACL edit, a move to a new folder, a retention label) go to the index as a\n  payload update keyed by `doc_id`, without re-chunking or re-embedding, because the vectors did not\n  change.\n- **So the pipeline needs two paths:** a content path through every stage and a metadata path straight to\n  the index payload.\n- **Without the metadata path,** a permission change either triggers an expensive full re-run or never\n  reaches the index, and the second outcome is a security bug.","example":"### A content-addressed transform stage\nChunk ids include the chunker version, embeddings are cached by `(model, text_hash)`, and index\nreconciliation deletes orphaned chunks of re-chunked documents. The stores are stand-ins for lakehouse\ntables and a vector database.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\nCHUNKER_VERSION, EMBED_MODEL = \"v3-semantic\", \"text-embedding-3-small\"\n\ndef h(*parts):\n    return hashlib.sha256(\"|\".join(map(str, parts)).encode()).hexdigest()[:24]\n\ndef build_chunks(doc_id, doc_version, parsed_sections, chunker):\n    rows = []\n    for pos, (section_path, text) in enumerate(chunker(parsed_sections)):\n        rows.append({\"chunk_id\": h(doc_id, doc_version, CHUNKER_VERSION, pos),\n                     \"doc_id\": doc_id, \"doc_version\": doc_version,\n                     \"chunker_version\": CHUNKER_VERSION, \"position\": pos,\n                     \"text\": f\"{section_path}\\n{text}\",            # cheap enrichment: section path\n                     \"text_hash\": h(section_path, text)})\n    return rows\n\ndef embed_stage(chunks, cache, embed_fn):\n    todo = [c for c in chunks if (EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"]) not in cache]\n    for c, vec in zip(todo, embed_fn([c[\"text\"] for c in todo])):   # batched, rate-limited call\n        cache[(EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"])] = vec\n    return [(c[\"chunk_id\"], cache[(EMBED_MODEL, c[\"text_hash\"])], c) for c in chunks]\n\ndef reconcile_index(index, doc_id, rows):\n    live = {cid for cid, _, _ in rows}\n    for cid, vec, meta in rows:\n        index.upsert(cid, vec, {k: meta[k] for k in (\"doc_id\", \"doc_version\", \"position\")})\n    stale = index.ids_for_doc(doc_id) - live                       # chunks from an older chunker version\n    index.delete(stale)\n    return {\"upserted\": len(live), \"deleted\": len(stale)}\n```\n\nSwitching chunkers is now `CHUNKER_VERSION = \"v4\"` plus a re-run from the parsed table. Unchanged text\nstill hits the embedding cache, and `reconcile_index` removes the v3 chunks of each document as its v4\nchunks land. Three details in the code are deliberate, plus one caveat:\n- **Upsert before delete.** A reader can briefly see old and new chunks of a document together, which is\n  better than briefly seeing none of them.\n- **Source deletion needs no special path.** Calling `reconcile_index(index, doc_id, [])` deletes every chunk\n  of that document.\n- **`text_hash` leaves out `doc_version`.** Edit one paragraph of a 40-chunk document and all 40 chunk ids\n  change because the version changed, but 39 texts hash the same, so only one embedding call is made.\n- **Caveat: that holds for structure-aligned chunking** (boundaries follow sections and paragraphs). With fixed-size\n  token windows, an edit that changes text length moves every later boundary, so every later chunk gets a\n  new hash. Chunk-boundary stability is a cost property as well as a quality one.\n\n### Missing keys are the work list\nRunnable as-is. Output keys include input hashes and versions, so the set of keys not yet materialized is\nexactly what each kind of change must recompute.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\ndef key(*parts):\n    return hashlib.sha256(\"|\".join(map(str, parts)).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef work_list(docs, chunker_version, embed_model, done):\n    \"\"\"docs: {doc_id: doc_version}. done: output keys already materialized. Missing keys = work.\"\"\"\n    todo = {\"chunks\": [], \"embeddings\": []}\n    for doc_id, doc_version in docs.items():\n        ck = key(\"chunks\", doc_id, doc_version, chunker_version)\n        if ck not in done:\n            todo[\"chunks\"].append(doc_id)\n        if key(\"embeddings\", ck, embed_model) not in done:\n            todo[\"embeddings\"].append(doc_id)\n    return todo\n\ndocs = {\"a\": \"h1\", \"b\": \"h2\", \"c\": \"h3\"}\ndone = set()\nfor d, v in docs.items():                                   # chunker v3 + model-1 already ran\n    ck = key(\"chunks\", d, v, \"v3\")\n    done |= {ck, key(\"embeddings\", ck, \"model-1\")}\n\nprint(work_list(docs, \"v3\", \"model-1\", done))   # nothing to do\nprint(work_list(docs, \"v3\", \"model-2\", done))   # model change: embeddings only, every doc\nprint(work_list(docs, \"v4\", \"model-1\", done))   # chunker change: chunks onward, every doc\ndocs[\"b\"] = \"h2-edited\"                          # one document edited\nprint(work_list(docs, \"v3\", \"model-1\", done))   # only b, in every stage\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what a full pass costs at 2 million documents\nThe cache matters most for the expensive optional stage. Take 2 million documents at 4,000 tokens and 12\nchunks each.\n\n| Quantity | Arithmetic | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chunks | 2 M × 12 | 24 million |\n| Embedding pass (each chunk read once, ~400 tokens) | 24 M × 400 | ≈ 10 billion tokens |\n| Contextual enrichment (whole document as context per chunk) | 24 M × 4,400 | ≈ 100 billion input tokens, plus an output sentence per chunk |\n| Enrichment vs embedding | 100 B ÷ 10 B | about ten times the embedding pass |\n\n- **Prompt caching of the shared document prefix** reduces the enrichment bill.\n- **A content-hash cache** means a chunker change does not repeat enrichment for text that did not move.\n- **Run this arithmetic** before choosing which stages to re-run and how often.","patterns":["**Embedding cache as a table, not a dict.** When any stage is re-run, look up `(embedding_model, text_hash)` in a lakehouse table before calling the provider and write misses back. Monitor cache hit rate per run; a re-run after a chunker change with a near-zero hit rate means boundaries moved.","**Versioned index plus alias cutover.** For a model or chunker upgrade, build a second collection or index and point a read alias at it after the retrieval eval wins (Qdrant collection aliases and OpenSearch index aliases both do this). Keep the old one for rollback and monitor retrieval eval scores and answer quality across the switch.","**Quality gates as orchestrator checks.** Express parse yield, empty-chunk ratio and orphan count as Dagster asset checks or dbt tests that block downstream materialization. Monitor gate failures per source, because one bad source usually explains most failures.","**Batch the expensive calls.** Send bulk re-embeds through a provider's batch endpoint (OpenAI's Batch API accepts embedding requests at a discount) and put the full document first in contextual-enrichment prompts so prompt caching applies. Monitor tokens and spend per run against the arithmetic above.","**Separate metadata path.** Route ACL and label changes to a payload update keyed by `doc_id` instead of the content DAG. Monitor the lag between a source permission change and the index payload update."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"documents: raw + ACL\")] --> P[(\"parsed\")]\n    P --> G1{\"Parse yield gate\"}\n    G1 -->|\"pass\"| C[(\"chunks, versioned ids\")]\n    C --> E[(\"enriched, optional\")]\n    E --> M[(\"embeddings cache by model + text hash\")]\n    M --> I[\"Vector index: upsert then delete orphans\"]\n    D -.->|\"ACL or label change only\"| U[\"Payload update by doc_id\"]\n    U --> I\n    I -.->|\"rebuildable from tables\"| C","caption":"Each stage is a stored table keyed by input hash plus version; the content path runs every stage, while\nmetadata-only changes skip straight to the index payload."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between ETL and ELT, and why does RAG favour ELT?","a":"- **ETL** transforms on a separate server and loads only final tables; **ELT** loads raw and transforms next\n  to the data, keeping intermediates.\n- **RAG favours ELT** because chunking, enrichment and embedding change far more often than sources, so\n  re-transforming from stored raw or parsed data is the operation you run most.\n- **The exception** is compliance: PII that may not be persisted must be redacted before the load."},{"q":"After a chunker change, answers cite duplicated passages. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check chunk ids:** if they come from `(doc_id, position)` only, the new chunker overwrote some rows and\n  left the old tail live.\n- **Check reconciliation:** count indexed ids per document that are absent from the current `chunks`; a\n  non-zero orphan count confirms it.\n- **Fix:** version the ids with `chunker_version`, upsert then delete stale ids per document, and add an\n  orphan-count gate."},{"q":"When is materializing every stage not worth it?","a":"- **A small static corpus** of a few thousand files rebuilt from scratch on any change gains little from\n  stage tables; keep only the raw copy.\n- **It pays off** once the team changes a chunker or embedding model a second time, or must audit why an\n  answer cited a chunk."},{"q":"Estimate the cost of adding contextual retrieval to 2 million documents.","a":"- **Chunks:** 2 M documents × 12 chunks = 24 million.\n- **Input tokens:** each call carries the document (~4,000) plus the chunk (~400), so about 24 M × 4,400 ≈\n  100 billion tokens, roughly ten times a full embedding pass.\n- **Levers:** prompt caching of the document prefix, a content-hash cache so re-chunking does not repeat\n  unchanged text, and applying enrichment only where evals show it helps."},{"q":"How do you propagate a permission change without re-embedding?","a":"- **Treat it as metadata:** the vectors did not change, so send a payload update keyed by `doc_id` straight\n  to the index.\n- **Why a separate path matters:** without it, the change either triggers a full re-run or never reaches the\n  index, and the second outcome is a security bug.\n- **Monitor** the lag from source permission change to index payload update."}],"tradeoffs":["**Materialized stages vs storage.** Every stage costs storage and one extra write, but it flips to a clear win as soon as the team changes a chunker or embedding model for the second time.","**ELT flexibility vs compliance.** Raw data stays available for any future transformation, but rules that forbid storing certain content force those specific transformations back before the load.","**Content-addressed ids vs re-embed cost.** They make orphans detectable and re-runs idempotent, yet any chunker version change re-embeds the whole corpus; a cache keyed by text hash limits that to chunks whose text actually changed.","**LLM enrichment vs pipeline cost.** Summaries and contextual prefixes can raise retrieval quality noticeably, but add an LLM call per chunk per version, which dominates pipeline cost at corpus scale unless cached and applied selectively."],"pitfalls":["**Chunk ids from `(doc_id, position)` only, without orphan reconciliation.** Symptom after a chunker change: old and new chunks of one document both rank in the top-k, often overlapping, because a new chunker that emits fewer chunks overwrites positions 0..n−1 and leaves the old tail live. Even with reconciliation, position-only ids overwrite in place, so a half-finished re-run leaves mixed chunker versions with no way to tell rows apart.","**Mixing vectors from two embedding models in one index.** Symptom: retrieval quality drops right after a model \"upgrade\", because similarity scores from different models are not comparable.","**Embedding before near-duplicate removal.** Symptom: the top-k fills with copies of the same boilerplate paragraph and the useful passage is pushed below the cutoff.","**Transforming inside the vector database's ingest hooks.** Symptom: a re-index that takes days and a pipeline that cannot move to another vendor without a rewrite.","**No quality gate after parsing.** Symptom: a retrieval failure traced, weeks later, to thousands of chunks containing nothing but page headers or OCR noise.","**Treating the index as the system of record.** Symptom: an outage or a vendor migration that requires re-crawling every source because the chunk tables never existed."],"when":"- **Use** staged ELT for any RAG corpus you expect to re-chunk, re-embed or audit, which is every corpus\n  that lives longer than a prototype.\n- **The signal you need it:** \"what would it cost to try a different chunk size?\" is answered in weeks\n  rather than hours.\n- **Measure** embedding cache hit rate, re-run time per stage, orphan count after reconciliation and parse\n  yield; these show whether the pipeline is incremental in practice.\n- **Skip the full apparatus** for a static corpus of a few thousand files rebuilt from scratch on any\n  change, but still keep the raw copy.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you upgrade the embedding model on a live system?\" Re-run the embedding stage\n  into a second index version from stored chunks, then an evaluated cutover; only possible if the chunk\n  table exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements stage functions with version parameters and content hashes, caches embedding calls by text hash, and writes the reconciliation step that deletes orphans after re-chunking.","architect":"Defines the chunk and embedding tables as the data product, sets which transformations must run before storage for compliance, and chooses the orchestrator and lakehouse that host the stages.","fde":"Shows the customer the parsed and chunked intermediates when a retrieval answer is wrong, so the fix is traced to a stage rather than blamed on \"the model\", and estimates re-index cost from them."},"check":{"q":"Your team wants to move from fixed 512-token chunks to a semantic chunker and, a month later, to a\nnew embedding model, on a corpus of two million documents that is live for customers. What does the\npipeline need so each change is a bounded, reversible job rather than a quarter-long project?","a":"It needs stored, versioned intermediates. The parsed table lets the semantic chunker run without\nre-crawling or re-parsing two million documents. Chunk ids that include the chunker version let the new\nchunks land beside the old ones and let reconciliation delete the old per document, so the cutover is\ngradual and reversible. An embedding cache keyed by model and text hash means only genuinely new chunk\ntext is embedded. For the model change, the embedding stage is re-run with a new model parameter into a\nsecond index version, evaluated against the same retrieval test set, and traffic is switched when it wins;\nthe old index remains for rollback. Quality gates on parse yield and orphan count catch regressions before\nusers do, and the vector index is treated as a projection that can always be rebuilt from the tables."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a RAG pipeline, why is the vector index treated as a derived structure rather than the system of record?","options":["Vector databases cannot store document metadata reliably","Chunking, enrichment and embedding change often, and keeping the chunk tables lets any of them be re-run without re-extracting from sources","Vector indexes are always rebuilt nightly regardless of changes","Regulations require a relational copy of every embedded chunk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The transformations are the volatile part of a RAG system. If the index is the only copy, every chunker or model change becomes a re-crawl; if chunk and embedding tables exist, the index is a cheap projection that can be rebuilt, versioned or moved to another vendor."},{"q":"Which transformation genuinely belongs before the load (ETL) rather than after it (ELT) in a RAG pipeline?","options":["Splitting documents into chunks","Prepending section titles to chunk text","Redacting personal data that policy forbids persisting anywhere in the system","Computing embeddings with the current model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ELT is preferable for quality-driven steps because they are re-tuned often. Compliance-driven steps are different: if the data may not be stored, it must be removed before the landing write, since a raw copy would itself be a violation."},{"q":"After switching chunkers, users see overlapping passages from the same document twice in the top-k. What is the likely cause?","options":["The embedding model was changed at the same time","The reranker was not retrained on the new chunk sizes","Old chunks were never reconciled away: ids lacked the chunker version and no step deleted ids absent from the new chunk set","The new chunker produces chunks that are too small for the index"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When ids depend only on document and position, a new chunker overwrites some old rows and leaves others (the tail beyond its own chunk count) live. Versioned ids plus a reconciliation step that deletes every indexed id for the document not in the current chunk set make the old chunks discoverable and removable."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/training-data-pipelines","sources":["Lee et al., 2021 — Deduplicating Training Data Makes Language Models Better","Penedo et al., 2024 — The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale","Raffel et al., 2020 — Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer","Brown et al., 2020 — Language Models are Few-Shot Learners"],"why":"- **\"Export a JSONL file and point the trainer at it\" fails invisibly.** The failures do not show in the\n  loss curve and are expensive to discover afterwards.\n- **Duplicates.** Web and enterprise corpora contain the same page, template or ticket thousands of times.\n  The model memorizes them and spends capacity on boilerplate; Lee et al. found near-duplicate removal made\n  models emit memorized training text roughly ten times less often, with the same or better accuracy in\n  fewer training steps.\n- **Contamination and formatting noise.** If eval questions leaked into training (a scraped forum, an\n  internal doc), the benchmark measures memorization and the deployment regression has no explanation.\n  Hand-assembled SFT examples with inconsistent chat templates, stray system prompts or missing end-of-turn\n  tokens teach the noise: the model stops at the wrong place or emits template fragments.\n- **Reproducibility and throughput.** Without a dataset version and a deterministic shuffle, a loss\n  divergence cannot be reproduced, a resumed run does not match, and a regression cannot be attributed to a\n  data change. Tokenizing, packing and globally shuffling raw text in the dataloader is CPU- and I/O-bound\n  and can leave accelerators idle in a sawtooth, most visibly for smaller models with high per-GPU token\n  consumption.\n- **The pipeline makes these properties structural, not hoped for.** Filter, deduplicate, decontaminate,\n  format, tokenize, pack and shard offline, and record what came from where. This lesson covers the\n  pipeline; the curation, labeling and synthetic-data lessons in Training & adaptation cover what makes an\n  individual example good.","how":"### Stage order, and why\nCheap operations shrink the data before expensive ones run.\n\n1. **Sourcing and provenance.** Every source gets a manifest: origin, license or contractual basis,\n   snapshot date, raw hash. This is the only point where that information is cheap to capture.\n2. **Heuristic filtering.** Language ID, length bounds, symbol-to-word ratio, fraction of lines ending in\n   terminal punctuation, repeated-line ratio, blocklisted domains. These C4 and Gopher-style rules are cheap\n   per document, remove a large fraction of junk, and run first because everything after costs more per\n   byte.\n3. **Deduplication.** Exact dedup by document hash catches byte-identical copies only; near-duplicates need\n   MinHash (next subsection). It exists because exact hashing misses the same article with a different\n   footer, and pairwise comparison of a billion documents is impossible.\n4. **Decontamination.** Compare training text against every eval set the model will be judged on with\n   n-gram overlap and remove or flag overlapping documents (details below). Redo it whenever an eval set is\n   added.\n5. **Model-based quality filtering.** A small classifier or a perplexity threshold from a reference model\n   ranks documents for \"educational\" or domain quality. It runs late because it is the most expensive per\n   document and operates on the smallest set. FineWeb is a good public reference for how much each stage\n   removes on web data.\n6. **Safety and PII scrubbing.** Regex and NER passes for emails, keys and phone numbers, plus toxicity\n   filters, with the policy decided by legal rather than by the pipeline author.\n7. **Formatting.** Pretraining: concatenate documents with an end-of-sequence token between them. SFT:\n   render each example through the exact chat template the model will serve with, and mask the loss so only\n   assistant tokens contribute; prompt tokens get the ignore index. Doing this offline, deterministically,\n   removes a whole class of template bugs.\n8. **Tokenization and packing.** Tokenize offline, store token ids, and pack short examples into\n   fixed-length sequences (see the packing subsection).\n9. **Sharding and mixture.** Write fixed-size shards with a shard index and sample sources by mixture\n   weight (see below).\n10. **Versioning.** A dataset version is the ordered list of shard hashes plus the pipeline config. The\n    training run records that version; lineage goes from any training sample back to its source manifest.\n\n### Near-duplicate detection with MinHash and LSH\n1. **Shingle** each document into overlapping n-grams.\n2. **Hash** each shingle with *k* hash functions and keep the minimum per function: the signature.\n3. **Band** the signature with locality-sensitive hashing; documents sharing a band become candidate pairs.\n4. **Cluster** candidates with union-find and keep one per cluster.\n\n**Why it works:**\n- For one random hash function, the chance two documents share the minimum shingle hash equals their Jaccard\n  similarity *s*, so a signature of *k* minima estimates *s*.\n- Split the signature into *b* bands of *r* rows (*k = b·r*). A pair is a candidate if all *r* values in at\n  least one band match: `P(s) = 1 − (1 − s^r)^b`.\n- That is an S-curve with its steep part near `(1/b)^(1/r)`. More rows per band moves the threshold up and\n  makes the curve steeper.\n\n| *k* = 100 as *b* = 20, *r* = 5 (threshold about 0.55) | Candidate probability |\n|---|---|\n| *s* = 0.8 | about 0.9996 |\n| *s* = 0.5 | about 0.47 |\n| *s* = 0.3 | about 0.05 |\n\n- **Verify candidates** against a real similarity before merging, because LSH controls recall, not\n  precision.\n\n### Decontamination and the validation split\n- **N-gram overlap against eval sets.** The GPT-3 report used 13-gram overlap. The right *n* is a judgement\n  between missing paraphrases and deleting common phrases.\n- **Your own validation split needs the same care.** Split *after* near-duplicate clustering and by group\n  (customer, thread, product), so a cluster never has members on both sides.\n- **Why:** a random row-level split of support tickets puts near-identical tickets in train and validation,\n  and validation loss then reports memorization.\n\n### Packing\n- **Padding waste without it.** A batch of 4,096-token slots filled with 300-token examples is over 90 %\n  padding.\n- **Cross-document attention.** Packing requires either resetting position ids and masking attention across\n  document boundaries, or accepting a small amount of cross-document attention; the choice depends on the\n  trainer's support.\n\n### Sharding and mixture weights\n- **Formats.** Parquet, Arrow, WebDataset tar, or a streaming format such as MDS, with a shard index.\n- **What the index buys.** The loader can shuffle at shard level and within a buffer, resume from a global\n  sample offset, and sample sources by mixture weight (for example up-weighting the customer's domain data\n  relative to general text).\n- **A weight implies a repetition count:** `epochs_of_source = weight × total_training_tokens / source_tokens`.\n  Giving a 50M-token domain source 20 % of a 1B-token run draws 200M tokens from it: four passes.\n- **Write that number down per source,** because a small source with a high weight is repeated many times,\n  and repetition is where memorization and overfitting come from.\n\n### The dataloader contract\n- **The loader only reads shards,** does buffered shuffling with a seeded RNG, and collates.\n- **Its throughput must exceed the model's consumption** in tokens per second with headroom, which is why\n  the expensive work moved offline. Prefetching and multiple workers handle the rest.","example":"### Offline SFT formatting and packing\nRender the chat template, mask prompt tokens with the ignore index, pack examples into fixed-length\nsequences separated by EOS, and emit shards with a content hash for the dataset manifest. The tokenizer is\nany Hugging Face tokenizer with a chat template.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\nIGNORE, SEQ_LEN, SHARD_ROWS = -100, 4096, 2048\n\ndef render(tok, messages):\n    prompt = tok.apply_chat_template(messages[:-1], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\n    full   = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)\n    enc = lambda s: tok(s, add_special_tokens=False)[\"input_ids\"]   # template already has BOS\n    p_ids, f_ids = enc(prompt), enc(full)\n    assert f_ids[:len(p_ids)] == p_ids, \"template must be prefix-stable\"\n    labels = [IGNORE] * len(p_ids) + f_ids[len(p_ids):]      # loss only on the final assistant turn\n    return f_ids, labels\n\ndef pack(examples, tok):\n    ids, labels, doc_ids = [], [], []\n    for n, msgs in enumerate(examples):\n        i, l = render(tok, msgs)\n        if len(i) + 1 > SEQ_LEN:\n            continue                                            # too long: drop (or truncate) and count it\n        if len(ids) + len(i) + 1 > SEQ_LEN:\n            yield ids, labels, doc_ids; ids, labels, doc_ids = [], [], []\n        ids += i + [tok.eos_token_id]; labels += l + [tok.eos_token_id]; doc_ids += [n] * (len(i) + 1)\n    if ids:\n        yield ids, labels, doc_ids                              # trainer pads this last one\n\ndef write_shards(packed, path):\n    manifest, rows = [], []\n    def flush():\n        blob = json.dumps(rows).encode(); name = f\"{path}/shard-{len(manifest):05d}.json\"\n        with open(name, \"wb\") as f: f.write(blob)\n        manifest.append({\"shard\": name, \"rows\": len(rows), \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(blob).hexdigest()})\n    for ids, labels, doc_ids in packed:\n        rows.append({\"input_ids\": ids, \"labels\": labels, \"doc_ids\": doc_ids})  # doc_ids -> attention mask\n        if len(rows) == SHARD_ROWS:\n            flush(); rows = []\n    if rows:\n        flush()                                                 # keep the final partial shard\n    with open(f\"{path}/manifest.json\", \"w\") as f:\n        json.dump(manifest, f)                                  # the dataset version is this file's hash\n```\n\n- **`doc_ids`** lets a trainer that supports it block attention across packed documents.\n- **The manifest of shard hashes** is what the training config references, so the run is reproducible.\n\n### Tuning the LSH threshold and checking mixture repetition\nRunnable as-is. It prints the candidate probability for three band layouts of the same 100 hash functions,\nthen the repetition and padding arithmetic from above.\n\n```python\ndef p_candidate(s, b, r):\n    \"\"\"Probability two docs with Jaccard s share at least one fully matching band.\"\"\"\n    return 1 - (1 - s**r) ** b\n\nfor b, r in [(20, 5), (10, 10), (50, 2)]:                  # each uses k = b*r = 100 hash functions\n    thr = (1 / b) ** (1 / r)\n    row = \"  \".join(f\"s={s}: {p_candidate(s, b, r):.4f}\" for s in (0.3, 0.5, 0.8))\n    print(f\"b={b:>2} r={r:>2} threshold~{thr:.2f}  {row}\")\n\ndef epochs(weight, total_tokens, source_tokens):\n    return weight * total_tokens / source_tokens\n\nprint(epochs(0.20, 1e9, 50e6))       # 4.0 passes over a 50M-token source at 20% of a 1B run\nprint(1 - 300 / 4096)                # padding fraction of 300-token examples in 4,096 slots, unpacked\n```\n\n| Layout | Threshold | P at s = 0.3 | P at s = 0.5 | P at s = 0.8 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| b = 20, r = 5 | ~0.55 | 0.0475 | 0.4701 | 0.9996 |\n| b = 10, r = 10 | ~0.79 | 0.0001 | 0.0097 | 0.6789 |\n| b = 50, r = 2 | ~0.14 | 0.9910 | 1.0000 | 1.0000 |\n\n- **Read it as a recall dial.** b = 10, r = 10 misses a third of pairs at s = 0.8; b = 50, r = 2 flags almost\n  everything and floods the verification step.","patterns":["**Use a dedup library built for scale.** For corpora beyond one machine, run MinHash dedup with an existing tool (Hugging Face `datatrove` was used for FineWeb; `datasketch` offers `MinHashLSH` with a `threshold` and `num_perm` for smaller jobs). Monitor the fraction removed and the cluster-size distribution; one giant cluster usually means a template or boilerplate problem.","**Decontamination as a blocking gate.** Before any training run, compute n-gram overlap between the new shards and every registered eval set and fail the pipeline above a set overlap rate. Re-run the gate when an eval set is added, and log overlap per eval set in the run record.","**Eyeball the loss mask.** For every new template or tokenizer, decode a few rendered examples with labels shown and confirm only assistant tokens and the end-of-turn token carry loss. Monitor the share of examples dropped as too long, because silent drops skew the data mix.","**Pin the dataset version in the run config.** Reference the manifest hash, the pipeline config and the loader seed in the training config, and refuse to launch without them. This makes resume and divergence debugging possible.","**Benchmark the loader before the run.** Measure loader tokens per second against the model's consumption on the target hardware, and watch GPU utilization for a sawtooth during the first minutes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Sources + provenance manifest\"] --> F[\"Heuristic filters\"]\n    F --> D[\"Exact + MinHash dedup\"]\n    D --> C[\"Decontaminate vs eval sets\"]\n    C --> Q[\"Model-based quality filter\"]\n    Q --> P[\"PII and safety scrub\"]\n    P --> T[\"Template render + loss mask\"]\n    T --> K[\"Tokenize + pack\"]\n    K --> SH[(\"Shards + manifest hash\")]\n    SH --> L[\"Seeded loader feeds trainer\"]\n    D -.->|\"split by group after clustering\"| V[\"Validation split\"]","caption":"Cheap filters shrink the data before expensive stages run; the output is a hashed set of shards the\ntraining run references."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does stage order matter in a training data pipeline?","a":"- **Cost per byte rises along the pipeline:** heuristic filters are nearly free, MinHash is moderate, and\n  model-based quality scoring is the most expensive per document.\n- **So cheap filters run first** and shrink the set before costly stages run; running MinHash first means\n  days of compute on data a length rule would have discarded.\n- **Provenance is captured first** because it is only cheap at the source."},{"q":"Validation loss keeps falling but the model is no better on new customers. What happened?","a":"- **Likely a leaky split:** rows were split at random, so near-duplicate tickets from one customer or thread\n  sit on both sides and validation measures memorization.\n- **Check** dedup clusters that span train and validation, and overlap with the eval set.\n- **Fix:** split by group after near-duplicate clustering, and rebuild the eval from data dated after the\n  training export."},{"q":"How do you choose the MinHash band layout?","a":"- **The threshold sits near `(1/b)^(1/r)`:** with 100 hash functions, 20 bands of 5 rows puts it near\n  0.55, while 10 bands of 10 rows moves it near 0.79 and makes the curve steeper.\n- **Trade recall for verification cost:** a lower threshold finds more near-duplicates but sends more\n  candidate pairs to the exact-similarity check.\n- **Domain matters:** a low threshold deletes templated-but-distinct documents such as product pages."},{"q":"A 50M-token domain dataset gets 20 % of a 1B-token run. Is that a problem?","a":"- **Compute repetitions:** 0.2 × 1B / 50M = four passes over the domain data.\n- **Four passes is a risk to watch, not automatically wrong;** repetition is where memorization and\n  overfitting come from, so track held-out loss on that domain.\n- **Levers:** lower the weight, add more domain data, or deduplicate it harder first."},{"q":"When would you not pack SFT examples?","a":"- **When the trainer cannot reset position ids or mask attention across documents** and examples are\n  short and unrelated, cross-document attention may hurt more than padding costs.\n- **Otherwise pack:** 300-token examples in 4,096-token slots waste over 90 % of each sequence on padding."}],"tradeoffs":["**Aggressive heuristic filtering vs coverage.** It removes junk cheaply but also legitimate code, tables and non-English text; the thresholds flip when the target domain is exactly the content the rules distrust.","**Near-duplicate removal vs templated content.** It improves generalization and reduces memorization, but a low similarity threshold deletes templated-but-distinct documents such as product pages, which can matter for a domain model.","**Packing vs isolation.** It eliminates padding waste and raises tokens per second, but without cross-document masking examples attend to unrelated neighbours; for short SFT examples masking is worth it.","**Offline tokenization vs flexibility.** It maximizes throughput and reproducibility, yet binds the dataset to one tokenizer, so a tokenizer or model-family change means re-tokenizing from the text tables.","**Strict decontamination vs data volume.** It protects eval validity, but at small *n* removes documents that merely share common phrases with the eval set, shrinking the training set for no gain."],"pitfalls":["**Decontaminating with exact match only.** Symptom: a benchmark score that collapses on a freshly written eval set of the same difficulty. Fix: n-gram overlap.","**A training template that differs from serving.** Symptom: the fine-tuned model emits role tags or fails to stop at the end of its turn. Fix: render with the exact serving template.","**Tokenizing, packing or globally shuffling inside the dataloader at scale.** Symptom: GPU utilization oscillates between near zero and full while CPUs are saturated. Fix: do it offline.","**Non-deterministic shuffling without a seeded, offset-addressable order.** Symptom: a resumed run whose loss curve does not continue the original, and a divergence nobody can reproduce.","**Near-dup detection before cheap filters.** Symptom: MinHash jobs that take days on data most of which a one-line length rule then discards.","**Random-row train/validation splits instead of group splits after dedup.** Symptom: validation loss keeps falling while quality on genuinely new customers or threads stays flat.","**Dropping provenance at the first join.** Symptom: a takedown or license question that can only be answered by retraining without the disputed source."],"when":"- **Use** this pipeline, at whatever scale, the moment training data comes from more than one source or\n  will be used more than once.\n- **Small fine-tunes still need the properties.** For a few thousand customer examples the stages are small\n  scripts, but keep template-exact rendering, loss masking, dedup against the eval set, and a manifest hash\n  in the training config.\n- **Measure** fraction removed per stage, near-duplicate cluster sizes, overlap rate with each eval set,\n  padding ratio after packing, and loader tokens per second against model consumption.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you prepare data to fine-tune on a customer's support tickets?\" Walk these\n  stages; the differentiators are decontamination against the eval set, prompt-token loss masking and a\n  reproducible dataset version.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the filtering, MinHash dedup, template rendering and packing jobs, verifies the loss mask on a handful of examples by eye, and checks loader throughput before launching a run.","architect":"Sets the stage order and the quality, PII and licensing policies, chooses the shard format and storage layout, and requires that every run references a hashed dataset version.","fde":"Assembles customer data into the pipeline with provenance intact, agrees PII rules with the customer's legal team, and holds back the eval set from the data the customer hands over for training."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tuned support model scores well on the team's internal benchmark but performs no better than\nthe base model on live tickets, and it sometimes ends its replies with a stray assistant tag. Which\npipeline stages would you inspect first and why?","a":"Two stages explain both symptoms. The benchmark gap points to contamination: the internal eval set\nwas probably drawn from the same ticket export as the training data, so near-identical tickets appear in\nboth and the benchmark measures memorization; check n-gram overlap between training shards and the eval\nset and decontaminate or rebuild the eval from tickets dated after the export. The stray tag points to\nformatting: examples were rendered with a chat template that differs from the serving template, or the\nloss mask included prompt tokens so the model learned to reproduce template structure; inspect a few\nrendered examples token by token, confirm the ignore index covers the prompt and that the end-of-turn\ntoken terminates the labels. Near-duplicate inflation in the training set would compound both, so look at\nthe dedup cluster sizes as well."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do near-duplicate removal with MinHash rather than relying on exact document hashing?","options":["MinHash is faster than computing a SHA-256 per document","Most duplicates differ in small ways such as footers or timestamps, which exact hashing misses, and MinHash with LSH finds similar pairs without comparing every pair","Exact hashing cannot be parallelized across shards","MinHash also detects contamination against evaluation sets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Real corpora contain the same content with trivial variations. MinHash signatures approximate shingle similarity, and LSH banding turns the impossible all-pairs comparison into candidate lookups, so near duplicates are found at scale; decontamination is a separate n-gram check."},{"q":"In SFT formatting, what does setting prompt tokens' labels to the ignore index achieve?","options":["It removes the prompt from the model's context so it only sees the answer","It prevents the tokenizer from splitting special template tokens","It excludes prompt tokens from the loss so the model learns to produce assistant turns rather than to reproduce prompts and templates","It allows examples to be packed without attention masking"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The prompt stays in the input, so the model conditions on it, but gradients only flow from assistant tokens. Training on prompt tokens wastes capacity and teaches the model to emit template structure, which shows up as stray role tags at inference."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to tokenize and pack offline rather than in the training dataloader?","options":["Offline tokenization produces smaller files than raw text","The trainer cannot apply chat templates at runtime","Tokenization and packing are CPU-bound; done at training time they bottleneck the loader and leave accelerators idle, and done offline they are also reproducible","Packing is only possible when all examples are known in advance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When accelerators consume tokens faster than the loader's CPU workers can tokenize and pack them, the result is a sawtooth utilization pattern; whether that happens depends on model size and worker count, but the reproducibility benefit holds regardless. Precomputed shards make the loader a simple reader and make the exact sequence of training tokens part of the versioned dataset."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/lakehouse","sources":["Armbrust et al., 2021 — Lakehouse: A New Generation of Open Platforms that Unify Data Warehousing and Advanced Analytics","Armbrust et al., 2020 — Delta Lake: High-Performance ACID Table Storage over Cloud Object Stores","Official docs: Apache Iceberg","Official docs: Apache Parquet"],"why":"- **The lake was cheap but was not a table.** Files in S3 or similar held raw and semi-structured data that\n  Spark or Python could read. But a writer dying halfway leaves readers seeing a partial dataset, two\n  concurrent writers corrupt each other, no schema is enforced, and deleting one user's rows means\n  rewriting files by hand with no record. This is the \"data swamp\".\n- **The warehouse was safe but closed.** Curated tables with ACID and SQL performance, but a proprietary\n  format, higher cost, and a poor fit for images, PDFs, embeddings or Python-first ML tooling.\n- **So data was copied between the two,** the copies drifted, and governance had to be enforced twice.\n- **AI workloads make the swamp worse.** A RAG pipeline appends millions of chunk rows while a retrieval\n  eval reads a consistent snapshot; a training job must reproduce against exactly the data it saw; a GDPR\n  request must delete one person's documents and prove it reached the derived tables. All need\n  transactions and versioning on cheap storage.\n- **The idea, plainly:** keep the files open and the storage cheap, and add a metadata layer that turns a\n  set of files into a versioned, transactional table any engine can read, because many engines need the\n  same data and none should own it.","how":"### Layer 1: open columnar files in object storage\n- **Parquet on S3, GCS or ADLS.** Columnar and compressed, split into row groups with per-column statistics\n  (min, max, null count).\n- **Statistics enable skipping.** A scan for `tenant_id = 'acme'` skips whole row groups whose statistics\n  exclude the value.\n- **Object storage's constraint drives everything.** Durable, essentially unlimited capacity at a low price\n  per GB, but high per-request latency and no in-place updates: a file is written whole or not at all.\n\n### Layer 2: the table format\nA table format is a metadata log saying which files make up the table at each version.\n\n| Format | Metadata structure |\n|---|---|\n| Delta Lake | a `_delta_log` of JSON commit entries with periodic Parquet checkpoints |\n| Iceberg | a tree of metadata files, manifest lists and manifests pointing at data files |\n| Hudi | a timeline of commits |\n\n**A write, in all three:**\n1. Add new data files (invisible to readers, because nothing references them yet).\n2. Atomically publish a new log entry or metadata pointer that references them.\n3. Readers see the old version or the new one, never a mix.\n4. Concurrent writers use optimistic concurrency: each validates that no conflicting commit landed since it\n   started, and retries if one did. That is ACID without a database server in front of the files.\n\n**The atomic publish still needs one primitive from somewhere:**\n- **A put-if-absent write in the object store.** S3 lacked this until it added conditional writes, so Delta\n  on S3 long relied on a single writer or an external lock table.\n- **Or an atomic pointer swap in the catalog,** which is how Iceberg commits.\n\n### What the log gives you\n- **Snapshot isolation and time travel.** A reader pins a version; a training job records the snapshot id\n  and re-reading it returns identical rows, as long as the snapshot has not been expired and its files\n  vacuumed. Default retention is days to weeks (Delta's default vacuum threshold is 7 days), so a\n  snapshot needed for a year must be explicitly retained (an Iceberg tag, a longer retention setting) or\n  materialized as its own dataset.\n- **Schema enforcement and evolution.** The current schema is in the metadata; writes with a mismatched\n  schema are rejected, and adding a column is a metadata change rather than a rewrite.\n- **Deletes and updates.** Copy-on-write rewrites the affected files and commits the replacement.\n  Merge-on-read records deletes in small side files (Iceberg delete files, Delta deletion vectors) that\n  readers apply at scan time, deferring the rewrite to compaction. Copy-on-write favours readers;\n  merge-on-read favours frequent small writes.\n- **Partitioning and clustering.** Rows are laid out by a partition value (date, tenant) and optionally\n  clustered within partitions so statistics-based skipping works. Iceberg's hidden partitioning derives the\n  partition from a column transform, so queries need not know the layout.\n\n### Delta, Iceberg and Hudi: what actually differs\nAll three implement the same idea and differ mainly in origin.\n\n| Format | Origin | Distinctive traits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Delta Lake | Databricks and Spark | linear log of JSON commits with Parquet checkpoints; most mature inside that ecosystem |\n| Iceberg | Netflix, as an engine-neutral spec | snapshots point to manifest lists, then manifests, then data files, so planning reads a small metadata tree instead of listing directories; hidden partitioning and partition evolution (change layout without rewriting old data or queries) |\n| Hudi | Uber, for upsert-heavy CDC-fed tables | explicit copy-on-write and merge-on-read table types; record-level indexes that locate the file holding a key, making frequent upserts cheaper |\n\n- **The choice is less permanent than it was.** Interoperability layers such as Delta UniForm and Apache\n  XTable write one format's metadata for another format's data.\n\n### Layer 3: the catalog\n- **What it does.** Maps table names to the current metadata location; access control, discovery and\n  lineage attach here.\n- **Examples.** Hive Metastore, AWS Glue, Unity Catalog, Nessie, or an Iceberg REST catalog.\n- **Why it matters.** It makes \"the same table\" mean the same thing to Spark, Trino, DuckDB, Flink, Ray and a\n  pandas script, which is the whole point: no copies, one access policy.\n\n### Maintenance the format does not do for you\n- **Compaction.** Streaming appends create many small files; object listing plus per-file overhead then\n  dominates query time. Periodic compaction rewrites them into large files.\n- **Snapshot expiry and vacuum.** Old snapshots and the files only they reference accumulate until expired\n  and vacuumed. That is also the moment a \"deleted\" row is physically gone, so retention policy and\n  deletion guarantees depend on running this job.\n\n### Where AI data sits in it\n- **Medallion layering.** Bronze (raw ingested bytes and metadata), silver (parsed, cleaned, conformed),\n  gold (serving-ready tables).\n- **Concrete AI tables.** Documents (raw pointer, hash, ACL), parsed text, chunks, embeddings (an array\n  column alongside `model_id`), training datasets as pinned snapshots, and evaluation and trace logs.\n- **Serving stores are derived.** The vector index and any feature-serving store are rebuilt from these\n  tables and are never the only copy.\n- **Not a serving database.** The lakehouse is an analytical and batch store; point lookups and sub-100 ms\n  serving belong to Postgres, a key-value store or the vector database.","example":"### A chunk table in Delta Lake\nUsing the `deltalake` Python package: append new chunks, upsert changed ones by `chunk_id`, read an older\nsnapshot for a reproducible evaluation, and compact small files. Spark, DuckDB or Trino can read the same\ntable through a catalog.\n\n```python\nimport pyarrow as pa\nfrom deltalake import DeltaTable, write_deltalake\n\npath = \"s3://ai-lake/silver/chunks\"           # or a local directory while developing\n\nbatch = pa.table({\n    \"chunk_id\":   [\"c-001\", \"c-002\"],\n    \"doc_id\":     [\"d-1\", \"d-1\"],\n    \"doc_version\":[\"9f3a\", \"9f3a\"],\n    \"text\":       [\"Refund window is 30 days.\", \"Returns need a receipt.\"],\n    \"model_id\":   [\"emb-v2\", \"emb-v2\"],\n    \"embedding\":  [[0.01, 0.42, -0.13], [0.33, 0.05, 0.27]],   # real width: hundreds to ~3,000 floats\n})\nwrite_deltalake(path, batch, mode=\"append\", partition_by=[\"model_id\"])\n\n# Upsert re-chunked rows: match on chunk_id, update text/embedding, insert new ids.\ndt = DeltaTable(path)\n(dt.merge(source=batch, predicate=\"t.chunk_id = s.chunk_id\", source_alias=\"s\", target_alias=\"t\")\n   .when_matched_update_all()\n   .when_not_matched_insert_all()\n   .execute())\n\n# Reproducible evaluation: pin the snapshot the eval ran against.\neval_version = dt.version()\nfrozen = DeltaTable(path, version=eval_version).to_pyarrow_table()\nprint(eval_version, frozen.num_rows)\n\n# Maintenance: fold small streaming files into large ones, then expire old files.\ndt.optimize.compact()\ndt.vacuum(retention_hours=168, dry_run=False)\n```\n\n**What the log looks like afterwards:**\n\n| Version | Operation | Log actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | append | one `add` per new Parquet file |\n| 1 | merge (rows exist, so the matched file is rewritten) | a `remove` for the old file and an `add` for its replacement, in one atomic entry |\n| 2 | compaction | `remove` for the small files and an `add` for the large one, no change to rows |\n\n- **Toy caveat.** On this two-row table compaction may find nothing to merge and commit nothing; on a real\n  table fed by streaming appends it is a large commit.\n- **`remove` is logical.** A reader pinned to version 0 still resolves the original file, because `remove`\n  only drops a file from later versions. Vacuum is the step that deletes files no retained version\n  references.\n- **Retention and reproducibility are one decision.** Every run records `eval_version`, and an audit can\n  re-read the same rows provided retention keeps that version. `vacuum(retention_hours=168)` deletes files\n  that only versions older than seven days reference, so time travel to them stops working.\n\n### Optimistic concurrency, simulated\nRunnable as-is. The table is at version 41. Writer A appends chunks for new documents; writer B runs a\nGDPR delete that rewrites three files (one in the simulation); both read version 41 and race for 42.\n\n```python\nclass Log:\n    \"\"\"A table's commit log: version -> actions. put_if_absent is the one atomic primitive.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, upto):\n        self.entries = {v: {\"add\": [f\"f{v}.parquet\"], \"remove\": []} for v in range(upto + 1)}\n    def put_if_absent(self, version, entry):\n        if version in self.entries:\n            return False\n        self.entries[version] = entry\n        return True\n\ndef commit(log, read_version, adds, removes, retries=3):\n    v = read_version + 1\n    for _ in range(retries):\n        if log.put_if_absent(v, {\"add\": adds, \"remove\": removes}):\n            return v\n        winner = log.entries[v]\n        # Simplified check: real formats also ask whether the winner's added files could hold\n        # rows matching our predicate (via partition values or file statistics).\n        if set(winner[\"remove\"]) & set(removes):\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"conflict at v{v}: re-run against the new state\")\n        v += 1                                        # disjoint change: publish as the next version\n    raise RuntimeError(\"too many retries\")\n\nlog = Log(upto=41)                                    # table is at version 41\nprint(\"A:\", commit(log, 41, adds=[\"chunks-new.parquet\"], removes=[]))            # A: 42\nprint(\"B:\", commit(log, 41, adds=[\"f7b.parquet\"], removes=[\"f7.parquet\"]))       # B: 43\ntry:\n    commit(log, 41, adds=[\"f7c.parquet\"], removes=[\"f7.parquet\"])                # C rewrote f7 too\nexcept RuntimeError as e:\n    print(\"C:\", e)                                                               # C: conflict at v43\n```\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| A and B both read version 41 | each writes new Parquet files no reader can see yet |\n| A publishes entry 42 | put-if-absent succeeds |\n| B tries entry 42 | fails; B reads entry 42 and checks for conflict with what it read and rewrote |\n| A only added files that cannot hold rows matching B's predicate | judged by partition values or file statistics, so the check can be conservative; no conflict, B publishes as version 43 |\n| Had A rewritten a file B removed | B aborts and re-runs against the new state (writer C above) |\n\n- **Cheap when writers touch disjoint data;** it degrades into retry storms when many writers rewrite the\n  same partitions, and that is when you serialize writers through one job instead.","patterns":["**Scheduled compaction per table.** For tables fed by streaming appends, run `OPTIMIZE` (Delta) or the `rewrite_data_files` procedure (Iceberg on Spark) on a schedule, after ingestion windows. Monitor file count and average file size per table; a falling average file size is the early warning.","**Retention as an explicit policy.** Set snapshot expiry (`VACUUM` with a retention period in Delta, the `expire_snapshots` procedure in Iceberg) to match the deletion SLA, and tag or materialize any snapshot a training run must keep longer. Monitor time from deletion request to physical removal.","**Pin snapshots in run configs.** Record the table version or snapshot id each training and evaluation run read, next to the code commit. Alert when a pinned version is about to fall outside retention.","**One writer per hot partition.** When several jobs rewrite the same partitions (re-chunking, GDPR deletes, CDC merges), route them through one job or queue. Monitor commit retries and conflict aborts per table.","**Merge-on-read for CDC tables.** For continuously upserted tables, enable deletion vectors (Delta) or merge-on-read deletes (Iceberg, Hudi) and compact regularly so read-time merge cost stays bounded. Monitor scan time and the number of delete files per data file."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Writer A append\n    participant B as Writer B GDPR delete\n    participant S as Object store\n    participant L as Commit log\n    A->>S: write new Parquet files\n    B->>S: write rewritten files\n    A->>L: put-if-absent entry 42\n    L-->>A: ok, table now v42\n    B->>L: put-if-absent entry 42\n    L-->>B: already exists\n    Note over B: read entry 42, A only added files\n    B->>L: put-if-absent entry 43\n    L-->>B: ok, table now v43","caption":"A commit writes invisible data files, then races to publish the next log entry; the loser checks for a\nconflict and either republishes as the next version or re-runs."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a lakehouse, and why not just use a data lake plus a warehouse?","a":"- **Definition:** a transactional metadata layer (Delta, Iceberg, Hudi) over open Parquet files in object\n  storage, giving atomic commits, snapshots, schema enforcement and real deletes.\n- **Why not two tiers:** copying lake data into a warehouse creates drifting copies and doubles governance,\n  and warehouses fit embeddings, PDFs and Python tooling poorly.\n- **The payoff for AI:** one copy of chunks, embeddings and training sets that Spark, Trino, DuckDB and\n  pandas all read through one catalog and one access policy."},{"q":"Commits on a busy table keep failing with conflicts. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Look at who rewrites what:** conflicts come from concurrent writers rewriting the same files or\n  partitions, such as a re-chunk job and a GDPR delete job.\n- **Check the partitioning:** a coarse partition (all chunks in one) makes every rewrite overlap; partition by\n  date or tenant so writers touch disjoint data.\n- **Then serialize** the remaining hot writers through one job, and track retries per table."},{"q":"When would you not put data in a lakehouse?","a":"- **Request-path serving:** sub-100 ms lookups belong in Postgres, a key-value store or the vector database,\n  because object storage has high per-request latency.\n- **Small single-service data:** a few gigabytes owned by one service is simpler in Postgres.\n- **Keep the lakehouse as the source** those serving stores are rebuilt from."},{"q":"A customer asks you to prove a user's data is deleted. What do you check?","a":"- **The delete commit:** a `DELETE` or `MERGE` produced a new version without the rows.\n- **Old snapshots:** the rows still exist in prior versions until snapshot expiry and vacuum run, so check\n  retention (Delta's default vacuum threshold is 7 days) and that the job ran.\n- **Derived stores:** the vector index, caches and any materialized training snapshots must also be purged."},{"q":"Why do training runs record a snapshot id, and what can break it?","a":"- **Reproducibility:** re-reading a pinned version returns identical rows, so a regression can be traced to a\n  data change.\n- **What breaks it:** vacuum deletes files only old versions reference; with `retention_hours=168`, versions\n  older than seven days stop resolving.\n- **Fix:** tag the snapshot (Iceberg), extend retention for that table, or materialize the training set as\n  its own dataset with deletion handled explicitly."}],"tradeoffs":["**Open files vs operations work.** Any engine can read the data without copies, but you now operate compaction, snapshot expiry and vacuuming yourself unless a managed platform does it.","**Cheap capacity vs latency.** Object storage makes capacity nearly free, but per-request latency makes the lakehouse wrong for point lookups or request-path serving; once a query needs sub-100 ms, the data needs a derived store.","**Copy-on-write vs merge-on-read.** Copy-on-write keeps reads simple and fast; merge-on-read makes frequent small updates and deletes cheap. The choice flips toward merge-on-read when CDC-style streams feed the table continuously.","**Time travel vs prompt deletion.** Reproducibility and audit come cheaply, but every retained snapshot pins files, and a deletion is not a deletion until expiry runs. Training snapshots that must outlive retention are usually materialized separately with deletion handled explicitly.","**Delta vs Iceberg vs Hudi.** Mostly an ecosystem decision (Databricks, Spark and Trino, or a streaming-first stack); the formats converge on features, so engine support and catalog integration should decide."],"pitfalls":["**Streaming small appends without compaction.** Symptom: query time dominated by listing and opening thousands of tiny files, visible as low bytes-scanned but long wall-clock.","**Partitioning on a high-cardinality column such as `doc_id`.** Symptom: millions of directories, slow metadata operations and no skipping benefit.","**Using the lakehouse as the serving database** for retrieval or features. Symptom: p99 latency in seconds and a request path that depends on object-store availability.","**Embeddings without a `model_id` column.** Symptom: an index rebuilt from mixed vectors after a model upgrade, with retrieval quality that silently degrades.","**Never expiring snapshots or vacuuming.** Symptom: storage growing without bound and a GDPR deletion that an auditor can still find in an old version.","**Every writer evolving the schema freely.** Symptom: downstream jobs failing on a renamed column, which the data-contracts lesson addresses."],"when":"- **Use when** more than one engine or team needs the same large tables, when reproducibility and audit\n  matter (training sets, evaluation snapshots, deletion proof), or when raw documents and derived chunk and\n  embedding tables must coexist with analytics.\n- **Avoid when** it is a single service with a few gigabytes; a Postgres schema is simpler and should win.\n- **Measure** file count and average file size per table, bytes scanned versus rows returned (skipping\n  effectiveness), snapshot retention versus storage growth, and time from deletion request to physical\n  removal.\n- **Day to day,** the lakehouse is where the RAG pipeline's stage tables and the training manifest live.\n- **Interviews:** explain how an atomic commit over immutable files yields ACID, and why the vector index is\n  derived from a table rather than being the source.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes stage outputs as Delta or Iceberg tables with partition keys and `model_id` columns, pins snapshot versions in evaluation and training configs, and schedules compaction and vacuum jobs.","architect":"Chooses the table format and catalog to match the engines in use, defines the bronze, silver and gold boundaries, and decides which derived serving stores are rebuilt from which tables.","fde":"Lands customer data into the customer's own lakehouse account and region, respects their catalog's access policies, and uses time travel to show exactly which data a delivered model was trained on."},"check":{"q":"A team keeps parsed documents and chunk embeddings as plain Parquet files in S3, written by a nightly\nSpark job and read by a Python evaluation script and a Trino dashboard. They see occasional evaluation\nruns with partially missing data and cannot honour a customer's deletion request confidently. What does\nmoving to a lakehouse table format change, mechanically, and what still remains their responsibility?","a":"Plain Parquet directories have no commit boundary, so the evaluation script sometimes lists files\nwhile Spark is mid-write and reads a mix of old and new. A table format records which files form each\nversion in a log and publishes a new version atomically, so readers see a complete snapshot and writers\ndetect conflicts through optimistic concurrency. Deletion becomes a `DELETE` or `MERGE` that commits a new\nversion excluding the rows, with copy-on-write or deletion files handling the physical layout, and the\nlog records what changed. What stays with the team: the deleted rows persist in prior snapshots until\nexpiry and vacuum run, so a retention job and a check against derived stores such as the vector index are\nstill needed; compaction of the nightly job's small files; and a catalog so Trino, Spark and Python all\nresolve the same table and access policy."},"quiz":[{"q":"How does a lakehouse table format provide atomic commits on object storage that has no in-place updates?","options":["By locking the S3 bucket for the duration of a write","By writing new immutable data files first and then atomically publishing a metadata entry that references them, so readers see the old or new version entirely","By buffering writes in a database server that fronts the files","By using Parquet row-group checksums to detect partial writes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The files themselves are immutable and written whole; the only mutable thing is the pointer or log entry naming the current version. Making that single publish step atomic, with optimistic conflict checks for concurrent writers, yields snapshot isolation without a server in the path."},{"q":"Which workload is a poor fit for a lakehouse and should use a derived store instead?","options":["Rebuilding a vector index from the embeddings table every night","Pinning the exact training set a model run read","Serving per-request vector similarity lookups within a 100 ms latency budget","Running an evaluation across a consistent snapshot while new chunks are appended"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Object storage has high per-request latency and tables are optimized for scans, not point lookups. Request-path retrieval belongs in a vector database or OLTP store that is rebuilt from the lakehouse table, which remains the system of record."},{"q":"After deleting a user's rows from a Delta or Iceberg table, why might an auditor still find them?","options":["Parquet files cannot be modified, so deletes are impossible","The catalog caches query results for 24 hours","Prior snapshots still reference the old files until snapshot expiry and vacuum physically remove them","Merge-on-read tables never apply deletes to readers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Time travel works precisely because old files are retained. A delete commits a new version without the rows, but the previous version remains readable until its files are expired, so the retention job is part of the deletion guarantee."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/document-processing-pipelines","sources":["Official docs: PyMuPDF","Official docs: Docling","Official docs: Unstructured"],"why":"- **The single library call breaks on the real corpus.** Scanned PDFs have no text layer, so it returns empty\n  strings and the pipeline embeds nothing. Two-column papers interleave lines of both columns.\n- **Tables, headers and encoding fail too.** Tables flatten into numbers with no column association, so\n  \"what was Q3 revenue\" retrieves the right number next to the wrong label. Every page adds its header and\n  footer, giving thousands of chunks starting \"Confidential — Page 14 of 62\". Hyphenated line breaks split\n  words; encoding glitches turn ligatures into garbage.\n- **None of this fails loudly.** An embedding model produces a valid vector for a garbled chunk, so the damage\n  appears months later as retrieval that \"sometimes misses obvious passages\".\n- **The opposite extreme, a vision LLM on every page, swaps quality for cost.** It is orders of magnitude more\n  expensive per page than native extraction (illustratively, a tenth of a cent to a few cents per page\n  depending on the model, versus milliseconds of CPU), slow, and non-deterministic, so a re-run gives\n  slightly different text, chunks and embeddings.\n- **Neither extreme is a pipeline.** Classify, route, verify and escalate, so the expensive path is spent only\n  where the cheap path demonstrably failed. The rag-retrieval parsing lesson covers the extractors (OCR,\n  layout, tables); this lesson covers the system around them.","how":"### 1. Intake and classification\n- **Real type by magic bytes, not extension;** customers rename files.\n- **Record page count, file size and, for PDFs, whether pages carry a text layer.** A page with an image\n  covering it and near zero extractable characters is a scan.\n- **Detect language,** because OCR engines need the right language packs and the quality heuristics below\n  (dictionary-word ratios, hyphen repair) are language-specific.\n- **Detect encrypted or malformed files early** and park them.\n\n### 2. Routing tiers\nOrder extractors by cost and by what they preserve; route each page to the first tier expected to succeed.\n\n| Tier | Examples | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Native extraction with layout heuristics | PyMuPDF, pdfplumber, python-docx, HTML parsers | born-digital files; fast, deterministic, cheap; reading order from glyph positions reasonably well | tables poorly |\n| 2. OCR | Tesseract, cloud OCR services | scans and images; per-word confidence and bounding boxes | depends on resolution, skew and language; preprocessing (deskew, binarize) matters as much as the engine |\n| 3. Layout-aware document models | Docling, Unstructured's high-resolution strategy, LayoutLM-family models | detect regions (title, paragraph, table, figure, caption), reconstruct tables; make multi-column and table-heavy documents usable | slower and heavier |\n| 4. Vision LLMs | any vision-capable model | the residue: handwritten forms, charts to describe, pages failing layout verification | most expensive, non-deterministic, so used last and cached |\n\n- **Why a ladder:** cost per page differs by orders of magnitude between tiers, while most pages in a typical\n  corpus are born-digital text that tier one handles well.\n\n**Route per page where the format allows it:**\n- **Real files are mixed:** a born-digital report with a scanned appendix, a slide deck where one slide is a\n  screenshot of a table.\n- **A per-document decision** picks one tier for the whole file, which either loses the odd pages or overpays\n  for the ordinary ones.\n- **Some decisions stay at document level,** because they need several pages at once: header and footer\n  detection, tables that continue across a page break, and section hierarchy. They run after per-page\n  extraction, over the assembled blocks.\n\n### 3. The canonical document model\n- **Blocks.** Each has `type` (title, heading with level, paragraph, list item, table, figure, caption,\n  header, footer, footnote), `text`, `page`, `bbox`, and a `parent` reference giving section hierarchy.\n- **Tables** are stored as cell grids with row and column spans, and also serialized to Markdown or HTML for\n  embedding.\n- **Figures** carry captions and optionally an LLM-written description.\n- **Why one schema:** chunkers, metadata extractors and citation rendering depend on it rather than on each\n  extractor's quirks, and a document re-processed by a better tier later slots into the same downstream\n  pipeline.\n\n### 4. Cleaning\n- **Headers and footers:** text repeating at the same position across many pages is dropped.\n- **Hyphenation** at line ends is repaired.\n- **Unicode normalized (NFKC),** control characters removed, whitespace collapsed while paragraph boundaries\n  are kept.\n- **HTML boilerplate** (cookie banners, navigation) stripped.\n- Each step is cheap and removes a class of chunk-level noise that would otherwise be embedded thousands of\n  times.\n\n### 5. Quality scoring and escalation\n- **Signals:** characters per page against an expectation for the document type, OCR mean confidence,\n  fraction of tokens that are dictionary words, ratio of single-character \"words\", and known garbage\n  patterns (replacement characters, repeated glyphs).\n- **Below threshold,** the page goes to the next tier or, after the last tier, to a human review queue.\n- **This gate** is what keeps extraction failures from becoming silent retrieval failures.\n\n### 6. Output, versioning and isolation\n- **Store canonical JSON next to the raw bytes** with `parser_tier` and `parser_version`, so a layout-model\n  upgrade re-processes only documents parsed by the old version or the lower tier.\n- **Isolate parsers** in workers with timeouts and memory limits, because a malformed PDF can hang or exhaust\n  a process; record failures per document rather than failing the batch.\n- **Run a gold evaluation set** (gold text and gold tables, measured by character error rate and table cell\n  accuracy) on every parser change.","example":"### A per-page router with escalation\nA text-layer check, a quality score and escalation. The extractors are stand-ins for real tiers that take\none page and return canonical blocks; the control flow is the point.\n\n```python\nimport fitz, re                                   # PyMuPDF\n\nMIN_CHARS_PER_PAGE, MIN_WORD_RATIO = 200, 0.85\n\ndef quality(blocks):                              # English prose heuristic; tables scored separately\n    text = \" \".join(b[\"text\"] for b in blocks if b[\"type\"] not in (\"header\", \"footer\", \"table\"))\n    tokens = text.split()\n    if not tokens:\n        return 0.0\n    wordlike = sum(1 for t in tokens if re.search(r\"[A-Za-z]{2,}\", t))\n    garbage = text.count(\"\\ufffd\") + len(re.findall(r\"(.)\\1{5,}\", text))\n    return (wordlike / len(tokens)) * (0.0 if garbage > 20 else 1.0)\n\ndef process(path, tiers):\n    \"\"\"tiers: ordered list of (name, extract_page) returning canonical blocks for one page.\"\"\"\n    pages, errors = [], []\n    for page in fitz.open(path):\n        native = page.get_text().strip()\n        if not native and not page.get_images():\n            pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": \"blank\", \"blocks\": []}); continue\n        start = 0 if len(native) >= MIN_CHARS_PER_PAGE else 1   # no usable text layer: skip native\n        for name, extract in tiers[start:]:\n            try:\n                blocks = extract(page)                          # worker timeout in production\n            except Exception as e:                              # a bad page must not kill the batch\n                errors.append((page.number, name, repr(e))); continue\n            if (q := quality(blocks)) >= MIN_WORD_RATIO:\n                pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": name, \"quality\": q, \"blocks\": blocks})\n                break\n        else:\n            pages.append({\"page\": page.number, \"tier\": \"needs_review\", \"blocks\": []})\n    return {\"pages\": pages, \"errors\": errors}\n\n# tiers = [(\"native\", native_page), (\"ocr\", ocr_page), (\"layout\", layout_page), (\"vlm\", vlm_page)]\n```\n\n- **Store each record** with the raw file's hash and per-page tiers; count `needs_review` pages on the pipeline\n  dashboard rather than silently indexing them as empty.\n- **The word-ratio score is deliberately crude and English-specific.** It excludes table blocks, because a\n  correct financial table is mostly numbers and would fail the gate; tables need their own check\n  (consistent column counts per row, numeric cells that parse).\n- **Header and footer removal runs after routing,** across all pages, because it depends on text repeating\n  from page to page.\n\n### Worked scenario: a contract with a scanned annex\nA 60-page born-digital contract whose last 8 pages are a scanned, signed annex.\n\n| Pages | Route | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| 52 text pages | native tier | pass at near-zero cost |\n| 8 annex pages | no text layer, start at OCR | 6 pass |\n| 2 handwritten annex pages | fail the word-ratio gate | escalate through layout to the vision model |\n| Total | | 52 native, 8 OCR, 2 vision-LLM pages |\n\n- **A per-document router does worse either way.** It sends the whole file to native extraction because\n  87 % of pages have text, and silently loses the annex. Or, with a stricter threshold, it OCRs 52 pages that\n  already had perfect text.\n- **The cost argument scales.** At an illustrative vision-model price of one cent per page, a 1-million-page\n  corpus costs about $10,000 per full pass with every page on the vision model, and about $500 when only the\n  5 % that fail the cheaper tiers go there.\n- **Re-processing after a parser change** multiplies whichever figure you chose.\n\n### Removing repeated headers and footers\nRunnable as-is. It normalizes digits so \"Page 3 of 62\" and \"Page 4 of 62\" count as the same line, then drops\nedge lines that repeat on most pages.\n\n```python\nimport re\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef norm(line):\n    return re.sub(r\"\\d+\", \"#\", line.strip().lower())       # \"Page 14 of 62\" -> \"page # of #\"\n\ndef strip_repeating(pages, min_share=0.6, edge=1):\n    \"\"\"pages: list of line lists. Drop lines in the top/bottom `edge` positions whose normalized\n    text repeats on at least min_share of pages: headers, footers, page numbers.\"\"\"\n    counts = Counter()\n    for lines in pages:\n        counts.update({norm(l) for l in lines[:edge] + lines[-edge:]})\n    repeated = {k for k, c in counts.items() if c >= min_share * len(pages)}\n    cleaned = []\n    for lines in pages:\n        n = len(lines)\n        cleaned.append([l for i, l in enumerate(lines)\n                        if not ((i < edge or i >= n - edge) and norm(l) in repeated)])\n    return cleaned, repeated\n\nbodies = [\"Scope of services.\", \"Fees and payment.\", \"Revenue grew 12% in Q3.\", \"Termination.\", \"Signatures.\"]\npages = [[\"ACME Corp - Master Agreement\", body, f\"Confidential - Page {p} of 62\"]\n         for p, body in enumerate(bodies, start=1)]\ncleaned, repeated = strip_repeating(pages)\nprint(sorted(repeated))   # ['acme corp - master agreement', 'confidential - page # of #']\nprint(cleaned[2])         # ['Revenue grew 12% in Q3.']\n```","patterns":["**Detect type from content.** At intake, sniff the MIME type from the first bytes (for example `magic.from_buffer(data, mime=True)` with python-magic) and route by that, not the extension. Monitor the count of extension/content mismatches per source; a spike usually means a new export format.","**Sandboxed parser workers.** Run each extractor in a separate process or container with a hard timeout and memory limit, and record timeouts and OOM kills per document. Monitor failure rate by file type and parser version.","**Cache expensive extractions by page hash.** Key vision-LLM and layout outputs by page content hash, model and prompt version, and reuse them on re-runs. This restores reproducibility and caps cost; monitor cache hit rate after parser changes.","**Gold-set regression gate.** Keep a few dozen hand-verified documents per document class and block a parser change if character error rate or table cell accuracy regresses. Track both per class.","**Escalation-rate alarm.** Chart the share of pages reaching each tier per source per day; a climbing escalation rate is the earliest sign of a new document type entering the corpus."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Intake: magic bytes, text layer, language\"] --> R{\"Usable text layer?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| N[\"Tier 1: native extraction\"]\n    R -->|\"no\"| O[\"Tier 2: OCR\"]\n    N --> Q{\"Quality score passes?\"}\n    O --> Q\n    Q -->|\"no\"| L[\"Tier 3: layout model\"]\n    L --> Q2{\"Passes?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| V[\"Tier 4: vision LLM, cached\"]\n    V -.->|\"still fails\"| H[\"Human review queue\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Canonical blocks + cleaning\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| C\n    V --> C\n    C --> S[(\"JSON with parser tier and version\")]","caption":"Each page starts at the cheapest tier that can work for it and climbs only when its quality score fails;\ndocument-level cleaning runs after all pages are assembled."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not send every page to a vision LLM, given they handle almost everything?","a":"- **Cost:** per page it is orders of magnitude more than native extraction; at an illustrative one cent per\n  page, 1 million pages is about $10,000 per full pass versus about $500 if only the 5 % that fail cheaper\n  tiers escalate.\n- **Latency and determinism:** it is slow, and re-runs produce slightly different text, so chunks and\n  embeddings churn.\n- **So** use it as the last tier, gated by a quality score and cached by page hash."},{"q":"Users say a contract \"should be there\" but retrieval never finds its annex. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the per-page record:** which tier processed each annex page and what quality it scored.\n- **Likely cause:** whole-document routing sent a mostly born-digital file to native extraction, and the\n  scanned annex yielded empty text that was indexed as nothing.\n- **Fix:** route per page, flag pages with near-zero characters, and count `needs_review` pages on a dashboard."},{"q":"What belongs in a canonical document model, and why have one?","a":"- **Typed, ordered blocks** with `type`, `text`, `page`, `bbox` and a `parent` for hierarchy; tables as cell\n  grids plus a Markdown or HTML serialization.\n- **Why:** chunking, metadata extraction and citations depend on one schema, so swapping or escalating an\n  extractor changes nothing downstream."},{"q":"When would you skip the tier ladder?","a":"- **A corpus that is almost entirely scans:** start at OCR, since tier one would fail nearly every page.\n- **A homogeneous Markdown or HTML corpus:** only cleaning and the canonical model are needed.\n- **Re-evaluate** when the escalation rate changes, because that signals a new document type."},{"q":"How do you score extraction quality without ground truth for every document?","a":"- **Cheap per-page heuristics:** characters per page against an expectation, OCR mean confidence, word-like\n  token ratio, single-character token ratio, replacement characters and repeated glyphs.\n- **Separate table checks:** consistent column counts and numeric cells that parse, because a correct table\n  fails a prose word-ratio test.\n- **Calibrate on a gold set** measured by character error rate and table cell accuracy."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tiered routing vs extra passes.** Average cost stays near the cheapest extractor, but there is a scoring step and a second pass for escalated pages. For a corpus that is almost entirely scans, start at OCR.","**Layout models and vision LLMs vs cost.** They recover tables and reading order native extraction loses, at a cost and latency that make them wrong for the whole corpus until retrieval evals prove the gain on your document types.","**Rich canonical model vs effort.** Bounding boxes and hierarchy enable precise citations and structure-aware chunking, but are more work to populate from simple sources such as plain HTML or email.","**Deterministic extractors vs vision LLMs.** Deterministic re-runs are reproducible, which vision LLM output is not; cache LLM extractions by page hash and treat them as versioned artifacts to recover reproducibility."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the file extension.** Symptom: parser crashes on files that are secretly HTML, images or zip archives, and documents marked processed that produced nothing.","**Not detecting scanned PDFs.** Symptom: documents in the index with zero or one chunk, found when a user asks about a contract that \"should be there\".","**Skipping header and footer removal.** Symptom: chunks that begin with a confidentiality notice and a page number, and retrieval that favours documents with the longest boilerplate.","**Flattening tables to text.** Symptom: numeric questions answered with the right number from the wrong column or year.","**No parser version in the output.** Symptom: you cannot re-process only the documents an old, buggy extractor touched, so every parser fix becomes a full re-run.","**Routing whole documents by an average text-layer ratio.** Symptom: a contract's scanned signature annex is missing from the index while the rest retrieves fine.","**Running parsers in the orchestrator's process.** Symptom: one malformed file hangs or OOM-kills an entire nightly batch."],"when":"- **Use** a tiered pipeline as soon as a corpus mixes formats or includes scans, tables or multi-column\n  layouts, which describes nearly every enterprise document set.\n- **A homogeneous Markdown or HTML corpus** needs only the cleaning and canonical-model steps.\n- **Measure** text-layer ratio, per-tier volume and cost, quality-score distribution, escalation rate,\n  review-queue size, and extraction accuracy on the gold set.\n- **Day to day,** this is where retrieval incidents are traced to and fixed.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you ingest 50,000 mixed PDFs for RAG?\" Answer with classification, routing by\n  cost, a canonical structure, quality gates and selective re-processing by parser version, not with the\n  name of a library.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements classification, the extractor adapters that emit the canonical block model, header and footer removal, and the quality score, and runs the gold-set accuracy check on every change.","architect":"Defines the canonical document schema and the tier ladder with its cost and quality thresholds, and decides where vision LLM extraction is allowed given cost, latency and data policy.","fde":"Profiles the customer's real documents in the first week, finds the scans and the table-heavy forms the demo never saw, and sets expectations about which document classes need review or custom handling."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG system answers well for policy documents but fails on their financial reports,\nwhich are multi-column PDFs with many tables; some older reports are scans. The current pipeline uses one\ntext-extraction library for everything. Describe how you would restructure processing and how you would\nknow it worked.","a":"First, classify: detect the scans by text-layer ratio and send them to OCR with deskewing instead of\nnative extraction, which currently yields nothing for them. Second, route the born-digital reports to a\nlayout-aware extractor that reconstructs reading order across columns and emits tables as cell grids,\nserialized to Markdown for embedding, with the original cells kept for citation. Third, define a canonical\nblock model so both routes produce the same structure, remove repeated headers and footers, and store\nparser tier and version with each output. Fourth, add a quality score with escalation to a vision LLM for\npages that fail, cached by page hash. To know it worked: build a small gold set from the failing reports\nwith hand-verified text and table cells, measure character error rate and cell accuracy before and after,\nand re-run the retrieval evaluation on the questions that used to fail, expecting the numeric questions to\nretrieve the correct table chunk."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why route documents through tiers rather than sending every page to the best available extractor?","options":["The best extractors cannot read born-digital PDFs","Cost and latency per page differ by orders of magnitude across tiers, and most pages are handled well by the cheapest one, so escalation should be driven by measured failure","Vision LLMs are not permitted to process documents in most jurisdictions","Layout models require a text layer to operate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A typical corpus is mostly born-digital text where native extraction is accurate and nearly free. A quality gate identifies the minority of pages that need OCR, layout analysis or a vision model, keeping spend proportional to difficulty."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a canonical document model shared by all extractors?","options":["It compresses documents for cheaper storage","It lets the vector database index documents without chunking","It decouples chunking, metadata and citation logic from each extractor's output format, so documents can be re-processed by a better tier without downstream changes","It guarantees deterministic output from vision LLMs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Downstream stages should depend on one schema of typed, ordered blocks with hierarchy and tables as cells. Then swapping or upgrading an extractor, or escalating a document to a higher tier, changes nothing after the parsing stage."},{"q":"A quality gate flags a batch of documents with chars-per-page near zero after native extraction. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["The documents are written in a language the chunker does not support","The pages are scanned images without a text layer and need OCR","The embedding model rejected the text as too short","The headers and footers were removed too aggressively"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Native extraction reads the PDF's text layer; scans have none, so the output is empty even though the pages are full of content. The gate exists precisely to catch this and route the documents to OCR instead of indexing them as empty."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/vector-database-architecture","sources":["Malkov & Yashunin, 2018 — Efficient and robust approximate nearest neighbor search using Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs","Subramanya et al., 2019 — DiskANN: Fast Accurate Billion-point Nearest Neighbor Search on a Single Node","Official docs: Qdrant","Official docs: pgvector"],"why":"- **Exact search does not scale.** It is a dot product per stored vector. A million 1,536-dimensional float32\n  vectors is about 6 GB of reads per query, which at the tens of GB/s of server memory bandwidth is on the\n  order of 100 ms or more per query, growing linearly with the corpus.\n- **ANN fixes the math, not the system.** HNSW cuts the work to a few thousand distance computations by\n  navigating a graph (the rag-retrieval module explains ANN). But an index alone is a library call; production\n  also needs live adds and deletes, metadata filters (tenant, date, ACL), restart durability, replication and\n  sharding.\n- **Each requirement collides with the index.** HNSW graph quality depends on insertion order and nodes cannot\n  be removed without disconnecting the graph, so deletes become tombstones and rebuilds. A filter after top-k\n  yields fewer than k, or zero, results when selective; a filter before means searching a set the graph was\n  not built for.\n- **Memory and distribution collide too.** Graph traversal is random memory access, so an index that spills\n  to disk becomes orders of magnitude slower unless designed for SSDs. Sharded similarity search means every\n  shard answers and results are merged.\n- **A vector database is the engineering that resolves those collisions.** Choosing a dedicated engine or a\n  vector extension in Postgres or OpenSearch is choosing which collisions you need solved and which you can\n  live with.","how":"### Data model\n- **Points in a collection:** an id, one or more named vectors (dense, and in many engines sparse for hybrid\n  search), and a payload of key-value metadata.\n- **Payload indexes** (keyword, numeric range, geo; typically inverted or bitmap) on every filtered field,\n  because filter evaluation must produce candidate id sets quickly.\n\n### Index layer and why it is memory-bound\n- **HNSW** keeps a layered proximity graph. A query enters at the top layer, greedily walks to the nearest\n  node, descends, and at the base layer explores a beam of `ef` candidates.\n- **Each hop reads a neighbour list and a vector from unpredictable addresses,** so throughput tracks memory\n  latency and the index must be RAM-resident.\n- **IVF** partitions vectors into k-means clusters and scans a few posting lists: more sequential and cheaper\n  in memory, but needs training and loses recall on skewed data.\n- **Quantization** (scalar int8, product quantization, binary) shrinks the vectors touched during traversal.\n  The small recall loss is recovered by rescoring final candidates against full-precision vectors on disk.\n- **DiskANN-style designs** keep a compressed copy in RAM to guide the walk and put full vectors plus\n  adjacency on SSD, one read per visited node; that is how billion-scale search fits on one machine.\n\n### Write path: segments, tombstones and compaction\nWrites do not go into the graph directly. The common design, shared in spirit by Qdrant, Milvus and\nLucene-based engines:\n1. Append to a write-ahead log for durability.\n2. Insert into a small mutable segment searched by brute force.\n3. At a threshold, seal it and build an index in the background.\n4. Serve queries across all segments and merge.\n5. Deletes mark a tombstone bitmap consulted at query time; updates are a delete plus an insert.\n6. Periodic compaction merges segments, drops tombstoned points and rebuilds the graph.\n\n- **Why:** writes stay cheap and the index stays immutable.\n- **The cost:** eventual visibility (a point is searchable once it lands in the mutable segment, possibly after\n  a refresh interval), and query cost that creeps up between compactions as tombstones and segment counts\n  grow.\n\n### Query path and the filtering problem\nA query: embed (outside the database), evaluate the filter to a candidate set, search, merge across segments\nand shards, rescore if quantized, fetch payloads, return top-k. The filter strategy is the subtle part.\n\n| Strategy | How | When it works | When it fails |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Post-filtering | ANN for top-k′ (k′ > k), then drop points failing the filter | filter matches most points | selective filters: at 1 % matching, top-100 has on average one survivor |\n| Pre-filtering | allowed-id bitmap from the payload index, search only among them | always correct; engines brute-force the allowed set when it is small, fine precisely because it is small | traversal restricted to 1 % of nodes is disconnected; exact scan is expensive when the set is large |\n| Filter-aware traversal | check the filter at each hop, keep walking through disallowed nodes without returning them | mid and large selectivity | needs engine support |\n\n**How engines implement filter-aware search:**\n- **Qdrant** builds extra graph links per indexed payload field so the filtered subgraph stays connected.\n- **Weaviate** uses a flat-search cutoff (`flatSearchCutoff`) below which it abandons the graph; newer\n  releases also offer an ACORN-style filtered traversal.\n- **pgvector** historically returned fewer than k rows under a selective `WHERE` because the index scan ran\n  first; newer releases added iterative index scans that keep walking until enough rows pass.\n\nA plain `WHERE tenant_id = ?` across millions of points is therefore an architecture question, not a syntax\nquestion, and the first thing to test when evaluating an engine.\n\n### Distribution and multi-tenancy\n- **Shard by hashed point id:** even load, but every query scatters to all shards and merges, so latency is\n  the slowest shard's.\n- **Shard by a routing key such as tenant:** one shard per query, faster and a natural isolation boundary, at\n  the cost of hot shards for large tenants.\n- **Replication** gives availability and read scaling; most engines offer per-request consistency levels\n  because replicas apply writes asynchronously.\n\n| Multi-tenancy pattern | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Collection per tenant | strong isolation | each HNSW graph carries fixed overhead; thousands of tiny collections waste memory |\n| Payload filter per tenant | cheap | relies on the filtered-search path and on nobody forgetting the filter |\n| Tenant-partitioned indexes (a graph per tenant value inside one collection) | the usual recommendation for many small tenants | engine support required |\n\n### Dedicated engine versus extension\n- **pgvector:** ANN inside the database that already holds the chunks, with transactions, joins and row\n  security. Right until the index no longer fits comfortably in the database's memory, or filtered recall and\n  write throughput become the bottleneck.\n- **OpenSearch and Elasticsearch:** HNSW beside BM25 and existing log infrastructure.\n- **Dedicated engines:** win on filtered-search machinery, quantization options, segment management and\n  horizontal scaling.\n- The architecture above is the lens for deciding which of those you will actually hit.","example":"### The filtering problem, made visible\nThis simulation uses exact similarity, so the only variable is *when* the filter is applied. One collection\nholds three tenants owning 90 %, 9.5 % and 0.5 % of the points. Post-filtering takes the top-k′ by\nsimilarity, then drops other tenants' points; pre-filtering builds the allowed-id set first and scans it\nexactly.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, D, K = 200_000, 64, 10\nX = rng.standard_normal((N, D)).astype(np.float32)\nX /= np.linalg.norm(X, axis=1, keepdims=True)              # cosine = dot product\nshare = {\"big\": 0.90, \"mid\": 0.095, \"small\": 0.005}         # tenant sizes: 90 %, 9.5 %, 0.5 %\ntenant = rng.choice(list(share), size=N, p=list(share.values()))\n\ndef post_filter(q, t, k_prime):                             # ANN-style: top-k' first, then filter\n    top = np.argpartition(-(X @ q), k_prime)[:k_prime]\n    return [i for i in top if tenant[i] == t][:K]\n\ndef pre_filter(q, t):                                       # allowed ids first, exact scan over them\n    allowed = np.flatnonzero(tenant == t)\n    scores = X[allowed] @ q\n    return allowed[np.argsort(-scores)[:K]], len(allowed)\n\nq = rng.standard_normal(D).astype(np.float32); q /= np.linalg.norm(q)\nfor t in share:\n    got = [len(post_filter(q, t, kp)) for kp in (10, 100, 1000)]\n    ids, scanned = pre_filter(q, t)\n    print(f\"{t:>5}: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> {got}; \"\n          f\"pre-filter -> {len(ids)} results, scanned {scanned:,} vectors\")\n# Output (seed 0):\n#   big: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [9, 10, 10]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 179,893 vectors\n#   mid: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [1, 10, 10]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 19,110 vectors\n# small: post-filter results for k'=10/100/1000 -> [0, 0, 4]; pre-filter -> 10 results, scanned 997 vectors\n```\n\n- **Read the output as the planner's decision table.** Post-filtering returns about `k′ × selectivity`\n  survivors: fine for the big tenant, empty for the small one even at k′ = 1,000, a hundred times the\n  requested k.\n- **Pre-filtering is always correct,** but its cost is the size of the allowed set.\n\n### Worked scenario: scaling the same tenants to 10 million vectors\nA production collection of 10 million 1,024-dimensional float32 vectors (4 KB each), same tenant shares.\n\n| Tenant | Vectors | Bytes to scan exactly | Right strategy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| small (0.5 %) | 50,000 | about 200 MB: a few ms to a few tens of ms on one node, depending on memory bandwidth and threads | exact scan of the allowed set |\n| big (90 %) | 9 million | about 36 GB: far too much per query | graph walk checking the filter during traversal, where the filter hardly prunes |\n| mid (9.5 %) | about 950,000 | about 3.8 GB | neither is cheap: the range to benchmark |\n\n- **A good engine picks per query.** It estimates the allowed-set size from the payload index: exact scan below\n  a threshold (Qdrant's `full_scan_threshold`, Weaviate's `flatSearchCutoff`), filter-aware graph search above.\n- **Test the tenant sizes in between,** where neither strategy is cheap.\n\n### Production setup in Qdrant\nIn a real engine the setup is a payload index on the filtered field, for example\n`client.create_payload_index(\"chunks\", field_name=\"tenant\", field_schema=\"keyword\")` in Qdrant, plus a filter\npassed with the query. For many small tenants, Qdrant's multitenancy setup builds graphs per tenant value\ninstead of one global graph.\n\n```python\nfrom qdrant_client import QdrantClient, models\n\nclient = QdrantClient(url=\"http://localhost:6333\")\nclient.create_collection(\n    \"chunks\",\n    vectors_config=models.VectorParams(size=1024, distance=models.Distance.COSINE),\n    hnsw_config=models.HnswConfigDiff(payload_m=16, m=0),     # per-tenant graphs, no global graph\n)\nclient.create_payload_index(\n    \"chunks\", field_name=\"tenant\",\n    field_schema=models.KeywordIndexParams(type=\"keyword\", is_tenant=True),\n)\n\ndef search(tenant_from_auth, query_vector, k=10):            # tenant comes from auth, never the request\n    return client.query_points(\n        \"chunks\", query=query_vector, limit=k,\n        query_filter=models.Filter(must=[\n            models.FieldCondition(key=\"tenant\", match=models.MatchValue(value=tenant_from_auth))]),\n    ).points\n```\n\n- **Deletes are eventual in memory.** A delete marks a tombstone and returns at once; its memory comes back\n  when the segment is optimized.\n- **Neither behaviour shows on a toy collection,** because servers brute-force segments below their indexing\n  threshold. Benchmark at realistic sizes.","patterns":["**Shadow exact search for recall.** Sample a few hundred real queries with their real filters each week, run them with exact search (Qdrant's `exact=True` search parameter, or a sequential scan in Postgres) and with normal settings, and chart recall@k next to p99 latency. Alert when recall drops after weeks of writes.","**Turn on iterative scans in pgvector.** On pgvector 0.8 or later, set `hnsw.iterative_scan` (for example to `relaxed_order`) so filtered queries keep walking until enough rows pass, and tune `hnsw.ef_search`. Monitor queries that return fewer than k rows.","**Quantize with rescoring.** Enable scalar int8 quantization with rescoring against original vectors for collections that strain RAM, and keep originals on disk. Compare recall@k before and after on the same query sample.","**Watch segment health.** Track segment count, deleted-point count and optimizer status per collection; rising tombstones with flat traffic mean compaction is falling behind, and latency will follow.","**Isolate whales, partition minnows.** Put the few very large tenants on dedicated shards or collections and keep many small tenants in one collection with per-tenant indexing. Monitor p99 latency per tenant size bucket, not just overall."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Upsert or delete\"] --> WAL[(\"Write-ahead log\")]\n    WAL --> MS[\"Mutable segment, brute force\"]\n    MS -->|\"threshold reached\"| IS[\"Sealed segment, HNSW built\"]\n    W -.->|\"delete\"| TB[\"Tombstone bitmap\"]\n    IS -->|\"compaction drops tombstones\"| IS\n    Q[\"Query vector + filter\"] --> P{\"Estimated allowed-set size\"}\n    P -->|\"small\"| EX[\"Exact scan of allowed ids\"]\n    P -->|\"large\"| FT[\"Filter-aware graph walk\"]\n    EX --> M[\"Merge segments, skip tombstones, rescore\"]\n    FT --> M\n    M --> R[\"Top-k + payloads\"]","caption":"Writes land in a log and a brute-force segment before background indexing; queries plan the filter by\nestimated selectivity, search every segment, skip tombstones and merge."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you just bolt a metadata filter onto an HNSW search?","a":"- **Post-filtering loses results:** survivors are about k′ × selectivity, so a 0.5 % tenant gets nothing from a\n  top-100 and only a handful from a top-1,000.\n- **Pre-filtering breaks the graph:** restricting traversal to a small allowed set leaves it disconnected, so\n  engines brute-force small sets and use filter-aware traversal for large ones.\n- **So the planner must estimate selectivity per query,** which is why payload indexes are mandatory."},{"q":"Deletes return instantly, yet memory and latency keep climbing. What is going on?","a":"- **Tombstones, not removal:** a delete marks a bitmap consulted at query time; the graph node and memory stay\n  until compaction rebuilds the segment.\n- **Check** deleted-point counts, segment counts and optimizer status; a backlog means compaction is starved.\n- **Fix:** give compaction resources or schedule it after bulk deletes, and re-measure recall afterwards."},{"q":"When would you stay on pgvector instead of a dedicated vector database?","a":"- **When the chunks already live in Postgres** and the corpus is in the low millions: you get transactions,\n  joins and row security with no extra system.\n- **Move** when the index no longer fits comfortably in the database's memory, or filtered recall and write\n  throughput become the bottleneck.\n- **Check first** whether iterative index scans on a newer pgvector already fix the filtered-recall problem."},{"q":"How many bytes does an exact scan read per query at 10 million 1,024-dimensional vectors?","a":"- **Per vector:** 1,024 × 4 bytes = 4 KB, so the whole collection is about 40 GB of reads.\n- **Per tenant:** a 0.5 % tenant is about 200 MB, cheap to scan; a 90 % tenant is about 36 GB, which needs the\n  graph.\n- **The planner's threshold** sits between those, and the mid-sized tenants are what you benchmark."},{"q":"How do you design multi-tenancy for thousands of tenants of very different sizes?","a":"- **Many small tenants:** one collection with per-tenant index structure, so each is searched exactly or as a\n  small graph.\n- **A few very large tenants:** dedicated shards or collections for isolation and predictable latency.\n- **Security:** a retrieval service derives the tenant from authentication and always applies the filter."}],"tradeoffs":["**HNSW vs IVF or disk-based indexes.** HNSW gives high recall at low latency but costs RAM for links plus vectors and a slow build; IVF or disk-based indexes win when the corpus is billions of vectors or memory is the budget line.","**Quantization vs recall.** It cuts index memory by 4× to 32× and often speeds traversal, at a recall cost that rescoring mostly recovers; not worth it when the collection fits unquantized anyway.","**Pre-filtering vs cost.** Correct for selective filters but expensive when the filter matches most of the collection; engines pick per query by estimated selectivity, and you should test both extremes.","**Tenant-routed sharding vs large tenants.** Isolation and single-shard queries, but a tenant larger than one shard forces routing plus scatter-gather, which most engines handle less gracefully.","**Extension vs dedicated engine.** An extension saves a system and gives transactions with the chunk tables; it flips toward a dedicated engine when filtered recall, write throughput or memory residency in the shared database become the bottleneck."],"pitfalls":["**Post-filtering with a selective filter.** Symptom: queries return fewer than k results, or none, for small tenants while large tenants work fine.","**Measuring recall only on a freshly built index.** Symptom: retrieval quality and latency drift worse over months as tombstones and segment fragmentation accumulate without compaction.","**Sizing memory for vectors alone,** forgetting graph links, payload and merge headroom. Symptom: OOM kills during background index builds rather than steady-state queries.","**Large payloads such as full chunk text inside the vector engine.** Symptom: RAM dominated by text while the index itself is small, and slow snapshots and rebalances.","**Relying on application code to add the tenant filter.** Symptom: a cross-tenant result in a log or a security review, which the ACL-aware retrieval lesson treats as a structural problem.","**Expecting writes to be immediately searchable.** Symptom: an integration test that upserts and queries in the same millisecond and intermittently fails."],"when":"- **Think in these terms** whenever you choose or tune a vector store, size a cluster, or debug retrieval that\n  is correct in notebooks and wrong in production.\n- **Answer concretely:** how selective are your filters, how many writes and deletes per hour, does the index\n  fit in RAM with headroom, how many tenants and how skewed, and what recall at what p99 latency.\n- **Measure recall precisely.** Take a few hundred real queries with their real filters, compute exact top-k\n  by brute force, report the fraction of those ids the engine returns at the p99 latency of the same\n  settings. Repeat after a month of writes and deletes, because a fresh index is the best case.\n- **Start with pgvector** when the chunk tables already live in Postgres and the corpus is in the low\n  millions; move to a dedicated engine when one of the mechanisms above is what you need.\n- **Interviews:** explaining why a selective filter breaks naive ANN, and how segments and tombstones make\n  deletes eventual, is the level expected of an architect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Creates payload indexes for every filtered field, sets `ef` and quantization per collection, tests filtered recall at real selectivity, and schedules or monitors compaction.","architect":"Chooses between an extension and a dedicated engine, defines the sharding and multi-tenancy pattern, and sets recall, latency and freshness targets the engine must be benchmarked against.","fde":"Maps the customer's tenant, ACL and retention requirements onto the engine's filtering and isolation model, and demonstrates that a deleted document stops being retrievable within the agreed window."},"check":{"q":"A SaaS product serves 3,000 tenants from one collection using a payload filter on `tenant_id`.\nSmall tenants complain that search returns only one or two results, large tenants report slow queries,\nand a security audit asks how cross-tenant leakage is prevented. Explain the mechanisms behind each\nsymptom and propose an architecture.","a":"Small tenants get few results because the filter is applied after similarity search: the engine\ntakes the top-k′ over all 3,000 tenants and then discards the others, so a tenant owning 0.1 % of points\ngets about `k′ × 0.001` survivors, which is effectively none. Large tenants are slow for related reasons.\nThe usual workaround for small tenants, a larger k′ or `ef` on every query, makes each query walk much\nmore of the shared graph. And a large tenant's points are spread over every shard, so each of its\nqueries pays scatter-gather and waits for the slowest shard. Leakage prevention currently depends on\napplication code remembering the filter. The fix has three parts. First, give the engine what it needs\nto plan by selectivity: a payload index on `tenant_id`, and ideally per-tenant index structure inside the\ncollection (in Qdrant, a tenant-marked keyword index with graphs built per payload value instead of one\nglobal graph). Small tenants are then searched exactly or as small graphs, and none of them is a sliver\nof a huge one. Second, put the handful of very large tenants on dedicated shards or collections, which\ngives them isolation and predictable latency. Third, enforce the filter in a retrieval service that\nderives `tenant_id` from authentication, never from the request body. Then verify with recall@k against\nexact search, measured separately at the smallest, median and largest tenant sizes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does an HNSW index need to be resident in RAM to perform well?","options":["Because the distance computation uses SIMD instructions that require RAM","Because graph traversal reads neighbour lists and vectors from unpredictable addresses, so each hop is a random access that disk cannot serve quickly","Because the graph is rebuilt on every query","Because payload filters are stored in the same memory region"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A query walks tens to thousands of nodes, each at an address determined by the previous hop. Random reads from SSD are orders of magnitude slower than from memory, which is why disk-based designs like DiskANN keep a compressed copy in RAM to guide the walk and read full vectors once per visited node."},{"q":"A deletion in a vector database returns instantly but memory is not freed. What explains this?","options":["The engine batches deletes until the next replica sync","Deletes are only applied to the payload store, not the vectors","The point is marked with a tombstone consulted at query time, and the graph and memory are rebuilt during a later compaction","Vector indexes cannot remove nodes, so deletes are ignored"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Removing a node from a proximity graph would leave neighbours disconnected and degrade recall, so engines mark deletions and filter them out of results. Compaction later rebuilds segments without the tombstoned points, which is when memory and query cost recover."},{"q":"With a filter that matches 0.5 % of a collection, which filtering strategy is likely to return correct top-k results?","options":["Retrieve the top-k by similarity, then discard points failing the filter","Compute the matching ids first and search only within them, falling back to brute force over that small set","Keep post-filtering but double the candidate count k′ before discarding non-matching points","Disable the HNSW index and rely on the payload index alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With 0.5 % selectivity, a top-100 similarity result contains about half a matching point on average, and doubling k′ to 200 still yields about one, so post-filtering returns almost nothing. Pre-filtering yields a small candidate set that is cheap to scan exactly, which is why engines switch strategy based on estimated selectivity."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/vector-store-sizing","sources":["Jégou et al., 2011 — Product Quantization for Nearest Neighbor Search","Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning","Official docs: hnswlib","Official docs: Qdrant"],"why":"- **Teams size vector stores by feel.** They pick a managed tier that \"looks big enough\" or the instance with\n  the most RAM they can justify. Both fail.\n- **Under-sizing fails in production.** Out-of-memory during a background index build, p99 latency jumping\n  when the index spills out of memory, or a managed plan that silently caps vectors so the last 30 % of the\n  corpus never lands.\n- **Over-sizing fails on the invoice.** A five-figure monthly bill for a corpus that would fit in a Postgres\n  replica.\n- **The inputs are not mysterious; omission is what breaks estimates.** Counting documents instead of chunks\n  (a 10× error), forgetting the ANN graph's own memory, storing full chunk text beside each vector and\n  finding text dominates, or forgetting a re-embedding migration doubles the collection while it runs.\n- **Same discipline as KV-cache sizing in serving.** This lesson is the checklist and the arithmetic, so the\n  number comes from the corpus rather than a pricing page.","how":"### Step 1: count vectors\n- **Formula:** `vectors = documents × chunks_per_document × vectors_per_chunk`.\n- **With token-based chunking:** `chunks_per_document ≈ tokens_per_document / (chunk_tokens − overlap_tokens)`,\n  rounded up per document.\n- **`vectors_per_chunk`** is 1 for standard dense retrieval, more than 1 if you embed several views (title\n  plus body, or a summary), and much more for late-interaction models that store one vector per token.\n- **Add growth:** a corpus adding 2 % per month is 27 % larger in a year.\n\n### Step 2: bytes per vector\n`dim × bytes_per_dim`:\n\n| Precision | Bytes per dimension |\n|---|---|\n| float32 | 4 |\n| float16 | 2 |\n| scalar int8 quantization | 1 |\n| binary quantization | 1 bit (one eighth of a byte) |\n| product quantization | a fixed number of bytes per vector, commonly tens of bytes |\n\n- **Dimension comes from the model.** Common text embedding models sit between 384 and 3,072 dimensions;\n  Matryoshka-style models let you truncate to a smaller prefix at a quality cost you must measure.\n- **Quantization frees RAM, not disk.** Quantized indexes usually keep full-precision vectors on disk for\n  rescoring.\n\n### Step 3: index overhead\n- **HNSW stores neighbour lists.** With degree parameter `M`, the base layer holds up to `2M` links per node,\n  each a 4-byte id, so links cost about `2 × M × 4` bytes per vector plus a little for upper layers.\n- **`M = 16` gives roughly 128 bytes per vector.** The hnswlib parameter guide puts link memory at roughly\n  `M × 8–10` bytes per element, which matches.\n- **Treat it as a lower bound,** because engines add per-point bookkeeping (ids, versions, locks).\n- **IVF** adds only centroids and posting lists of ids, negligible next to the vectors.\n\n### Step 4: payload and secondary indexes\n- **Everything beside the vector costs memory** in most engines: the id, filterable fields and their indexes,\n  and the chunk text if you keep it there.\n- **Text is the surprise.** At roughly 4 characters per token (an English-prose approximation), a 400-token\n  chunk is about 1.6 KB, larger than an int8-quantized 1,024-dimensional vector.\n- **If memory is the constraint,** keep text and rich metadata in Postgres or object storage keyed by\n  `chunk_id`, and store only ids and filter fields with the vector.\n\n### Step 5: replicas and headroom\n- **Multiply by the replication factor** for availability.\n- **Add headroom,** because segment merges and index rebuilds temporarily hold two copies of a segment, and\n  running at the edge of RAM turns every compaction into an incident. About 1.5 is a common rule of thumb.\n- **Know what 1.5 is applied to.** Qdrant's capacity guidance multiplies raw vector bytes by 1.5 to cover\n  index structures, metadata and temporary segments together. This lesson itemizes links and payload first,\n  then applies 1.5× as build headroom: the more conservative reading. Treat either as a floor, not a target.\n\n### Step 6: throughput and shards\n- **Memory gives one lower bound on node count; QPS gives another.**\n- **Per-node throughput** depends on dimension, `ef`, filter selectivity and hardware, so measure it with your\n  own vectors and filters, not a benchmark.\n- **Formula:** `nodes = max(ceil(total_RAM / RAM_per_node), ceil(peak_QPS / QPS_per_node))`, sized at peak\n  rather than average, within the latency budget the product allows retrieval.\n\n### Step 7: decide what must be RAM-resident, then size the disk\nThe line between \"must be in RAM\" and \"may be on disk\" is set by access pattern.\n\n| Data | Access pattern | Placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Graph links and the vectors traversal compares | random reads on every hop | RAM; if memory-mapped and the working set falls out of page cache, latency rises by orders of magnitude |\n| Full-precision vectors for rescoring | a few dozen to a few hundred per query (k times the oversampling factor) | disk on an SSD |\n| Payload | fetched only for the final k | disk |\n\n- **Engines expose this per collection** (for example on-disk vectors or payload in Qdrant, with the quantized\n  copy kept in RAM).\n- **Then size the disk separately:** full-precision vectors, the quantized copy, the write-ahead log, and\n  snapshots or backups, which are complete copies. Disk is cheap but not free, and the snapshot is what you\n  restore from.\n\n### Step 8: migrations\n- **A new embedding model means old and new vectors coexist until cutover.**\n- **Budget for the collection to double** during that window, or migrate tenant by tenant so the overlap is\n  bounded; the re-indexing lesson in this module covers the strategy.","example":"### Worked scenario: 200,000 documents\nA knowledge base has 200,000 documents averaging 4,000 tokens. Chunks are 400 tokens with 50 overlap, so\neach document yields about 4,000 / 350 ≈ 11.4 chunks; with rounding per document call it 12, giving 2.4\nmillion vectors. The embedding model is 1,024-dimensional.\n\n| Line item | Arithmetic | Size |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vectors in float32 | 2.4 M × 4,096 B | ≈ 9.8 GB |\n| HNSW links at `M = 16` | 2.4 M × 128 B | ≈ 0.3 GB |\n| Payload: chunk text (≈ 1.6 KB) plus ids and filter fields (≈ 0.4 KB) | 2.4 M × 2 KB | ≈ 4.8 GB |\n| Steady state per replica | sum | ≈ 14.9 GB |\n| With 1.5× headroom | × 1.5 | ≈ 22 GB |\n| Two replicas | × 2 | ≈ 45 GB of RAM in total |\n\n**Now change two decisions:**\n\n| Change | In-memory total per replica |\n|---|---|\n| Scalar int8: vectors become 2.4 M × 1,024 B ≈ 2.5 GB | 2.5 + 0.3 + 4.8 ≈ 7.6 GB; text is now the biggest line |\n| Also move text to the chunk table, keep 200 B of ids and filters | 2.5 + 0.3 + 0.5 ≈ 3.3 GB, about 5 GB with headroom |\n\n- **The same corpus went from large-memory nodes to one modest node,** with full-precision vectors (9.8 GB)\n  kept on disk for rescoring.\n- **Engine support varies.** pgvector offers half-precision (`halfvec`, 2 bytes per dimension) and binary\n  (`bit`) types rather than int8, so its nearest equivalent is about 4.9 GB of vectors, still small enough\n  for an existing database host.\n- **All figures are illustrative order-of-magnitude arithmetic;** measure recall after quantizing.\n\n**Push one step further to see the next bottleneck:**\n- **Binary quantization** stores 1,024 bits, which is 128 bytes per vector: 2.4 M × 128 B ≈ 0.3 GB, the same as\n  the HNSW links.\n- **Shrinking vectors further barely helps,** because the graph and per-point ids and filter fields now\n  dominate. Remaining levers: a smaller `M` (fewer links, lower recall), or IVF-style or disk-based indexes\n  whose overhead is not per-link.\n- **Binary codes lose more recall than int8,** so they normally come with heavier oversampling and rescoring\n  from full-precision vectors on disk.\n- **Each lever you pull exposes the next term of the formula as the largest one.**\n\n### A calculator you can keep\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef vector_store_bytes(docs, tokens_per_doc, chunk_tokens, overlap, dim,\n                       bytes_per_dim=4.0, M=16, payload_bytes=2048, replicas=2, headroom=1.5):\n    chunks_per_doc = math.ceil(tokens_per_doc / (chunk_tokens - overlap))\n    n = docs * chunks_per_doc\n    vec = n * dim * bytes_per_dim\n    links = n * 2 * M * 4                      # base-layer HNSW neighbour ids\n    payload = n * payload_bytes\n    per_replica = (vec + links + payload) * headroom\n    return {\"vectors\": n, \"vector_gb\": vec / 1e9, \"links_gb\": links / 1e9,\n            \"payload_gb\": payload / 1e9, \"ram_per_replica_gb\": per_replica / 1e9,\n            \"cluster_ram_gb\": per_replica * replicas / 1e9}\n\nbase  = vector_store_bytes(200_000, 4_000, 400, 50, 1024)\nlean  = vector_store_bytes(200_000, 4_000, 400, 50, 1024, bytes_per_dim=1.0, payload_bytes=200)\nfor name, r in ((\"float32 + text in engine\", base), (\"int8 + ids only\", lean)):\n    print(f\"{name}: {r['vectors']:,} vectors, {r['ram_per_replica_gb']:.1f} GB per replica, \"\n          f\"{r['cluster_ram_gb']:.1f} GB cluster\")\n```\n\nRun it with your own corpus numbers before any vendor conversation; the two scenarios print about 22.6 GB\nand 4.9 GB per replica, a factor of roughly 4.6 in RAM for the same retrieval corpus.\n\n### Node count: memory bound vs throughput bound\nRunnable as-is. The QPS figures are assumptions for illustration: 1,200 peak QPS and 400 QPS per node as if\nmeasured on staging with production-shaped filters.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef nodes_needed(cluster_ram_gb, ram_per_node_gb, peak_qps, qps_per_node):\n    by_ram = math.ceil(cluster_ram_gb / ram_per_node_gb)\n    by_qps = math.ceil(peak_qps / qps_per_node)            # qps_per_node: measured, not from a benchmark\n    return max(by_ram, by_qps), by_ram, by_qps\n\nprint(nodes_needed(45, 32, 1200, 400))      # (3, 2, 3): throughput binds, not memory\nprint(nodes_needed(90, 32, 1200, 400))      # migration window doubles RAM: (3, 3, 3)\n```\n\n- **Throughput can bind before memory.** Quantizing shrinks the RAM bound but does nothing for the QPS bound\n  unless it also speeds traversal.\n- **The migration window** doubles the RAM line, so check it still fits before an embedding upgrade.","patterns":["**Size from measured chunk statistics.** Run the real chunker over a sample of a few thousand documents and use the p50 and p95 chunks per document, not the mean of document lengths. Re-run when chunking changes and compare the vector count actually loaded with the estimate.","**Keep the quantized copy in RAM, originals on disk.** In Qdrant, store full vectors with `on_disk=True` on the vector config and keep scalar-quantized vectors in RAM (`always_ram=True` in the quantization config), with `on_disk_payload=True` for large payloads. Monitor p99 latency and page-cache misses after the change.","**Half-precision indexes in pgvector.** Index a cast of the column, for example `USING hnsw ((embedding::halfvec(1024)) halfvec_cosine_ops)`, to halve index memory, and query with the same cast so the index is used. Compare recall@k with the float32 index on a labelled query set.","**Alarm below the headroom line.** With a 1.5× headroom factor, steady-state RAM should stay under about two thirds of node memory; alert when it crosses that, before compaction fails rather than after.","**Bound the migration window.** Re-embed tenant by tenant or collection by collection, and delete the old vectors as each slice cuts over, so the doubling applies to one slice at a time. Track RAM per collection through the migration."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Documents x chunks per doc\"] --> V[\"Vector count\"]\n    V --> B[\"x bytes per vector: dim x precision\"]\n    V --> L[\"x HNSW links: about 2 x M x 4 B\"]\n    V --> P[\"x payload: ids, filters, text?\"]\n    B --> S[\"Steady state per replica\"]\n    L --> S\n    P --> S\n    S --> H[\"x 1.5 build headroom\"]\n    H --> R[\"x replicas = cluster RAM\"]\n    R --> N{\"Nodes = max of RAM bound and QPS bound\"}\n    Q[\"Peak QPS / measured QPS per node\"] --> N\n    R -.->|\"x 2 during re-embedding\"| M[\"Migration window\"]","caption":"The estimate is a chain of multiplications; each decision box is a lever that changes one term."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through estimating RAM for a vector store.","a":"- **Count vectors from chunks, not documents:** documents × chunks per document × vectors per chunk, plus\n  growth.\n- **Add the terms per vector:** dim × bytes per dimension, about 2 × M × 4 bytes of HNSW links, and payload.\n- **Multiply** by 1.5 for build headroom and by the replica count, then check the QPS bound on node count."},{"q":"A cluster runs fine under load but crashes during nightly compaction. Why?","a":"- **Merges and rebuilds hold two copies of a segment** while building the new one, so peak memory exceeds\n  steady state.\n- **The estimate likely omitted headroom or graph links.** Check steady-state RAM against node memory; above\n  about two thirds leaves no room for a 1.5× build peak.\n- **Fix:** quantize, move payload out, or add memory, then re-test compaction under load."},{"q":"When is quantization not worth it?","a":"- **When the collection already fits unquantized with headroom:** it adds configuration and a recall check\n  for no saving that matters.\n- **When recall loss on your model is large:** binary codes in particular need heavy oversampling and\n  rescoring.\n- **Otherwise** int8 is a 4× RAM cut that rescoring mostly makes invisible."},{"q":"Estimate memory for 200,000 documents of 4,000 tokens, 400-token chunks with 50 overlap, 1,024 dimensions.","a":"- **Vectors:** ceil(4,000 / 350) = 12 chunks per document, so 2.4 million vectors.\n- **Float32 with text in the engine:** about 9.8 GB vectors, 0.3 GB links, 4.8 GB payload; about 22 GB per\n  replica with headroom.\n- **Int8 and ids only:** about 3.3 GB, roughly 5 GB with headroom per replica, a 4.6× reduction."},{"q":"Vectors are already binary-quantized and the index is still too big. What next?","a":"- **Look at which term now dominates:** binary vectors at 1,024 dimensions are 128 bytes, equal to the HNSW\n  links at `M = 16`, so ids, filter fields and the graph are the bulk.\n- **Levers:** a smaller `M` (fewer links, lower recall), IVF-style or disk-based indexes whose overhead is not\n  per-link, or moving payload out of the engine."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quantization vs recall.** It cuts RAM by 4× (int8) to 32× (binary) and speeds traversal, but costs recall that must be measured per model; for a collection that fits unquantized with headroom, it is complexity for nothing.","**Text in the engine vs memory.** Retrieval becomes a single call and the stack is simpler, but text often outweighs quantized vectors; move it out when memory, snapshot time or rebalance time are the constraint.","**Larger chunks vs precision.** Fewer vectors and a smaller index, but retrieval precision usually falls as chunks grow; size for the chunking the retrieval evals chose, not the other way round.","**Lower dimension vs quality.** A smaller model or a Matryoshka prefix shrinks memory linearly, at a quality cost that is small for some models and tasks and large for others; a measured decision, not a default.","**More replicas vs cost.** Availability and read throughput at linear cost; a single replica with a fast rebuild from the chunk table is acceptable for internal tools and wrong for a customer-facing SLA."],"pitfalls":["**Counting documents instead of chunks.** Symptom: a cluster sized for 200,000 vectors receives 2.4 million and fails to load the index.","**Forgetting graph links and merge headroom.** Symptom: out-of-memory kills during background compaction or index rebuild rather than under query load.","**Ignoring payload size.** Symptom: RAM dominated by text with an index that is a fraction of it, and slow snapshots and rebalances.","**Sizing throughput on average QPS.** Symptom: p99 latency collapses during the daily peak while dashboards show healthy averages.","**Assuming a spill to disk degrades gracefully.** Symptom: latency rises by orders of magnitude once the graph no longer fits, because traversal is random access.","**Not budgeting for the migration window.** Symptom: a model upgrade blocked because the cluster cannot hold both embedding versions at once."],"when":"- **Do the arithmetic** whenever a vector store is chosen, a tier is upgraded, a chunking or embedding change\n  is proposed, or a tenant is onboarded with a known corpus.\n- **Measure** the real chunks-per-document distribution from a sample rather than assuming the mean, recall\n  before and after any quantization, and QPS under production-shaped filters.\n- **What the number tells you:** whether pgvector in the existing database suffices, whether quantization is\n  worth its recall cost, and what the managed-service bill will be at the chosen precision.\n- **Interviews:** \"how big a vector store does this corpus need?\" Walk the eight steps with explicit\n  assumptions, then name the two levers that change the answer most: quantization and payload placement.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes vectors, bytes and overhead from the pipeline's real chunk statistics, chooses quantization and payload placement, and verifies recall and peak QPS on a staging cluster.","architect":"Owns the capacity model and the cost line for retrieval, decides between an extension and a dedicated engine on the basis of the estimate, and budgets memory for re-embedding migrations.","fde":"Sizes a customer deployment from their document count and page statistics during scoping, and explains why the quoted tier changes when they add a second content source or a larger embedding model."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to index 1.5 million support tickets averaging 300 tokens with a 1,536-dimensional\nfloat32 embedding, one chunk per ticket, on a managed service, and they want the full ticket text\nreturned with each hit. Estimate the memory, then show how you would cut it by more than half without\nchanging the embedding model or the retrieval results they see.","a":"One chunk per ticket gives 1.5 million vectors. Float32 at 1,536 dimensions is 6,144 bytes per\nvector, so about 9.2 GB of vectors; HNSW links at `M = 16` add about 128 bytes each, roughly 0.2 GB; ticket\ntext at 300 tokens is about 1.2 KB plus metadata, roughly 2 GB. Steady state is about 11.4 GB, around\n17 GB with 1.5× headroom per replica, and double that for two replicas. To cut it: store the vectors with\nscalar int8 quantization, bringing vectors to about 2.3 GB while keeping float32 copies on disk for\nrescoring so returned results are effectively unchanged, and move ticket text out of the engine into the\nticket database keyed by id, fetched after retrieval. The in-memory total drops to roughly 2.3 + 0.2 +\nsmall metadata, under 3 GB before headroom, which is a quarter of the original. Then measure recall on a\nlabelled query set to confirm quantization did not change the top-k, and load-test at peak QPS."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which omission most often causes a vector store to be under-provisioned by an order of magnitude?","options":["Forgetting that cosine similarity needs normalized vectors","Counting documents rather than chunks when estimating the number of vectors","Using float16 instead of float32 in the estimate","Ignoring the number of HNSW layers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A document with a few thousand tokens becomes ten or more chunks, each with its own vector. Estimates that start from document count miss this multiplier entirely; precision and graph layers change the total by much smaller factors."},{"q":"After enabling int8 scalar quantization, why does disk usage not fall (it even grows slightly) while vector RAM drops roughly 4×?","options":["The engine compresses the payload instead of the vectors","Quantization only applies to the HNSW links","The quantized copy replaces float32 in memory, but full-precision vectors are kept on disk for rescoring the final candidates","Disk usage is always dominated by the write-ahead log"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quantization trades precision during graph traversal for memory, and engines recover accuracy by re-ranking the shortlist against the original vectors. Those originals must still exist, so disk holds both copies while RAM holds only the small one."},{"q":"Why should the capacity estimate include a headroom factor beyond steady-state bytes?","options":["Because object storage charges per request","Because embedding dimensions grow over time","Because segment merges and index rebuilds temporarily hold additional copies, and a cluster at the edge of RAM fails during those operations","Because replicas must each hold the full write-ahead log"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Background maintenance builds a new segment or graph before releasing the old one. Without free memory for that overlap, compaction and re-indexing trigger out-of-memory failures even though steady-state queries fit."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/embedding-versioning-re-indexing-strategy","sources":["Kusupati et al., 2022 — Matryoshka Representation Learning","Official docs: pgvector","Official docs: Qdrant"],"why":"- **The index silently depends on three things.** Which model turned text into vectors; how it was called\n  (dimension, normalization, instruction prefix, input truncation); and how documents were cut into chunks.\n  None of those appear in the vector itself.\n- **Mixing models fails silently.** A query embedded with model B against an index built with model A is\n  not \"slightly worse\". If dimensions differ, the store at least rejects the query. If they happen to\n  match, cosine similarity across spaces is noise, so retrieval degrades to near-random while the pipeline\n  reports no error at all.\n- **Change is forced on you.** Embedding models improve and vendors deprecate old ones; you will want\n  longer context, a multilingual or a cheaper model; you will tune the chunker; you may quantize vectors to\n  save memory. Each of those invalidates every stored vector.\n- **\"Just re-embed in place\" fails three ways.** During the hours or days the job runs, live queries hit a\n  mix of two spaces. If the new model is worse on your eval set, there is nothing to roll back to. And it\n  pays full cost to re-embed unchanged content, when usually only a small fraction changed since the last\n  run.\n- **What versioning buys.** Versioning the index and swapping it as a unit removes all three problems.","how":"### What makes up an embedding version\nTreat the index as the product of a recipe and give the recipe a version identifier:\n\n```\nembedding_version = hash(model_id, model_revision, dimension, normalization,\n                         instruction_prefix, max_input_tokens, chunker_id, chunker_params)\n```\n\n- **Store it on every vector row and on the collection,** *because* the only reliable compatibility check\n  is to compute the query-side recipe and compare. The query path reads the collection's version and\n  refuses, or routes elsewhere, on mismatch.\n- **Pin the model *revision*, not just its name.** Hosted models can be silently updated, and open-weight\n  models on a hub have commit hashes.\n- **Chunker parameters belong in the recipe,** because retrieval granularity changes what a vector means. A\n  512-token chunk and a 128-token chunk of the same paragraph are different items even under the same\n  model, and the citation offsets you return to users change with them.\n\n### Re-embedding versus re-indexing\nTwo operations hide behind the word \"re-index\", and they cost very different amounts.\n\n| Operation | Trigger | What is rebuilt | Typical duration |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| *Re-embedding* | the recipe changed | new vectors, then the ANN structure | days of API throughput |\n| *Re-indexing* | index parameters, data drift, churn | only the ANN structure over existing vectors | hours of index build |\n\nRe-indexing is needed even when the model never changes:\n- **Parameter changes.** HNSW's `M` and `ef_construction` are fixed when the graph is built, so raising them\n  to buy recall means a rebuild. `ef_search` is a query-time knob and needs no rebuild, which is why it is\n  the first thing to tune.\n- **Centroid staleness.** IVF-style indexes assign each vector to the nearest of a set of centroids learned\n  from the data present at build time. pgvector's documentation advises creating an IVFFlat index only after\n  the table holds data for this reason. As the corpus grows into topics the original sample did not cover,\n  lists become unbalanced and recall at a fixed `nprobe` falls, so the centroids must be retrained.\n- **Accumulated deletes and updates.** Many implementations mark deleted vectors rather than removing them,\n  so a heavily churned index carries dead entries that cost memory and can weaken graph connectivity until\n  compaction or rebuild.\n- **Same swap, no embedding calls.** Re-indexing uses the same blue-green swap but skips the embedding\n  calls. Keep two version counters (`embedding_version` for the vectors, an index build id for the\n  structure) so you can tell which one moved when recall changes.\n\n### The query side is part of the version\n- **The alias resolves to a pair:** the collection and the recipe that built it.\n- **The query path embeds with exactly that recipe** (model, prefix, dimension), *because* swapping the\n  collection while the query embedder stays on the old model recreates the cross-space mismatch in reverse.\n- **Key query-embedding caches on `(query_text, embedding_version)`,** never on text alone.\n- **Adapters are a temporary bridge only.** A learned mapping from the new model's space into the old one\n  (a linear or small adapter trained on pairs of embeddings of the same texts) lets a new query model run\n  against the old index before the rebuild finishes. It recovers only part of the quality, so it must pass\n  the same eval gate and is not an alternative to rebuilding.\n\n### Content hashing to avoid re-embedding the unchanged\n1. Give each chunk a stable key: `chunk_key = hash(source_id, chunk_text)`.\n2. Keep a table mapping `(chunk_key, embedding_version) -> vector`.\n3. On each ingestion run, chunk the source, compute keys and skip any key already embedded under the\n   current version.\n4. Chunks whose text changed get new keys and are embedded; keys that disappear are deleted.\n\n- **Why it is safe:** embedding is, for practical purposes, a pure function of (text, recipe). The same\n  inputs yield the same vector up to small floating-point noise from batching and hardware.\n- **Version changes are full misses:** every key misses and you pay for a full rebuild, which is correct.\n\n### Blue-green indexes and the alias swap\n1. **Build beside.** Build the new version into a separate collection or table (`docs_v7` beside\n   `docs_v6`) while the old one keeps serving.\n2. **Resolve through a pointer.** Most stores support an alias the application resolves at query time:\n   Qdrant collection aliases, OpenSearch index aliases; in pgvector a view or a config row naming the live\n   table.\n3. **Repoint in one operation** when the build finishes and passes evaluation. Queries never see a mixed\n   index.\n4. **Roll back** by repointing the alias back; delete the old collection after a soak period.\n\n- **The price:** temporarily holding two copies of vectors and two ANN graphs, which must be planned into\n  memory and disk sizing.\n\n### Catching up on writes during the build\nDocuments keep changing while the rebuild runs.\n\n| | Dual-write | Replay |\n|---|---|---|\n| How | ingestion embeds every new chunk under both versions until the swap | the build records a start watermark, then re-processes everything changed since |\n| Embedding cost | each changed chunk embedded once per version | same, slightly cheaper when a document changes several times in the window, because only its final state is embedded |\n| New collection currency | current at every moment | short lag to close just before the swap |\n| Operational risk | backfill and live writes race on the same collection: a backfill must not overwrite a newer live write, and deletes must reach both | needs a reliable change watermark and a final catch-up pass |\n\n### Evaluate before you swap\n- **A new model is not better by assumption.** Run the retrieval eval set (queries with known relevant\n  chunks) against both collections and compare recall@k and MRR.\n- **Also check answers on a sample,** because a model that retrieves different-but-also-relevant chunks can\n  shift answers in ways retrieval metrics miss.\n- **The swap is gated on this comparison,** which is why the build must be separate.\n\n### Dimension and quantization changes\n- **These are new embedding versions too.** Truncating a Matryoshka-trained model to fewer dimensions, or\n  changing storage precision (float32 to int8 or binary), changes the stored representation and the ANN\n  index parameters, even if the model is the same.\n- **Rebuild rather than convert in place, and re-run the eval,** because quantization costs recall that must\n  be measured, not assumed.\n\n### Cost and duration (illustrative arithmetic)\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Corpus | 10 million chunks averaging 400 tokens = 4 billion tokens |\n| Cost at an illustrative few cents per million tokens | on the order of a hundred dollars, rarely the problem |\n| Time at an illustrative rate limit of a million tokens per minute | roughly 67 hours of embedding calls, before HNSW build time |\n\nThat duration is what makes dual-write or replay necessary, and makes content hashing worth having for the\nroutine runs in between.","example":"### Recipe hash, content-keyed cache and alias swap\nA minimal version of the three mechanisms. The vector store is abstracted as a dict of collections so the\nlogic is visible.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\n# \"revision\" is your own pin; hosted APIs may not expose one, so record the date you validated it\nRECIPE = {\"model\": \"text-embedding-3-small\", \"revision\": \"2024-01\", \"dim\": 1536,\n          \"normalize\": True, \"chunker\": \"recursive\", \"chunk_tokens\": 400, \"overlap\": 40}\nEMB_VERSION = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(RECIPE, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef chunk_key(source_id: str, text: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(f\"{source_id}\\x00{text}\".encode()).hexdigest()[:16]\n\ncollections = {}                 # name -> {chunk_key: (vector, meta)}\nalias = {\"docs\": \"docs_v6\"}      # what queries resolve against\ncache = {}                       # (chunk_key, EMB_VERSION) -> vector\n\ndef embed(text):                 # stand-in for the model call\n    return [float(b) for b in hashlib.md5(text.encode()).digest()[:4]]\n\ndef build(target: str, sources: dict[str, list[str]]):\n    col = collections.setdefault(target, {})\n    seen, calls = set(), 0\n    for sid, chunks in sources.items():\n        for text in chunks:\n            k = chunk_key(sid, text); seen.add(k)\n            if (k, EMB_VERSION) not in cache:\n                cache[(k, EMB_VERSION)] = embed(text); calls += 1\n            col[k] = (cache[(k, EMB_VERSION)], {\"source\": sid, \"emb_version\": EMB_VERSION})\n    for k in set(col) - seen:    # chunks that vanished upstream\n        del col[k]\n    return calls\n\ndocs = {\"kb/1\": [\"Refunds take 5 days.\", \"Contact support via chat.\"]}\nprint(\"first build, calls:\", build(\"docs_v7\", docs))          # 2\ndocs[\"kb/1\"][0] = \"Refunds take 3 days.\"\nprint(\"incremental, calls:\", build(\"docs_v7\", docs))          # 1 (only the changed chunk)\n\ndef swap(name, new_target):      # gate this on the eval comparison in real life\n    old, alias[name] = alias.get(name), new_target\n    return old\nprint(\"retired:\", swap(\"docs\", \"docs_v7\"), \"live:\", alias[\"docs\"])\n```\n\nThe eval gate is deliberately a comment: in production `swap` runs only after recall@k on the eval set for\n`docs_v7` is at least as good as for `docs_v6`.\n\n### Worked scenario: full rebuild versus routine run\nUsing the illustrative rate limit above (a million tokens per minute) on the 10-million-chunk corpus.\n\n| Run | Chunks embedded | Tokens | Embedding time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model upgrade (every key misses) | 10,000,000 | 4 billion | ~4,000 min, about 67 h |\n| Nightly run, 1% of chunks changed (illustrative) | 100,000 | 40 million | ~40 min |\n| Nightly run without content hashing | 10,000,000 | 4 billion | ~67 h, longer than a day, so it overruns the next run |\n\n- **Reading it:** content hashing turns the nightly job from impossible into minutes; the full rebuild is\n  rare but long enough that it needs dual-write or replay.\n\n### Production snippet: blue-green in pgvector with a view as the alias\nThe application queries `docs`; the swap is a single statement, and rollback is the same statement naming\nthe old table.\n\n```sql\n-- build v7 beside v6, index it without blocking writes\nCREATE TABLE docs_v7 (LIKE docs_v6 INCLUDING ALL);\n-- ... backfill embeddings into docs_v7, then:\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY docs_v7_hnsw ON docs_v7\n  USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops) WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);\n\n-- after the eval gate passes: atomic repoint\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW docs AS SELECT * FROM docs_v7;\n```","patterns":["**Recipe-stamped collections with a fail-loud query path.** Use from the first production index. Store `embedding_version` on the collection and each row; at query time compare it with the embedder's recipe hash and raise on mismatch instead of searching. Monitor mismatch errors (should be zero) and which version each request was served from.","**Alias-based blue-green swap.** Use for any corpus that takes more than minutes to rebuild. Qdrant collection aliases, OpenSearch's `_aliases` endpoint with remove and add actions in one request, or a Postgres view repointed with `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`. Monitor recall@k on the eval set before and after the swap, plus p95 latency for the first hour.","**Content-hash cache table for incremental runs.** Use once ingestion runs on a schedule. Keep a table keyed on `(chunk_key, embedding_version)` and skip hits. Monitor fraction of chunks re-embedded per run: near zero normally, 100% only on version changes. A sudden jump with no recipe change usually means the chunker or text extraction changed.","**Tune query-time recall before rebuilding.** When recall dips, raise `hnsw.ef_search` (pgvector session setting) or the IVF probe count first, because they need no rebuild. Rebuild with higher `M` or `ef_construction` only if that cannot reach the target within the latency budget.","**Weekly fixed-eval recall tracking between swaps.** Run the same eval set against the live alias on a schedule. A slow decline with no recipe change signals stale centroids or accumulated deletes: schedule a re-index, not a re-embed.","**Retirement window for old versions.** Keep the previous collection for an explicit soak (for example a week, set by your rollback needs), then delete it. Monitor disk and memory headroom, because two indexes coexist during every rebuild."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Recipe change: model, dim, chunker\"] --> V[\"New embedding_version hash\"]\n    V --> B[\"Build docs_v7 beside docs_v6\"]\n    L[\"Live writes\"] -->|\"dual-write or replay\"| B\n    B --> E{\"Eval: recall at k, MRR vs v6\"}\n    E -->|\"worse\"| X[\"Discard v7, keep v6 live\"]\n    E -->|\"at least as good\"| S[\"Repoint alias docs to v7\"]\n    S --> Q[\"Query side embeds with v7 recipe\"]\n    S --> K[\"Soak period\"]\n    K -->|\"regression\"| RB[\"Repoint alias back to v6\"]\n    K -->|\"stable\"| D[\"Delete docs_v6\"]","caption":"A recipe change never touches the live collection: the new one is built beside it, caught up, and only\npromoted if it wins the eval."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you just point ingestion at the new embedding model and overwrite vectors in place?","a":"- **Mixed spaces during the run.** For hours or days the index holds vectors from two unrelated spaces, so\n  live queries get near-random results with no error.\n- **No rollback.** If the new model is worse on your eval set, the old vectors are already gone.\n- **Wasted cost.** It re-embeds unchanged content; building beside, gating on eval and swapping an alias\n  fixes all three."},{"q":"Recall has drifted down 6 points over three months and nobody changed the model. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Rule out a recipe change first:** compare the stored `embedding_version` with the query embedder's, and\n  check whether a vendor silently updated an unpinned model.\n- **Then suspect the index structure:** stale IVF centroids as the corpus grew into new topics, or\n  accumulated deletes in a churned HNSW graph.\n- **Try the cheap knob, then re-index:** raise `ef_search` or probes; if that recovers recall within the\n  latency budget, schedule a rebuild of the structure, with no re-embedding."},{"q":"Dual-write or watermark replay for a five-day rebuild: which do you pick and why?","a":"- **Replay** if the change watermark is trustworthy: ingestion stays untouched, and documents edited several\n  times are embedded once in their final state.\n- **Dual-write** if the new collection must be current at every moment, accepting that backfill must not\n  overwrite newer live writes and deletes must reach both versions.\n- **Either way** close the gap before the alias swap, never after."},{"q":"How do you estimate how long a re-embed will take, and what dominates?","a":"- **Tokens divided by throughput.** 10 million chunks at 400 tokens is 4 billion tokens; at an illustrative\n  million tokens per minute that is roughly 67 hours.\n- **Throughput, not price, is the constraint:** at a few cents per million tokens the bill is around a\n  hundred dollars. Add HNSW build time on top."},{"q":"What has to change on the query side when you swap the index?","a":"- **The query embedder:** the alias must resolve to collection plus recipe, so queries use the same model,\n  prefix and dimension.\n- **Caches:** query-embedding caches keyed on text alone serve old-space vectors for popular queries; key\n  them on `(query_text, embedding_version)`."}],"tradeoffs":["**Atomic cutover vs double memory.** Blue-green rebuilds give atomic cutover and instant rollback but double vector and index memory during the build. If the corpus barely fits one GPU or one node, you need a staged or tiered rebuild instead.","**Cheap routine runs vs edit blast radius.** Content hashing makes routine runs cheap, but a hash on exact text means a one-character edit re-embeds the chunk and its overlapping neighbours. With fixed-size chunking an insertion shifts every later boundary in that document, so all following chunks re-embed too. Structure-aware chunking that cuts on headings or paragraphs keeps the blast radius local.","**Pinned revision vs owning upgrades.** Pinning a model revision protects consistency but means you consciously own upgrades. Letting a vendor update silently is cheaper to operate right up until retrieval quality shifts with no deploy to blame.","**Dual-write vs replay.** Dual-write keeps the new index current with no catch-up step but couples live ingestion to the new collection and makes it race the backfill. Replay keeps ingestion untouched at the cost of a catch-up pass, and tends to win when builds take days and the change watermark is trustworthy.","**Rollback safety vs storage.** Keeping old versions for rollback is safe, but each held version is storage and an ANN graph; set an explicit retirement window rather than letting versions accumulate."],"pitfalls":["**Upgrading the query-side model without rebuilding the index.** Symptom: plausible-looking but irrelevant chunks with no errors, and eval recall that collapses overnight. Fix: the query path reads the recipe from the alias target.","**Changing the chunker and reusing the old vectors** because \"the model is the same\". Symptom: citations pointing at wrong offsets and duplicated or truncated passages in context. Fix: chunker params are in the recipe hash.","**Re-embedding in place against the live collection.** Symptom: a window of mixed-space results and user complaints that correlate with the ingestion job's schedule. Fix: build beside and swap.","**Swapping on build completion rather than on eval results.** Symptom: a regression discovered by users, with the old index already deleted. Fix: gate the swap on the eval and keep a soak window.","**Forgetting that filters and metadata live beside vectors.** A rebuild that drops ACL or tenant fields produces an index that passes retrieval eval and fails a security review. Fix: diff schemas and test a filtered query before the swap.","**Sizing memory for one copy of the index.** Symptom: out-of-memory or evictions precisely during the rebuild, the moment you can least afford it. Fix: plan for two.","**Caching query embeddings keyed on query text alone.** Symptom after a swap: popular (cached) queries retrieve garbage while rare, uncached queries work fine. Fix: key on `(query_text, embedding_version)`.","**Treating every recall drop as a model problem.** When recall decays slowly with no recipe change, the cause is usually stale IVF centroids or accumulated deletes, and a re-index (no re-embedding) fixes it."],"when":"- **Version from the first production index,** because the first model upgrade arrives sooner than planned\n  and the retrofit is painful.\n- **Use the alias swap** for any corpus that takes longer than a few minutes to rebuild; below that, a\n  rebuild-and-replace in a transaction is fine.\n- **Add content hashing** as soon as ingestion runs on a schedule rather than once; it pays for itself.\n- **Measure four things:** fraction of chunks re-embedded per run (small except on version changes); build\n  duration versus the rate of upstream change (decides dual-write versus replay); eval recall before and\n  after every swap; and recall on a fixed eval set tracked weekly between swaps, since a slow decline with\n  no recipe change is the signal to re-index rather than re-embed.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer to \"how do you upgrade the embedding model in production\" is this\n  exact sequence: version the recipe, build beside, evaluate, swap the alias, retire later.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Stamps every vector with the recipe hash, writes the content-keyed embedding cache, and wires the alias lookup into the query path so mismatches fail loudly.","architect":"Owns the capacity plan that includes two live indexes during rebuilds and the policy for how many versions are retained and for how long.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a \"quick model upgrade\" is a multi-day rebuild with an eval gate, and designs the replay window around their document change rate."},"check":{"q":"Your team wants to move a 20-million-chunk index from a 1536-dimension model to a newer\n1024-dimension model. Walk through the plan and the two biggest risks.","a":"First, define the new recipe (model id, pinned revision, 1024 dims, normalization, same chunker if\nchunking is unchanged) and hash it as a new embedding version. Build it into a new collection while the\nold serves; estimate duration from token count divided by the embedding throughput limit, and because\nthat is likely days, either dual-write new chunks under both versions or record a watermark and replay\nchanges afterward. Run the retrieval eval set against both collections and compare recall@k and MRR,\nplus a sample of end-to-end answers. Swap the alias only if the new one is at least as good, keep the old\ncollection for a rollback window, then delete it. The two biggest risks are memory: both collections and\nboth ANN graphs coexist, so the cluster must have headroom; and silent quality regression: the new model\nmay retrieve differently on your domain, which only the eval gate catches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't you query an index built with embedding model A using vectors from model B?","options":["Model B produces vectors that are too large to compare efficiently","The two models define unrelated vector spaces, so similarity between their vectors is meaningless","Model B normalizes vectors differently, which the index can correct for at query time","The ANN index stores the model name and rejects the query"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each model learns its own geometry; the same text lands at unrelated points. Cosine similarity across spaces is noise, which is why retrieval degrades to near-random, with no error when the dimensions happen to match. Indexes check dimension at most, never which model produced a vector, unless you store and check the version yourself."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of keying the embedding cache on both chunk content hash and embedding version?","options":["To allow the same vector to be reused across different embedding models","To detect duplicate documents across sources","To skip re-embedding unchanged chunks on routine runs while forcing a full rebuild when the recipe changes","To make vector lookups faster at query time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding is a pure function of (text, recipe). Keying on both means an unchanged chunk under the same recipe is a cache hit, and any change to model, dimension or chunker makes every key miss, which is the correct behaviour."},{"q":"During a multi-day rebuild, documents keep changing. Which approach keeps the new index current without a mixed-space window?","options":["Pause ingestion until the rebuild finishes","Write new chunks only to the old index and copy them over after the swap","Dual-write new chunks under both versions, or record a watermark and replay changes after the build","Embed new chunks with whichever model is faster and tag them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dual-write or watermark-and-replay both deliver a complete new collection at swap time. Pausing ingestion leaves users with stale data for days; copying after the swap creates a gap; mixing models in one collection is exactly the failure versioning exists to prevent."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/knowledge-management","sources":[],"why":"- **Similarity is not correctness.** Point a crawler at a wiki, a ticketing system and a shared drive, and\n  \"what is the refund window\" matches a 2019 policy page, a 2023 revision, a never-approved draft, a Slack\n  export of someone guessing, and three copies of one PDF with different file names. The retriever ranks by\n  similarity, which has nothing to do with correctness or currency, so the model's blended answer is\n  confidently wrong.\n- **Better models do not fix bad candidates.** The embedding model, the reranker and the prompt all operate\n  on candidates, and the candidates are the problem.\n- **\"Index more, the model will sort it out\" fails.** The model has no signal that one chunk is\n  authoritative and another deprecated unless the data carries it. Larger context windows make it worse:\n  more contradictory passages fit, and the model's choice between them is arbitrary.\n- **A one-time cleanup fails too.** Corpora drift continuously: pages are edited, products renamed, people\n  leave and their documents go unmaintained. Without ownership and lifecycle rules, the cleaned corpus\n  decays back within months.\n- **What it buys.** A standing process with owners, metadata and feedback loops instead of a one-off\n  cleanup.","how":"### Decide what is canonical\n- **Name a system of record per class of question.** Pricing comes from the catalogue, not from sales decks\n  that quote it. Policy comes from the policy repository, not from support macros that paraphrase it.\n- **Why:** retrieval needs a tiebreaker between similar passages, and \"which source is authoritative for\n  this topic\" is the one humans use.\n- **Encode it** as a `source_rank` or `authority` field on every chunk, so the retriever or reranker can\n  prefer canonical sources and the generator can cite them.\n- **Secondaries still help recall,** but they should lose to primaries on conflict.\n\n### Assign ownership and freshness expectations\n- **Every indexed source gets an owner** (a team, not a person who may leave) and a review cadence. The\n  owner confirms the content is still correct or retires it.\n- **Record `last_verified_at` separately from `last_modified_at`.** A document untouched for two years may\n  be perfectly current; one edited yesterday may be a typo fix on an obsolete page.\n- **Score freshness on verification, not modification,** where the distinction exists.\n\n### Give documents a lifecycle\n| State | Index behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| draft | not indexed by default |\n| published | indexed |\n| deprecated | leaves the index, or carries a flag the prompt surfaces (\"this page is marked superseded by X\") |\n| archived | removed |\n\n- **The lifecycle lives in the source system** where possible (a wiki label, a status field) and is read by\n  ingestion, *because* content owners work in the source tool, not in the vector database, and a state they\n  cannot see will not be maintained.\n\n### Deduplicate and canonicalize\n- **Why:** near-duplicates waste context slots and make the retriever look confident when it has really\n  found the same passage three times.\n- **Exact duplicates** are caught by content hashing at ingestion.\n- **Near-duplicates** (the same PDF exported twice with different headers) need a similarity threshold or a\n  MinHash pass, plus a rule for which copy survives, usually the one in the canonical source.\n- **Keep the mapping,** so retrieval hits on a discarded copy can be redirected to the surviving one.\n\n### Decide what belongs in the index at all\n- **An index is a snapshot** refreshed on the ingestion cadence. Any fact that changes faster than that, or\n  must be exact for this user right now (a price, stock level, account balance, ticket status), is stale by\n  construction once indexed.\n- **Look those up at query time** from the system of record through a tool or API call. The index holds the\n  stable prose that explains them (how pricing tiers work, what a status means).\n- **Rule of thumb: index explanations, look up values.** *Because* a retrieved chunk carrying last week's\n  price is indistinguishable to the model from a correct one, while a live lookup either returns the current\n  value or fails visibly.\n\n### Keep a controlled vocabulary\n- **The problem:** products get renamed, teams reorganise, and three departments call one thing three\n  names. With free-text tags, a filter on `product = \"Atlas Pro\"` misses every page still tagged with last\n  year's name.\n- **The fix:** a small glossary mapping aliases and former names to one canonical term, applied to tags at\n  ingestion and used for query expansion at retrieval time.\n- **Why it is worth it:** it is cheap, owned like any other source, and closes a class of misses no\n  embedding model reliably closes, because a new product name often has no semantic relationship to the old\n  one.\n\n### Carry metadata that the retriever can act on\n- **Minimum set:** source system, owner, authority rank, lifecycle state, verified date, audience or access\n  scope, product or domain tags, and language.\n- **Why:** metadata filters and rerankers use these to turn \"similar\" into \"relevant to this user now\".\n- **Reject, do not default.** Metadata not populated at ingestion is never populated, so the ingestion\n  contract should reject documents missing required fields.\n\n### Close the loop from failures to content\n1. **Collect signals:** user thumbs-downs and missed eval queries are the best signal about the corpus.\n2. **Classify the cause:** missing document, stale document, duplicate crowding out the right one, or a\n   genuine retrieval failure.\n3. **Route the first three to the content owner** with the query attached. Cheap once ownership exists;\n   without it, every user who hits the gap rediscovers it.\n\n- **Detect equal-authority conflicts explicitly.** When top candidates share an authority rank but\n  contradict each other on a value, show the user both with their dates and open a ticket for the owners.\n  Do not let the model pick silently, because the silent pick is right only by chance and nobody learns the\n  conflict exists.\n\n### Access and tenancy are part of knowledge, not an afterthought\n- **Permissions are knowledge about who may see a document.** Ingestion must capture ACLs from the source and\n  retrieval must filter on them.\n- **A correct corpus that leaks HR pages to engineering is a failed deployment.** Treat access scope as a\n  required metadata field like any other.","example":"### Lifecycle gate, ACL filter, dedup and authority-aware ranking\nRaw similarity would put the deprecated 14-day page first. The gates leave only the canonical answer.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom datetime import date\n\nchunks = [\n    {\"text\": \"Refund window is 30 days.\", \"source\": \"docs\", \"authority\": 1, \"state\": \"published\",\n     \"verified\": date(2025, 1, 10), \"acl\": {\"all\"}, \"sim\": 0.81},\n    {\"text\": \"Refund window is 14 days.\", \"source\": \"confluence\", \"authority\": 2, \"state\": \"deprecated\",\n     \"verified\": date(2021, 3, 2), \"acl\": {\"all\"}, \"sim\": 0.86},\n    {\"text\": \"Refund window is 30 days.\", \"source\": \"drive\", \"authority\": 2, \"state\": \"published\",\n     \"verified\": date(2024, 6, 1), \"acl\": {\"all\"}, \"sim\": 0.81},\n    {\"text\": \"Refunds over $500 need HR sign-off.\", \"source\": \"hr\", \"authority\": 1, \"state\": \"published\",\n     \"verified\": date(2025, 2, 1), \"acl\": {\"hr\"}, \"sim\": 0.70},\n    {\"text\": \"DRAFT: refund window may move to 60 days.\", \"source\": \"confluence\", \"authority\": 2,\n     \"state\": \"draft\", \"verified\": None, \"acl\": {\"all\"}, \"sim\": 0.84},\n]\n\ndef candidates(chunks, user_groups, today=date(2025, 3, 1)):\n    seen, out = set(), []\n    for c in chunks:\n        if c[\"state\"] != \"published\" or not (c[\"acl\"] & user_groups):\n            continue                                   # lifecycle gate and ACL filter\n        h = hashlib.sha256(c[\"text\"].encode()).hexdigest()\n        if h in seen:\n            continue                                   # exact duplicate: first (canonical) copy wins\n        seen.add(h)\n        age_days = (today - c[\"verified\"]).days        # verification, not modification\n        score = c[\"sim\"] - 0.05 * (c[\"authority\"] - 1) - 0.0001 * age_days\n        out.append((round(score, 3), c[\"source\"], c[\"text\"]))\n    return sorted(out, reverse=True)\n\nordered = sorted(chunks, key=lambda c: c[\"authority\"])  # canonical sources first for dedup\nfor row in candidates(ordered, {\"all\"}):\n    print(row)        # (0.805, 'docs', 'Refund window is 30 days.')\n```\n\nThe weights (0.05 per authority step, a small age penalty) are illustrative; in production they are\nreranker features tuned on the eval set.\n\n### Worked scenario: an internal support assistant at 58 percent\nIllustrative numbers. Three sources: the product documentation site (about 4,000 pages), the support\nteam's Confluence space (about 9,000 pages accumulated over six years) and a Google Drive folder of PDFs\n(about 2,500 files). Everything is chunked and embedded; the eval set of 300 support questions scores 58\npercent \"answer correct\" as judged by the support leads.\n\n**Failure analysis of the 126 wrong answers:**\n\n| Cause | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Cites a Confluence page superseded by a newer one still in the index | 51 |\n| Blends numbers from a PDF export of an old price list with the current docs | 23 |\n| Near-identical chunks from three copies of one onboarding guide push the relevant page out of the top five | 19 |\n| Answers from an unapproved draft | 14 |\n| Genuine retrieval or generation problems | 19 |\n\n**Fixes, with no model change:**\n1. The documentation site is canonical for product behaviour and the pricing catalogue for prices, both\n   `authority=1`; Confluence gets `authority=2`.\n2. Confluence pages get a lifecycle label. Support leads spend two afternoons marking 3,100 pages deprecated\n   and 800 archived, which ingestion now respects.\n3. Content hashing plus a near-duplicate pass removes about 1,900 chunks.\n4. Price-list PDFs are excluded entirely, because the catalogue API is now ingested directly.\n5. The reranker gets `authority` and `last_verified_at` as features.\n\n**Result:**\n\n| | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Correct | 174 of 300 (58 percent) | 243 of 300 (81 percent) |\n| Content-caused failures | 107 | 38 (69 recovered) |\n| Genuine retrieval or generation failures | 19 | 19, now a clean, isolated bucket |\n\n- **The 38 content leftovers:** pages superseded but not yet labelled, near-duplicates below the dedup\n  threshold, and questions whose answers exist nowhere in writing, logged as content requests to the docs\n  team. Together with the 19 they make the 57 remaining failures.\n- **The standing process:** a weekly report, owned by the support leads, lists deprecated pages still\n  receiving retrieval hits and unanswered question clusters.","patterns":["**Ingestion contract that rejects incomplete documents.** Use for every connector. Validate each document against a schema (for example a Pydantic model with required `owner`, `authority`, `lifecycle_state`, `acl` and `last_verified_at` fields) and send failures to a rejection queue tagged with the source owner. Monitor rejection rate per source; a spike means a connector or source schema changed.","**Lifecycle read from the source tool.** Use when owners already work in a wiki or CMS. Map a source label or status field (for example a Confluence page label such as \"deprecated\") to `lifecycle_state` at every sync, so retiring a page needs no AI-team action. Monitor deprecated pages still receiving retrieval hits.","**Near-duplicate pass with MinHash LSH.** Use when the same content is exported or copied across systems. Shingle chunks, index them in an LSH structure (the `datasketch` library's `MinHashLSH` is one common choice), keep the canonical-source copy and store the redirect mapping. Monitor duplicate ratio in top-k.","**Glossary-backed tag normalization and query expansion.** Use once products or teams have been renamed. A small owned table of alias to canonical term, applied to tags at ingestion and appended to queries. Monitor zero-result filtered queries, which often point at a missing alias.","**Failure triage queue routed to owners.** Use from launch. Every thumbs-down or eval miss gets one cause label (missing, stale, duplicate, access, retrieval, generation) and content causes become tickets in the owner's tracker with the query attached. Monitor the share of failures per cause week over week.","**Live lookups for volatile values.** Use for prices, stock, balances and statuses. Expose a tool that calls the system of record and keep only explanatory prose in the index. Monitor answers citing indexed values that also exist in a live API."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Source systems: docs, wiki, drive\"] --> C{\"Required metadata present?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| R[\"Reject, notify owner\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| L{\"Lifecycle state\"}\n    L -->|\"draft or archived\"| X[\"Not indexed\"]\n    L -->|\"published or deprecated\"| D[\"Dedup and glossary tags\"]\n    D --> I[(\"Index with authority, ACL, verified date\")]\n    I --> Q[\"Retrieve: ACL filter, rerank by authority\"]\n    Q --> A[\"Answer with citations\"]\n    A -->|\"thumbs-down or eval miss\"| T[\"Triage cause\"]\n    T -->|\"missing, stale, duplicate\"| O[\"Content owner backlog\"]\n    O --> S","caption":"Each document passes the same gates on the way in, and failures flow back to the owner rather than into\nprompt tweaks."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"canonical source\" mean in a RAG corpus, and why does retrieval need it?","a":"- **Definition:** the named system of record for a class of question, such as the pricing catalogue for\n  prices or the policy repository for policy.\n- **Why:** similarity cannot tell a current policy from a superseded paraphrase, so an `authority` field on\n  every chunk gives the reranker a tiebreaker and the generator something trustworthy to cite."},{"q":"Users say the assistant quotes a refund window that changed last quarter. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Trace the citation:** find which chunk was used, its source, lifecycle state and `last_verified_at`.\n- **Likely causes:** the old page is still published in the source, its deprecation label is not read by\n  ingestion, or a copy lives in another system with no dedup mapping.\n- **Fix the class, not the instance:** read lifecycle from the source, dedup to the canonical copy, and route\n  the case to the page owner."},{"q":"When would you index drafts or secondary sources despite the risk?","a":"- **Thin canonical sources:** if primaries do not cover a topic, secondaries should win on recall and lose\n  only on conflict.\n- **Draft-heavy teams:** engineering groups that live in drafts may need them indexed with a visible flag\n  the prompt surfaces, rather than hidden."},{"q":"Which facts should not be embedded at all, and how do you serve them?","a":"- **Volatile or per-user values:** prices, stock levels, account balances, ticket status, because an index\n  snapshot is stale by construction.\n- **Serve them by live lookup** through a tool calling the system of record, and keep only the explanatory\n  prose in the index; a failed lookup fails visibly, a stale chunk does not."},{"q":"How would you show a customer that content, not the model, is the bottleneck?","a":"- **Classify a sample of failures** by cause and present the table with cited pages, as in the support\n  assistant case: 107 of 126 failures were content-caused.\n- **Fix the largest buckets and re-run the eval:** in that case 58 to 81 percent with no model change, which\n  also isolates the 19 genuine retrieval or generation errors for later work."}],"tradeoffs":["**Correctness on conflicts vs secondary context.** Declaring canonical sources improves correctness on conflicts but suppresses useful secondary context. When the canonical source is thin, let secondaries win on recall and lose only on conflict.","**Clean corpus vs hidden work in progress.** Strict lifecycle gating (only published content indexed) keeps the corpus clean but hides drafts. For engineering teams that live in drafts, indexing drafts with a visible flag can be better.","**Sharper ranking vs merged versions.** Aggressive deduplication frees context slots and sharpens ranking, but a threshold set too low merges legitimately different versions; the choice depends on how much the corpus versions in place.","**Trustworthy corpus vs owner time.** Ownership and review cadences make the corpus trustworthy but cost owner time every cycle. If nobody will do the reviews, a shorter freshness-based demotion is more honest than a fake cadence.","**Better filtering vs rejections.** Rich required metadata improves filtering, but every required field is a reason for a document to be rejected at ingestion. Keep the required set small and the optional set large."],"pitfalls":["**Treating a bad answer as a model problem and iterating on prompts.** Symptom: eval scores that move a point or two per week while the same stale pages keep getting cited. Fix: classify failures by cause first.","**Indexing everything a connector can reach because recall seems free.** Symptom: top-k results dominated by duplicates and drafts, and a context window full of contradictory passages. Fix: lifecycle gate and dedup.","**Using modification date as freshness.** Symptom: a correct, stable policy page demoted below a recently edited but obsolete one. Fix: rank on `last_verified_at`.","**Storing lifecycle state only in the vector database.** Symptom: owners mark pages obsolete in the wiki while the index keeps serving them, because nobody re-ran the cleanup. Fix: read state from the source.","**Skipping ACL capture at ingestion to ship faster.** Symptom: a security finding, or a user seeing a page they could never open in the source system. Fix: access scope is a required field.","**No feedback path from users to content owners.** Symptom: the same gap reported many times with nobody positioned to fix it. Fix: a triage queue routed to owners.","**Indexing volatile values such as prices or stock levels instead of looking them up.** Symptom: answers that were right on the day of ingestion and drift wrong until the next run. Fix: index explanations, look up values."],"when":"- **Use for any RAG system over content people maintain,** which is nearly all of them.\n- **Skip only for effectively immutable, single-sourced corpora,** such as a frozen archive of closed case\n  files or a product's own generated logs.\n- **Start before the first index,** since the ingestion schema must carry the metadata.\n- **Measure:** share of eval failures attributed to content versus retrieval versus generation, count of\n  deprecated pages still receiving hits, duplicate ratio in top-k, and median `last_verified_at` of cited\n  chunks. Day to day it shows up as the ingestion contract and the weekly content report.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you improve a RAG system scoring 60 percent\" should start with failure analysis\n  of the corpus, not with a bigger model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds ingestion that reads lifecycle and ACL state from source systems, enforces required metadata, deduplicates, and exposes authority and verification fields to the reranker.","architect":"Decides which systems are canonical for which domains, defines the metadata schema every connector must satisfy, and sets the ownership and review model with the business.","fde":"Runs the failure analysis with the customer's subject-matter experts, negotiates who owns which content, and turns \"the bot is wrong\" into a prioritized content backlog."},"check":{"q":"A customer's RAG assistant is scoring poorly, and they want to switch to a larger model. What do you\ndo first, and how do you argue for it?","a":"Before touching the model, sample the failures and classify each by cause: missing document, stale or\nsuperseded document, duplicates crowding out the right chunk, access-filtered content, or a true retrieval\nor generation error. In enterprise corpora the first three often dominate, and no model fixes them because\nthe model only sees the candidates it is given. Show the customer the breakdown with concrete cited pages:\n\"this answer came from a 2021 page that your own wiki marks obsolete\". Then propose the fixes that address\nthe largest buckets: canonical sources with authority ranks, lifecycle labels read by ingestion,\ndeduplication, and a feedback loop to content owners. Re-run the eval after those changes; if genuine\nretrieval or generation errors remain the largest bucket, that is when a model or retriever change is\njustified, and the eval now isolates its effect."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a larger context window not fix a corpus with contradictory documents?","options":["Larger windows are slower, so fewer documents can be retrieved","More contradictory passages fit, and the model has no signal about which one is authoritative","Embedding models cannot embed documents longer than the window","The reranker discards documents beyond the first few thousand tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The problem is the absence of an authority signal, not capacity. Fitting more passages increases the number of conflicting claims the model must choose between, and without metadata indicating which source is canonical, its choice is arbitrary."},{"q":"Which field is the better basis for freshness in retrieval, and why?","options":["last_modified_at, because recent edits indicate active maintenance","last_verified_at, because an owner confirming correctness is different from any edit having happened","created_at, because older documents are more established","indexed_at, because it reflects when the chunk entered the system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A stable policy page may be unedited for years yet fully current, while an obsolete page may get a typo fix yesterday. Verification captures what freshness actually means; modification only captures activity."},{"q":"Where should a document's lifecycle state (published, deprecated, archived) be maintained?","options":["In the vector database metadata, since that is what retrieval reads","In a spreadsheet maintained by the AI team","In the source system where content owners work, read by ingestion","In the prompt, as a list of deprecated page titles"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Content owners maintain state where they already work. A state stored only in the index or a side spreadsheet drifts immediately because the people who know a page is obsolete never see it. Ingestion propagates the source's state into the index."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-lineage","sources":["Official docs: OpenLineage","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"- **AI systems are long chains of transformations.** Source documents become parsed text, then chunks,\n  then vectors joined with metadata into an index. Separately, raw records become cleaned datasets,\n  training mixes, checkpoints and served models. Each hop is written by a different job, often a different\n  team.\n- **Each hop forgets the one before.** A vector in the index does not know which parser version produced\n  its text; a checkpoint does not know which rows were filtered out of its training set.\n- **The question is always upstream or downstream.** Deletion: which chunks, caches, fine-tuning sets and\n  checkpoints contain this customer's data? Regression on Tuesday: did the source, parser, chunker,\n  embedding model or index build change? Impossible output: was the offending example in the training data,\n  and which pipeline let it through? Each is \"walk backward to inputs\" or \"walk forward to derivatives\".\n- **Reading pipeline code fails,** because code describes what *could* run, not what *did* run with which\n  inputs at which version.\n- **Naming conventions and READMEs fail,** because jobs do not update them and they drift immediately.\n  Lineage must be emitted by the job at run time, as a side effect of doing the work, or it will not be\n  accurate.","how":"### The graph\n- **Two node types:** **datasets** (a table, a file prefix, a vector collection, a model artifact, a prompt\n  template) and **jobs** (a pipeline step).\n- **A run is one execution of a job** and carries edges: run *reads* datasets A and B, run *writes* dataset\n  C.\n- **Dataset versions are nodes, not just names,** *because* \"the training table\" is not an input; \"the\n  training table as of snapshot 2024-06-01T00:00Z, 4.2 million rows, content hash h\" is. Without versioned\n  nodes, the graph says a job touched a table and nothing about which state of the table.\n\n### Granularity\n| Level | Example | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Table-level | job J read table T | the warehouse |\n| Column-level | `price_usd` in C derives from `price_cents` in A through J | schema changes, impact analysis |\n| Record- or chunk-level | each chunk carries source document id, document version, parser version, chunker version | anything feeding retrieval, fine-tuning or a model's outputs |\n\n- **Record-level is heavier,** but it is the only granularity at which \"delete this person's data\" and \"why\n  was this passage retrieved\" can be answered.\n- **Practical compromise:** table-level for the warehouse, record-level for anything that feeds retrieval,\n  fine-tuning or a model's outputs.\n\n### Capture at run time, not by declaration\n1. **Start event:** job name, run id, inputs with their versions.\n2. **Complete event:** outputs with their versions, plus facets such as row counts, schema and the code\n   commit.\n3. **Fail and abort events too.** OpenLineage defines FAIL and ABORT states, *because* a run that wrote half\n   its output before crashing is exactly the one you need to find later.\n\n- **Why run time:** declared lineage (a YAML file describing the DAG) tells you the intended graph, not the\n  actual one for a specific run.\n- **Tooling:** OpenLineage is the common open standard for these events. Orchestrators and transformation\n  tools (Airflow, Spark, dbt among them) can emit them; Marquez is the reference backend that stores and\n  renders the graph; catalogs such as DataHub can ingest the events.\n- **Check formats:** other catalogs (Apache Atlas, for example) use their own lineage model, so check which\n  format your catalog actually consumes.\n- **No tooling needed to start:** a table of `(run_id, job, direction, dataset, version, facets)` rows gives\n  you the same power.\n\n### Facets that make lineage useful\n- **A bare edge is not enough.** Each run records the code version (commit hash, container digest), the\n  parameters (chunk size, embedding model id), the environment, and per-dataset statistics (row count,\n  schema fingerprint, content hash).\n- **Facets turn events into explanations:** \"the index was rebuilt\" becomes \"the index was rebuilt by commit\n  abc with chunk size 512 from document snapshot h\".\n- **Debugging is a facet diff:** when two runs produce different results, diff their facets.\n\n### Traversals\n- **Impact analysis (forward):** from a dataset version, everything derived from it. Used before changing a\n  source schema and for deletion propagation: a document id walks forward to chunks, vectors, caches, eval\n  sets and fine-tuning examples.\n- **Root cause (backward):** from an artifact, its ancestors. Used when quality regresses: compare the\n  ancestor facets of the good run and the bad run.\n- **Reproduction:** the backward closure plus the code versions is the recipe to rebuild an artifact.\n\n### Lineage versus provenance\nThe two words are often used interchangeably; the useful distinction is scope.\n\n| | Lineage | Provenance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scope | the whole derivation graph across the platform | the verifiable record attached to one artifact |\n| Typical query | traversal: \"what depends on this table\" | \"this checkpoint was built from these digests by this commit, and here is a signature\" |\n| Relationship | the graph lets you walk from one manifest to every artifact sharing an input | manifests are nodes and facets in the lineage graph |\n\n### Runtime-derived artifacts are the usual hole\n- **Batch jobs are easy** to instrument because an orchestrator wraps them.\n- **Request-path artifacts escape:** an agent's memory summary of a conversation, a semantic-cache entry, a\n  generated answer saved as a draft, a user-feedback row copied into an eval set.\n- **Same treatment for each:** record the ids of the inputs it was derived from (conversation, retrieved\n  chunk ids, model version) at write time. Otherwise forward traversal from a deleted document stops at the\n  batch boundary and misses the copies users actually see.\n- **Know where traversal stops being actionable.** Forward traversal can show a document reached a training\n  snapshot and therefore a checkpoint, but cannot remove its influence from the weights; it tells you which\n  models must be retrained or retired. Lineage finds the blast radius; retention and deletion architecture\n  decides what to do about it.\n\n### Lineage for RAG specifically\n- **Stamp every chunk** with `source_id`, `source_version`, `parser_version`, `chunker_version`,\n  `embedding_version` and `ingest_run_id`.\n- **Log each retrieved chunk with the request trace,** which connects the serving-time trace to the\n  ingestion-time graph.\n- **That join makes citations trustworthy:** not \"a chunk that looks like page 12\" but \"chunk c, from\n  document d at version v, ingested by run r\".\n\n### Lineage for training\n- **Inputs and outputs:** a training run reads dataset versions and a base model version, and writes\n  checkpoints and eval results.\n- **Keep the exact data snapshot** as an immutable version, retained and addressed by its hash. That lets you\n  answer \"was example x in the training set\" by lookup rather than re-running the filter pipeline. It is\n  also what dataset contamination checks need.\n- **A hash alone is not enough:** it proves which bytes were used but cannot be searched, so the snapshot\n  must be kept for as long as that question can be asked.","example":"### A minimal lineage recorder with forward and backward traversal\nIt writes versioned dataset nodes and run edges to an in-memory store, then answers the two traversals\nthat matter: forward impact from a document and backward root cause from a chunk.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, uuid, time\n\nedges = []          # (run_id, direction, dataset_id)\nruns = {}           # run_id -> facets\nchunk_lineage = {}  # chunk_id -> facets\n\ndef version_of(content: bytes) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(content).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef ingest(doc_id: str, text: str, commit: str, chunker=\"recursive-v3\", emb=\"emb-v7\"):\n    run_id = str(uuid.uuid4())[:8]\n    doc_ver = version_of(text.encode())\n    runs[run_id] = {\"job\": \"ingest\", \"commit\": commit, \"chunker\": chunker,\n                    \"embedding_version\": emb, \"started\": time.time()}\n    edges.append((run_id, \"reads\", f\"doc:{doc_id}@{doc_ver}\"))\n    for i, piece in enumerate(text.split(\". \")):            # stand-in chunker\n        chunk_id = f\"chunk:{doc_id}:{doc_ver}:{i}\"\n        chunk_lineage[chunk_id] = {\"source\": f\"doc:{doc_id}@{doc_ver}\", \"run\": run_id,\n                                   \"chunker\": chunker, \"embedding_version\": emb}\n        edges.append((run_id, \"writes\", chunk_id))\n    return run_id\n\ndef forward(dataset_id: str):\n    \"\"\"Everything derived from a dataset version (deletion propagation / impact).\"\"\"\n    out, frontier = set(), {dataset_id}\n    while frontier:\n        d = frontier.pop()\n        for run_id in {r for r, dir_, ds in edges if dir_ == \"reads\" and ds == d}:\n            for r, dir_, ds in edges:\n                if r == run_id and dir_ == \"writes\" and ds not in out:\n                    out.add(ds); frontier.add(ds)\n    return out\n\ndef backward(chunk_id: str):\n    \"\"\"Why does this chunk look the way it does?\"\"\"\n    lin = chunk_lineage[chunk_id]\n    return {**lin, \"run_facets\": runs[lin[\"run\"]]}\n\nr1 = ingest(\"kb/42\", \"Refunds take 5 days. Chat support is 24/7.\", commit=\"a1b2c3\")\ndoc_node = next(ds for r, d, ds in edges if r == r1 and d == \"reads\")\nprint(sorted(forward(doc_node)))      # the chunks to delete if kb/42 must be erased\nprint(backward(\"chunk:kb/42:%s:0\" % doc_node.split(\"@\")[1]))\n```\n\nIn a real system the same records would be OpenLineage events to a catalog, and the serving trace would\nlog the chunk ids it retrieved so a user-visible answer links back to `backward(...)`.\n\n### Production snippet: forward traversal over a lineage table in SQL\nWith lineage stored as `(run_id, job, direction, dataset, version, facets)` rows, a recursive query finds\neverything derived from one document version. `UNION` (not `UNION ALL`) drops repeats, so the query\nterminates even if the graph has a cycle.\n\n```sql\nWITH RECURSIVE downstream(dataset, version) AS (\n  SELECT 'doc:kb/42', 'h1'                         -- the deleted document version\n  UNION\n  SELECT w.dataset, w.version\n  FROM downstream d\n  JOIN lineage r ON r.direction = 'reads'  AND r.dataset = d.dataset AND r.version = d.version\n  JOIN lineage w ON w.direction = 'writes' AND w.run_id = r.run_id\n)\nSELECT dataset, version FROM downstream;\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the Tuesday regression with no deploy\nRetrieval recall on one product area drops overnight. Backward traversal from a chunk that should have been\nretrieved, comparing the last good run with the bad one (illustrative values):\n\n| Facet | Good run (Monday) | Bad run (Tuesday) | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `source_version` | h | h | upstream content unchanged |\n| code commit | abc | abc | no deploy of our code |\n| `parser_version` | pdf-parser 1.x | pdf-parser 2.x | a dependency upgrade slipped in via an unpinned image |\n| `chunker_version` | recursive-v3 | recursive-v3 | unchanged |\n| output row count | baseline | far fewer chunks for that area | the new parser drops tables |\n\n- **Root cause in minutes:** the parser changed under an unchanged commit, which only a container-digest or\n  parser-version facet reveals. Fix: pin the parser, re-ingest the affected sources, add a row-count check.","patterns":["**Orchestrator-emitted OpenLineage events.** Use when jobs run under Airflow, Spark or dbt. Enable the tool's OpenLineage integration and point its transport at your backend (Marquez or a catalog that ingests OpenLineage). Monitor event volume per job and jobs with START but no COMPLETE, FAIL or ABORT.","**Lineage coverage gate in CI.** Use once more than a few pipelines exist. Fail the build if a new job definition does not emit run events or omits required facets (commit, container digest, input versions). Monitor coverage as fraction of jobs emitting lineage.","**Chunk stamping plus trace join.** Use for every RAG index. Write `source_version`, `parser_version`, `chunker_version`, `embedding_version` and `ingest_run_id` on each chunk, and log retrieved chunk ids as attributes on the request's trace span. Monitor the share of served answers whose citations resolve to a document version.","**Request-path lineage at write time.** Use for agent memories, semantic caches, saved drafts and feedback-to-eval copies. Store input ids (conversation id, chunk ids, model version) in the same row as the derived artifact. Monitor derived rows with empty input-id fields.","**Scheduled deletion drill.** Use where erasure requests are possible. Pick a test document, traverse forward, and assert every derived artifact (chunks, vectors, caches, eval rows, fine-tune rows) is found and purged. Monitor drill pass rate and time to complete."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"doc kb/42 at version h\"] -->|\"read by\"| R1[\"Run: ingest, commit a1b2c3\"]\n    R1 -->|\"writes\"| C[\"Chunks with source, parser, chunker versions\"]\n    C -->|\"read by\"| R2[\"Run: embed and index\"]\n    R2 -->|\"writes\"| V[(\"Vector collection docs_v7\")]\n    C -->|\"read by\"| R3[\"Run: build fine-tune mix\"]\n    R3 -->|\"writes\"| S[\"Training snapshot\"]\n    S --> M[\"Checkpoint\"]\n    V --> T[\"Request trace logs chunk ids\"]\n    T --> A[\"Answer and citation\"]\n    A -.->|\"backward: root cause\"| D","caption":"Runs connect versioned datasets. Forward edges answer deletion and impact; backward edges answer root\ncause; the trace join links a user-visible answer into the graph."},"interview":[{"q":"What is data lineage, and why must it be captured at run time?","a":"- **Definition:** a directed graph of versioned datasets and job runs, with read and write edges and facets\n  such as code commit and row counts.\n- **Why run time:** code and DAG files describe what could run; only events emitted by the run record which\n  input versions were actually read and what was written, including failed partial runs."},{"q":"A customer invokes right to erasure. Walk me through finding every copy of their data.","a":"- **Start forward from the source records** at every version, following writes to chunks, vectors, caches,\n  eval examples and fine-tuning rows.\n- **Include request-path artifacts:** memories, semantic-cache entries and saved answers, which only appear\n  if their input ids were recorded at write time.\n- **Stop at weights honestly:** lineage names the checkpoints trained on a snapshot containing the data;\n  deletion policy decides whether to retrain or retire them."},{"q":"How do lineage and provenance differ, and when do you need both?","a":"- **Lineage** is the platform-wide derivation graph, queried by traversal.\n- **Provenance** is a verifiable, often signed, record attached to one artifact.\n- **Both** when you ship models or datasets to others: provenance proves what one artifact is, and the\n  lineage graph finds every other artifact sharing its inputs."},{"q":"Record-level lineage at billions of rows: is it worth it?","a":"- **For retrieval, fine-tuning and outputs, yes:** it is the only granularity that answers deletion and\n  citation questions.\n- **For the warehouse, usually no:** table-level is enough, and the extra metadata fields per row are real\n  storage at that scale."},{"q":"How would you measure whether your lineage is actually trustworthy?","a":"- **Coverage:** fraction of jobs emitting run events with required facets, enforced in CI.\n- **Deletion drills:** pick a document, traverse forward, and verify every derived artifact is found;\n  a miss means an uninstrumented job or request-path write."}],"tradeoffs":["**Exact deletion and citation vs storage.** Record-level lineage makes deletion and citation exact, but adds a handful of metadata fields to every chunk and row; at billions of records the storage is real, and table-level may be all the warehouse side needs.","**Accuracy vs instrumentation effort.** Run-time capture is accurate but requires every job to be instrumented. One uninstrumented job creates a hole exactly where you later need it, so coverage is worth enforcing in CI.","**Cross-team impact analysis vs another system to run.** A full lineage catalog serves many teams, but a single lineage table owned by the AI platform is enough until multiple teams need to query it.","**Precise reproduction vs pipeline changes.** Versioned dataset nodes need immutable snapshots or content hashes for every input, which changes how pipelines write data; mutable tables with no snapshot are the enemy."],"pitfalls":["**Recording dataset names instead of dataset versions.** Symptom: a graph that says \"the training job read the events table\" and cannot tell you which rows were present. Fix: version nodes.","**Documenting lineage in a diagram or README rather than emitting it from jobs.** Symptom: a graph that is confidently wrong six weeks later. Fix: run-time events.","**Storing chunk metadata without the ingest run id or source version.** Symptom: being unable to explain why two retrievals of the \"same\" page differ after a re-index.","**Treating the serving trace and the ingestion lineage as separate systems.** Symptom: a citation that cannot be linked to a specific document version when a user disputes it. Fix: log chunk ids on the trace.","**Skipping lineage for manual one-off fixes** (\"I just patched that table by hand\"). Symptom: an unexplained discontinuity nobody can reproduce. Fix: run manual fixes as recorded jobs.","**Instrumenting batch pipelines but not request-path writes** such as memories, caches and saved answers. Symptom: a deletion drill that finds every chunk yet misses the summaries that quote the document."],"when":"- **Mandatory when** a system must honour deletion requests, must explain its outputs, or has more than one\n  pipeline feeding retrieval or training, which describes nearly every production AI system.\n- **Start with the minimum:** a run table with inputs, outputs, versions and code commit, plus source and\n  run identifiers on every chunk.\n- **Adopt a catalog** when more than one team needs the graph.\n- **Measure:** coverage (fraction of jobs emitting lineage), and test it with a deletion drill: pick a\n  document, traverse forward, and verify every derived artifact is found.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you handle a right-to-erasure request across a RAG and fine-tuning stack\" is\n  answered by describing forward traversal over a lineage graph; without it the honest answer is \"we could\n  not\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments ingestion and training jobs to emit run events with versions and facets, and stamps every chunk with its source version and ingest run id.","architect":"Decides the lineage granularity per data domain, picks declarative versus run-time capture and the catalog, and makes lineage coverage a release requirement.","fde":"Uses the graph to answer a customer's auditor or DPO: which of their records are in which indexes and datasets, and proves deletion with a forward traversal."},"check":{"q":"Retrieval quality for one product area dropped sharply after last night's pipeline runs. Nothing was\ndeployed. How does lineage help you find the cause, and what would you need to have recorded?","a":"Take a failing query, find the chunks that should have been retrieved, and walk backward: each chunk\nrecords its source document version, parser version, chunker version, embedding version and ingest run.\nCompare those facets against the same chunks from the last good index. If the source version changed, the\nupstream content moved or was reformatted; if parser or chunker versions changed, a dependency upgrade\nslipped in without a deploy of your code; if the embedding version changed, a model revision moved under\nyou; if only the run differs, compare run facets such as row counts and schema fingerprints to find a\npartial or failed load. For this to work, jobs must emit run events with code and container versions and\nper-dataset statistics, and chunks must carry their run and source versions. Without versions, you only\nknow that \"ingestion ran\", which is where the investigation would stall."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must lineage nodes be dataset versions rather than dataset names?","options":["Names are not unique across teams","A job reads a specific state of a dataset, and reproduction or deletion requires knowing which state","Versions are easier to store in a graph database","Names change too often to be reliable identifiers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"Job J read table T\" cannot answer which rows were present or let you rebuild the output. Recording the snapshot or content hash as the node makes the graph a recipe rather than a sketch."},{"q":"Which lineage traversal answers \"which artifacts must be purged when this document is deleted\"?","options":["Backward traversal from the index to the document","Forward traversal from the document version to everything derived from it","A search of the vector index for similar content","A diff of the last two ingestion runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deletion propagation starts from the source and follows writes downstream: chunks, vectors, caches, eval examples, fine-tuning rows. Backward traversal explains a single artifact; similarity search would miss derived data that no longer resembles the original."},{"q":"Why is lineage captured by jobs at run time preferred over a maintained diagram of the pipeline?","options":["Diagrams cannot represent cycles in the pipeline","Run-time capture is cheaper to implement","A diagram describes the intended graph; run-time events describe what actually ran, with which versions","Regulators require machine-readable formats"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Declared lineage drifts the moment someone changes a job, and it never knows which input version a given run used. Events emitted as a side effect of doing the work are accurate by construction."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-contracts","sources":["Official docs: Confluent Schema Registry","Official docs: Great Expectations"],"why":"- **Upstream changes break AI pipelines silently.** The ticketing team renames `resolution_text` to\n  `resolution` and starts writing `category` as an integer id instead of a label. Nothing errors. The\n  chunker reads the missing field as an empty string and emits empty chunks; the filter on\n  `category == \"billing\"` silently matches nothing; the fine-tuning set gets three weeks of examples with\n  blank targets.\n- **Detection is late and slow.** The first signal is a product manager asking why answers got worse, and\n  the investigation takes days because the producer did not know anyone depended on those fields.\n- **Data has no type-checked boundary.** A typed API client breaks at compile time or returns a 400. Data\n  pipelines do not, *because* data flows through files and tables with no type-checked boundary, and\n  *because* consumers are typically discovered after the fact.\n- **AI makes it worse.** Embeddings and gradients absorb any input, so bad data becomes a slightly wrong\n  vector or weight, invisible until an evaluation runs, if one runs.\n- **Consumer-side validation alone fails three ways.** It runs after the bad data was written, so the\n  producer still has to be found and persuaded. Every consumer writes its own checks and they disagree. And\n  semantic changes (the same column now in cents instead of dollars) pass schema checks entirely. A contract\n  makes the producer own a published, versioned spec enforced where the change is made.","how":"### What a contract contains\n1. **Schema.** Field names, types, nullability and allowed values, machine-readable (JSON Schema, Avro,\n   Protobuf, or a Pydantic or dataclass model that generates one). Catches renames and type changes.\n2. **Semantics.** What each field means, its unit, timezone and reference data: `amount` is \"USD, two\n   decimal places, net of tax\". Prose plus, where possible, checks: ranges, regexes, cross-field rules such\n   as `closed_at >= opened_at`.\n3. **Quality guarantees.** Null rates, key uniqueness, referential integrity, row-count bounds per\n   partition, and distribution expectations that catch \"all categories are suddenly 0\".\n4. **Freshness and delivery.** How often data lands, maximum lag, partitioning, and how late data is\n   handled.\n5. **Ownership and change policy.** Who owns the dataset, how changes are proposed, what counts as breaking,\n   and how long a deprecated version stays available.\n6. **Version.** The contract has a version and the data carries it, so consumers know which contract a\n   record was written under.\n7. **Known consumers.** Which pipelines depend on which fields, so the producer's CI can tell the author of\n   a breaking change whom it will break. Derive this from lineage (a forward traversal from the dataset),\n   because hand-kept lists miss the newest consumer, which is usually the AI pipeline.\n\n- **Format matters less than discipline.** The schema usually uses an existing format; the rest often lives\n  in a YAML spec beside it. Open specifications for the whole document exist (the Open Data Contract\n  Standard is one), but what matters is that it is versioned, reviewed and machine-checked.\n\n### Where enforcement lives\nEnforce at the **producer boundary**, *because* a failure there is attributed correctly and happens before\nany consumer reads bad data.\n\n| Producer type | Enforcement point |\n|---|---|\n| Service | validates each record against the schema before publishing |\n| Streaming platform | schema registry rejects incompatible schema versions at registration time (Confluent's registry has backward, forward and full compatibility modes for exactly this) |\n| Batch pipeline | quality suite (Great Expectations or a hand-rolled equivalent) on the output partition; refuses to mark it complete if checks fail |\n\n- **Violations go to a dead-letter location** with the violation attached: not silently dropped, not passed\n  through.\n- **Consumers still validate,** but against the same published contract, so the two sides cannot disagree\n  about what valid means.\n- **The contract lives in version control.** A CI check diffs proposed changes and classifies them as\n  compatible (adding an optional field) or breaking (removing or renaming a field, narrowing a type,\n  changing a unit). Breaking changes need a new major version and a migration window during which both\n  versions are produced.\n\n### Batch-level circuit breaker\nPer-record dead-lettering needs a batch-level threshold beside it.\n\n| Violation rate | Likely cause | Right action |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.1% of a partition | scattered bad records | quarantine those rows, publish the rest |\n| 40% of a partition | a systematic upstream change | fail the whole partition, publish nothing |\n\n- **Why fail everything at 40%:** publishing the surviving 60% hands consumers a biased sample that looks\n  valid, for example a training set missing every ticket from one product.\n- **So the contract states a violation-rate threshold,** *because* \"no new data today\" is a loud, safe\n  failure and \"a skewed subset\" is a silent one.\n\n### Compatibility rules\nThe two directions are easy to swap, so use the schema-registry definitions:\n\n| Mode | Meaning | Lets you | Permits |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| *Backward* | a consumer on the new schema can read data written with the old one | upgrade consumers first | deleting fields; adding fields that have defaults |\n| *Forward* | data written with the new schema can be read by consumers still on the old one | upgrade producers first | adding fields; deleting only fields that had defaults |\n| *Full* | both | either order | the intersection |\n\n- **Renaming a field breaks both directions.**\n- **Forward matters most for AI consumers** fed by an independent producer, *because* the producer ships\n  first and a chunker or feature pipeline deployed last month must keep working on today's data.\n- **Backward matters** when you deploy a new consumer and reprocess history written under older schemas.\n- **Write the direction into the contract explicitly,** since teams outside the streaming world often use\n  the words the other way round.\n\n### Contracts specific to AI pipelines\n- **Text fields intended for embedding:** encoding, language tag, maximum length, and whether the field may\n  contain PII (which decides redaction before embedding).\n- **Metadata used for filtering:** the exact value set for `tenant_id`, `access_scope`, `product`, since a\n  filter on a value that stopped being produced returns nothing with no error.\n- **Labels for fine-tuning or evaluation:** the label taxonomy version and the annotation guideline version,\n  because a change in labeling policy is a semantic break even when the schema is unchanged.\n- **Timestamps for point-in-time joins:** which timestamp is event time versus ingestion time, because\n  confusing them creates leakage in training data.\n\n### Testing the contract\n1. **Producer CI** runs the validation suite against sample output.\n2. **Consumer CI** runs its pipeline against fixture data generated from the contract.\n3. **A compatibility check** compares the proposed contract version against the current one.\n\nThis is the data equivalent of contract testing between services.","example":"### Contract as code with producer-side dead-lettering\nA Pydantic model carrying schema and semantic checks, a contract version embedded in every record, and a\nproducer-side gate that routes violations to a dead-letter list instead of publishing them.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError, model_validator\n\nCONTRACT_VERSION = \"support_tickets.v2\"\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    contract_version: Literal[\"support_tickets.v2\"] = CONTRACT_VERSION\n    ticket_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^T-\\d{6,}$\")\n    tenant_id: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    category: Literal[\"billing\", \"login\", \"bug\", \"feature\", \"other\"]   # closed value set\n    opened_at: datetime\n    closed_at: datetime | None = None\n    resolution: str | None = Field(default=None, max_length=8000)       # text that will be embedded\n    language: Literal[\"en\", \"de\", \"fr\"] = \"en\"\n    contains_pii: bool                                                  # decides redaction before embedding\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def semantics(self):                    # tz check first: naive-vs-aware compare raises TypeError\n        if any(t is not None and t.tzinfo is None for t in (self.opened_at, self.closed_at)):\n            raise ValueError(\"timestamps must be timezone-aware (UTC)\")\n        if self.closed_at and self.closed_at < self.opened_at:\n            raise ValueError(\"closed_at before opened_at\")\n        if self.closed_at and not self.resolution:\n            raise ValueError(\"closed ticket must carry a resolution\")\n        return self\n\ndef publish(raw_records: list[dict]):\n    good, dead = [], []\n    for r in raw_records:\n        try:\n            good.append(Ticket(**r).model_dump())\n        except ValidationError as e:\n            dead.append({\"record\": r, \"violations\": e.errors(include_url=False)})\n    return good, dead\n\nnow = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\ngood, dead = publish([\n    {\"ticket_id\": \"T-000123\", \"tenant_id\": \"acme\", \"category\": \"billing\",\n     \"opened_at\": now, \"closed_at\": now, \"resolution\": \"Refunded.\", \"contains_pii\": False},\n    {\"ticket_id\": \"T-000124\", \"tenant_id\": \"acme\", \"category\": 3,       # upstream changed to int ids\n     \"opened_at\": now, \"contains_pii\": False},\n])\nprint(len(good), \"published;\", len(dead), \"dead-lettered:\", dead[0][\"violations\"][0][\"msg\"])\n```\n\nThe second record is the silent failure from the \"Why it exists\" section, now caught at the producer with\na message naming the field, before the embedding job ever sees it.\n\n### Batch-level circuit breaker on a partition\nRuns after per-record validation. The 1% threshold and the 10-point distribution tolerance are illustrative\nvalues a contract would state explicitly.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nMAX_VIOLATION_RATE = 0.01      # contract: above 1% the whole partition fails\n\ndef gate_partition(good: list[dict], dead: list[dict], baseline: dict[str, float]):\n    total = len(good) + len(dead)\n    rate = len(dead) / total if total else 1.0\n    if rate > MAX_VIOLATION_RATE:\n        return \"FAIL\", f\"violation rate {rate:.1%} > {MAX_VIOLATION_RATE:.0%}; nothing published\"\n    share = Counter(r[\"category\"] for r in good)\n    for cat, expected in baseline.items():              # distribution check on what survives\n        seen = share[cat] / len(good)\n        if abs(seen - expected) > 0.10:\n            return \"FAIL\", f\"category {cat!r} share {seen:.0%} vs expected {expected:.0%}\"\n    return \"PUBLISH\", f\"{len(good)} rows published, {len(dead)} quarantined ({rate:.1%})\"\n\nbaseline = {\"billing\": 0.4, \"login\": 0.3, \"bug\": 0.3}\ngood = [{\"category\": c} for c in [\"billing\"] * 400 + [\"login\"] * 300 + [\"bug\"] * 299]\nprint(gate_partition(good, [{}], baseline))            # 1 of 1,000 bad: publish\ngood = [{\"category\": c} for c in [\"login\"] * 300 + [\"bug\"] * 300]\nprint(gate_partition(good, [{}] * 400, baseline))      # every billing row broke upstream: fail\n```\n\n| Partition | Dead-lettered | Outcome | Why it is right |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1,000 rows, 1 bad | 0.1% | publish 999 | scattered noise, consumers lose nothing material |\n| 1,000 rows, all 400 billing rows bad | 40.0% | publish nothing | the 600 survivors contain zero billing tickets, a silently skewed sample |","patterns":["**Registry-enforced compatibility on streams.** Use for Kafka topics feeding embedding or feature pipelines. Set the subject's compatibility mode in Confluent Schema Registry (its config endpoint accepts `{\"compatibility\": \"FORWARD\"}` or `\"FULL\"`) so incompatible schemas are rejected at registration. Monitor rejected registrations; each one is a breaking change caught before production.","**Contract diff in producer CI.** Use for every contract file in version control. A CI step compares the proposed schema with the current one, labels the change compatible or breaking, and blocks breaking changes without a major version bump and listed consumer sign-off. Monitor breaking changes per quarter.","**Dead-letter queue with rate and age alerts.** Use with any per-record validation. Write violations with the record and the error list, alert on a change in rate (not only its level) and on the oldest unprocessed item's age. Monitor both on the producer team's dashboard, not only the AI team's.","**Partition circuit breaker.** Use for batch partitions feeding training or indexes. Fail the whole partition above the contract's violation-rate threshold, and check category distribution on survivors. Monitor partitions failed by the threshold.","**Tolerant readers on consumers.** Use when producers may add fields. In Pydantic, `model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\")` ignores unknown fields while required fields stay required, which is the \"ignore unknown fields, forbid missing ones\" compromise. Monitor unknown-field counts as an early signal of an upcoming contract change.","**Dual-production migration window.** Use for breaking changes. The producer emits old and new fields (or topics) with the version stamped on each record until consumers migrate; CI prevents dropping the old field early. Monitor reads of the old field to know when the window can close."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[\"Producer writes records\"] --> V{\"Record valid vs contract?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| DL[(\"Dead-letter with violations\")]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| B[\"Partition buffer\"]\n    B --> G{\"Violation rate under threshold?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| F[\"Fail partition, alert producer\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| Q{\"Quality and distribution checks pass?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no\"| F\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| PUB[\"Publish with contract_version\"]\n    PUB --> C1[\"RAG chunker and filters\"]\n    PUB --> C2[\"Fine-tuning set\"]\n    CI[\"CI: diff contract, classify change\"] -.->|\"breaking needs new major\"| P","caption":"Every record and every partition passes the contract at the producer; consumers only ever see data that\npassed."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a data contract, and how is it different from schema validation?","a":"- **A contract is broader:** schema plus semantics (units, timezones, meaning), quality guarantees,\n  freshness, ownership, change policy and a version carried by every record.\n- **Why it matters:** schema validation passes a dollars-to-cents switch; a contract's semantic checks and\n  version bump catch it."},{"q":"An embedding job started producing empty chunks last week with no errors. How do you investigate and prevent a repeat?","a":"- **Investigate:** compare recent source records with older ones for renamed or retyped fields, such as\n  `resolution_text` becoming `resolution`; the chunker read a missing field as an empty string.\n- **Prevent:** a producer-side contract that requires the field, dead-letters violations, and fails the\n  partition above a violation-rate threshold, plus a CI diff that flags the rename as breaking."},{"q":"Backward versus forward compatibility: which do you enforce for a feed into your RAG pipeline?","a":"- **Forward,** because the independent producer ships first and last month's chunker must still read\n  today's data.\n- **Add backward** if you will deploy new consumers that reprocess history; renames break both, so they\n  always need a new major version and a migration window."},{"q":"When would you publish a partial partition, and when would you fail it entirely?","a":"- **Publish** when violations are scattered, such as 0.1%, quarantining the bad rows.\n- **Fail** when the rate signals a systematic change, such as 40%, because the surviving rows are a biased\n  sample; \"no new data today\" is the safer, louder failure."},{"q":"Which fields in an AI-facing contract do teams most often forget?","a":"- **PII flags on text that will be embedded,** which decide redaction.\n- **Closed value sets for filter metadata** like `tenant_id` and `access_scope`.\n- **Label taxonomy and guideline versions,** and which timestamp is event time versus ingestion time, to\n  avoid leakage."}],"tradeoffs":["**Correct attribution vs organisational buy-in.** Producer-side enforcement attributes failures correctly and stops bad data early, but asks the producing team to own a specification. Without buy-in, consumer-side checks are the realistic fallback.","**Safe filters vs category churn.** A closed value set for categorical fields makes filters safe, but every new business category needs a contract change. If categories churn weekly, use an open set plus a reference table the contract points to.","**No drift vs rejecting harmless additions.** Strict schemas that reject unexpected fields prevent drift but also reject harmless additions; \"ignore unknown fields, forbid missing ones\" is the usual compromise.","**Repairable data vs silent loss.** Dead-lettering preserves data for repair, but a dead-letter queue nobody monitors is silent data loss; alert on its rate and age.","**Safe breaking changes vs doubled output.** Dual-production windows make breaking changes safe but double the producer's output for the window. For high-volume streams, keep the window short and the migration automated."],"pitfalls":["**Validating only schema and not semantics.** Symptom: model quality drops with no schema errors, traced weeks later to a unit or label-taxonomy change. Fix: range and unit checks plus taxonomy versions.","**Enforcing the contract only in the consumer.** Symptom: the AI team discovers every upstream change and argues for a fix after the damage is done. Fix: producer-side gate.","**Omitting the contract version from the records themselves.** Symptom: a reprocessing job that cannot tell which rules applied to which historical rows.","**Treating filter metadata as free text.** Symptom: `tenant_id` values with different casing or whitespace making ACL filters leak or return nothing. Fix: closed or normalised value sets.","**Silently dropping violating records to keep the pipeline green.** Symptom: row counts drift down and a training set over-represents whatever happened to validate. Fix: dead-letter and alert.","**Making every field required to be safe.** Symptom: the producer defaults fields to placeholder values just to pass, which is worse than null.","**Dead-lettering per record with no batch-level threshold.** Symptom: a partition that publishes on schedule with a fraction of its usual rows and a quietly shifted category distribution."],"when":"- **Use for any dataset crossing a team boundary** into an embedding, fine-tuning, feature or evaluation\n  pipeline; these consumers fail silently, so they benefit most.\n- **Defer when** the same team produces and consumes the dataset: in-pipeline validation is enough until a\n  second consumer appears.\n- **Measure:** dead-letter rate (alert on its change, not only its level), partitions failed by the batch\n  threshold, time from an upstream change to detection (should be minutes, at the producer), and breaking\n  changes per quarter.\n- **Day to day:** the contract is the file you open before adding a metadata filter or a training feature.\n- **Interviews:** \"how do you keep upstream schema changes from degrading your model\" is answered with\n  producer-side contracts, compatibility checks in CI and versioned records, not with \"we validate inputs\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the contract as a typed model, wires validation and dead-lettering into the producer, and generates fixtures from the contract for consumer tests.","architect":"Decides which datasets require contracts, the compatibility policy and versioning scheme, and the organisational rule that producers own their published data.","fde":"Negotiates the contract with a customer's data owners during onboarding, including PII flags and tenant scoping, so the pipeline is protected from their internal changes."},"check":{"q":"A customer's CRM team wants to change the `region` field from free text to a code list and add a\n`segment` field next month. Your RAG metadata filters and a fine-tuned router depend on `region`. How do you\nhandle the change?","a":"Classify the two changes. Adding `segment` as an optional field with a default is compatible in both\ndirections and can ship under the current contract version once documented. Changing `region` from free text to codes is a\nbreaking semantic change even though the type stays string: existing filters compare against the old\nvalues and the router was trained on them. So it requires a new major contract version. Agree a migration\nwindow in which the producer emits both `region` (old values) and `region_code` (new), with the version\nstamped on each record. During the window, update the filter logic to use codes, rebuild the index\nmetadata, and retrain or remap the router with the new values, validating on the eval set. Run the\ncompatibility check in CI so the old field cannot be dropped before consumers have migrated, and dead-letter\nany record whose `region_code` is outside the agreed list."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are AI pipelines more exposed to upstream data changes than typical application code?","options":["They process more rows per second than applications","Embeddings and training absorb malformed inputs without errors, so damage appears only as degraded quality","They usually run in a different programming language from the producer","Their schemas change more frequently"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A typed application fails fast on a renamed field. An embedding job turns an empty string into a vector and a training job fits whatever labels it is given, so the only symptom is a worse index or model, discovered late."},{"q":"Why is the contract enforced at the producer rather than only in consumers?","options":["Producers have more compute available for validation","Consumers cannot parse schema files","A failure at the producer is attributed to the change that caused it and happens before any bad data is read","It is required by schema registries"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Producer-side enforcement makes the person who changed the data see the failure immediately. Consumer checks still matter, but they run after the bad data exists and leave the AI team to track down the cause."},{"q":"Which change passes a schema check yet should be treated as a breaking contract change?","options":["Adding a new optional field with a default value","Widening a string field's maximum length","Switching an amount column from dollars to cents while keeping its type","Adding a new enum value to a category list that consumers treat as open"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The type is unchanged, so schema validation passes, but every consumer's interpretation is now wrong by a factor of 100. Semantic checks (ranges, units documented and tested) and a version bump are what catch and manage this class of change."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/model-and-dataset-provenance","sources":["Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting","Gebru et al., 2021 — Datasheets for Datasets","Official docs: Hugging Face Hub"],"why":"- **The questions arrive months later.** A fine-tune lands in the registry as `router-v4.safetensors`.\n  Six months on: Legal asks about non-commercial data sources; a customer asks whether their data was used;\n  the eval team suspects the benchmark leaked; Security asks whether the base model came from the official\n  repository or a lookalike; an engineer wants to reproduce the run to fix a regression.\n- **Only build-time records answer them,** and only if someone recorded the inputs precisely and made the\n  record tamper-evident.\n- **After-the-fact model cards and READMEs fail,** because they are prose written from memory: what the\n  author believes the inputs were, not what the job read. \"The cleaned support tickets\" folder has since had\n  rows added and removed; a base model pulled by name resolves to a different revision today. Prose cannot\n  be diffed, verified or traced.\n- **\"Git is enough\" fails,** because code is one of six inputs. The data snapshot, base model revision,\n  environment (library and CUDA versions), hyperparameters and random seed all change outputs, and none\n  lives in the training script.\n- **What provenance is:** the discipline of capturing all of them as checkable identifiers at the moment\n  the artifact is created.","how":"### Content addressing: identity by hash\n- **Identify by bytes, not by name or path.** A dataset snapshot is the sorted list of its files with their\n  SHA-256 digests, hashed again into one dataset digest (a Merkle-style root). A model artifact is its file\n  digests.\n- **Why:** a hash is the only identifier stable across copies, it cannot drift when someone edits the\n  folder, and it lets a reader verify they hold the same bytes.\n- **Pin hub revisions.** Hugging Face model repositories are git repositories, so a commit hash identifies a\n  revision exactly. Always pin `revision` rather than relying on the default branch, which moves.\n\n### The build manifest\nEvery training or fine-tuning run writes a manifest beside its output:\n\n| Section | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| Base model | repository id and revision hash, plus file digests |\n| Datasets | each snapshot digest, row counts, filter or sampling parameters used |\n| Code | commit hash, container or environment digest, library versions |\n| Parameters | the full hyperparameter set and the seed |\n| Outputs | checkpoint digests, eval results with the eval set's own digest |\n| Actor and time | who or what launched the run, and when |\n\n- **Merged, distilled and adapter models list every parent** as an input, each with its own manifest\n  digest, *because* a merge has several ancestors and the licence and contamination questions must be\n  answered for all of them.\n- **Completeness beats format.** Software supply-chain attestation formats (in-toto and SLSA-style\n  provenance) and bill-of-materials formats with ML profiles (CycloneDX and SPDX both have them) are being\n  adopted so registries and scanners can read manifests without bespoke parsers.\n- **The job writes it, not a person,** *because* that is the only way it describes what ran. Store it in\n  the model registry as the artifact's metadata, and commit it where the lineage system can link it to\n  dataset versions.\n\n### Dataset documentation\n- **Datasheets (Gebru et al.)** formalize what a dataset should answer: motivation, composition, collection\n  process, preprocessing, intended uses, distribution and maintenance.\n- **The operational subset for an AI team:** source systems and their terms, consent or licence basis, PII\n  handling applied, the filtering recipe and its parameters, known gaps, and the eval sets that must never\n  overlap with it.\n- **Store it next to the snapshot digest** as a structured file, so documentation and bytes are versioned\n  together.\n\n### Model documentation\n- **Model cards (Mitchell et al.)** cover intended use, out-of-scope use, training data summary, evaluation\n  results by relevant slices, limitations and ethical considerations.\n- **Two layers:** the provenance manifest is the machine-readable backbone; the model card is the\n  human-readable layer that references it.\n- **Regulation increasingly expects both.** The EU AI Act, for example, requires technical documentation\n  covering data and evaluation for high-risk systems, and for general-purpose model providers technical\n  documentation plus a public summary of training content. Templates, codes of practice and application\n  dates are still being phased in, so check current obligations rather than relying on a summary.\n\n### Synthetic and model-generated data\n- **A generated dataset has more inputs than its seed data:** the generator model and revision, the prompt\n  template version, the sampling parameters and the filter that kept or discarded outputs. Record all of\n  them.\n- **Why each matters:** the generator's errors and biases propagate into your model; a change of generator\n  explains a change of downstream quality; and the generator's usage terms travel with its outputs.\n- **Terms are a counsel question:** some providers' terms restrict using outputs to train competing models,\n  and the manifest must make that answerable.\n\n### Licence and terms tracking\n- **Each dataset and base model carries its licence identifier** in the manifest.\n- **Derived artifacts inherit combined obligations,** computed by walking the lineage graph, *because*\n  publishing a fine-tune under a permissive licence does not release it from the base model's terms (many\n  model licences explicitly bind derivatives).\n- **Licences are not a simple ranking.** Two inputs can impose different, even incompatible, obligations,\n  and whether a dataset licence reaches the trained weights is a legal question. The graph surfaces every\n  applicable term for counsel; it does not decide.\n- **Customer isolation is enforced here:** the manifest of any shared model must show no\n  customer-restricted dataset digests among its inputs.\n\n### Integrity and supply chain\n- **A hash says what you have; a signature says who produced it.** Signing artifacts and manifests\n  (Sigstore-style keyless signing is one approach being adopted for ML artifacts) lets a deployer verify a\n  checkpoint came from the registry's build pipeline, not a laptop or a tampered download.\n- **Prefer `safetensors` over pickle-based formats,** because loading a pickle executes arbitrary code\n  embedded in the file. A signed safetensors file is both authentic and inert.\n- **Record dependency digests too,** since a training run is only as trustworthy as the libraries that ran\n  it.\n\n### Using provenance\n- **Contamination check:** hash the eval set's examples and the training snapshot's examples; exact overlap\n  is found by digest comparison without re-reading the pipeline. Hashes only catch exact copies, so\n  normalize text before hashing and add n-gram or embedding-similarity checks for reformatted or paraphrased\n  leaks.\n- **Data subject requests:** the dataset snapshot digests in a model's manifest, joined with lineage, answer\n  whether a person's records contributed to a model.\n- **Reproduction:** the manifest is the command line: same base revision, snapshot, commit, parameters and\n  seed. Bitwise identity is not guaranteed on GPUs, but the result should land within the known run-to-run\n  variance.\n- **Incident response:** when a model misbehaves, compare its manifest with the last good one; the diff is\n  the list of suspects.","example":"### A manifest writer for a fine-tuning run\nIt hashes the dataset snapshot and base model files, captures code and environment identifiers, and writes\na JSON manifest beside the checkpoint. The training call is a stub; everything around it is what\nprovenance requires.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, subprocess, sys, platform, time\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef file_digest(p: Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with p.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\ndef tree_digest(root: Path) -> dict:\n    files = sorted(p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file())\n    per_file = {str(p.relative_to(root)): file_digest(p) for p in files}\n    root_hash = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(per_file, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    return {\"root\": root_hash, \"files\": len(per_file), \"per_file\": per_file}\n\ndef git_commit() -> str:\n    return subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], text=True).strip()\n\ndef train(out_dir: Path, params: dict) -> Path:       # stand-in for the real trainer\n    out_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    ckpt = out_dir / \"model.safetensors\"\n    ckpt.write_bytes(b\"weights-placeholder\")\n    return ckpt\n\ndef run(base_model: dict, dataset_dir: Path, eval_dir: Path, out_dir: Path, params: dict):\n    data, evalset = tree_digest(dataset_dir), tree_digest(eval_dir)\n    overlap = set(data[\"per_file\"].values()) & set(evalset[\"per_file\"].values())\n    if overlap:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"eval files present in training snapshot: {len(overlap)}\")\n    ckpt = train(out_dir, params)\n    manifest = {\n        \"base_model\": base_model,                       # {\"repo\": ..., \"revision\": <commit hash>}\n        \"datasets\": [{\"path\": str(dataset_dir), \"digest\": data[\"root\"], \"files\": data[\"files\"],\n                      \"license\": \"customer-restricted:acme\"}],\n        \"eval_set_digest\": evalset[\"root\"],\n        \"code\": {\"commit\": git_commit(), \"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"platform\": platform.platform()},\n        \"params\": params,\n        \"outputs\": {\"checkpoint\": str(ckpt), \"digest\": file_digest(ckpt)},\n        \"created_at\": time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\", time.gmtime()),\n    }\n    (out_dir / \"manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n    return manifest\n```\n\nThe overlap check is deliberately crude (whole-file hashes); a real contamination check hashes individual\nexamples or n-grams. The licence field on the dataset is what later blocks this checkpoint from being\npromoted to a shared, multi-customer deployment.\n\n### Example-level contamination check: exact digests plus n-grams\nThe step the crude file check above defers. Normalizing before hashing catches reformatted copies; shared\nn-grams catch light paraphrases. The 8-token n-gram size is an illustrative choice; tune it on your data.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, re\n\ndef normalize(text: str) -> str:\n    return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", re.sub(r\"[^\\w\\s]\", \"\", text.lower())).strip()\n\ndef digest(text: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(normalize(text).encode()).hexdigest()\n\ndef ngrams(text: str, n: int = 8) -> set[tuple[str, ...]]:\n    toks = normalize(text).split()\n    return {tuple(toks[i:i + n]) for i in range(len(toks) - n + 1)}\n\ndef contamination(train: list[str], evalset: list[str], n: int = 8, min_shared: int = 1):\n    train_digests = {digest(t) for t in train}\n    train_grams = set().union(*(ngrams(t, n) for t in train))\n    exact = [e for e in evalset if digest(e) in train_digests]\n    near = [e for e in evalset if e not in exact and len(ngrams(e, n) & train_grams) >= min_shared]\n    return exact, near\n\ntrain = [\"How do I reset my password? Go to Settings, then Security, then Reset.\",\n         \"Refunds are issued to the original card within five business days of approval.\"]\nevalset = [\"how do I reset my password?  go to settings, then security, then reset\",   # reformatted copy\n           \"Refunds are issued to the original card within five business days, per policy.\",\n           \"What is the API rate limit for the free tier?\"]\nexact, near = contamination(train, evalset)\nprint(len(exact), \"exact;\", len(near), \"near-duplicate;\", len(evalset) - len(exact) - len(near), \"clean\")\n# 1 exact; 1 near-duplicate; 1 clean\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: diffing manifests after a regression\n`router-v4` routes worse than `router-v3` in production. Diff the two manifests (illustrative values):\n\n| Field | router-v3 | router-v4 | Suspect? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Base model revision | commit e1f… | commit e1f… | no |\n| Training snapshot digest | 9ab… (120k rows) | c42… (131k rows) | yes: 11k new rows |\n| Synthetic subset generator | model A, prompt v2 | model B, prompt v2 | yes: generator changed |\n| Code commit | 5d0… | 5d0… | no |\n| Seed and params | identical | identical | no |\n| Eval set digest | 77c… | 77c… | no, scores are comparable |\n\n- **Two suspects, both in data:** retrain v4's recipe with the old generator's subset to isolate the\n  effect. Without the generator recorded, \"the same\" synthetic dataset would look unchanged.","patterns":["**Pin every hub download to a commit.** Use for all base models and hub datasets. Pass `revision=\"<commit sha>\"` to `from_pretrained` or `huggingface_hub.snapshot_download`, and record that sha in the manifest. Monitor any training config that resolves a branch name instead of a commit.","**Manifest required for registry promotion.** Use for every artifact that reaches a registry. Attach the job-written manifest to the registered model (in MLflow, for example, `mlflow.log_dict(manifest, \"manifest.json\")` on the run), and have the promotion step reject artifacts without one. Monitor coverage: fraction of registry artifacts with complete manifests.","**Safetensors-only loading.** Use for any third-party or shared checkpoint. In Transformers pass `use_safetensors=True` so a pickle file is refused rather than executed. Monitor registry artifacts still stored as pickle-based formats.","**Sign at build, verify at deploy.** Use for artifacts that cross trust boundaries. Sign the manifest and checkpoint in the build pipeline (Sigstore's `cosign sign-blob` is one option), and verify signature and digest before the serving process loads weights. Monitor verification failures; each is a blocked tampered or out-of-band artifact.","**Licence and isolation gate in CI.** Use before promoting shared models. Walk the manifest closure and fail if any input carries a customer-restricted tag or an unreviewed licence. Monitor gate failures and time to counsel review.","**Contamination gate before training.** Use whenever eval sets exist. Run the digest and n-gram overlap check between the training snapshot and every registered eval set, and record the result in the manifest. Monitor overlap counts per run."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Base model at pinned revision\"] --> H[\"Hash every input\"]\n    D[\"Dataset snapshot\"] --> H\n    G[\"Code commit and container digest\"] --> H\n    H --> C{\"Eval overlap or licence conflict?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| X[\"Block the run\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| T[\"Train with params and seed\"]\n    T --> K[\"Checkpoint safetensors\"]\n    K --> M[\"Job writes manifest with all digests\"]\n    M --> S[\"Sign manifest and checkpoint\"]\n    S --> R[(\"Model registry\")]\n    R -->|\"deploy verifies signature and digest\"| P[\"Serving\"]","caption":"Every input is reduced to a digest before training; gates run on the digests, and only a signed manifest\nlets the checkpoint into the registry."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a model card and a provenance manifest?","a":"- **Manifest:** machine-written at build time, with digests of the base model, data snapshots, code,\n  environment, params, seed and outputs; it can be verified and diffed.\n- **Model card:** human-readable intended use, limitations and sliced eval results that reference the\n  manifest; written alone from memory it drifts from what actually ran."},{"q":"Your benchmark scores jumped 9 points after a data refresh. How do you check for contamination?","a":"- **Exact overlap:** normalize and hash every eval example and every training example, then intersect the\n  digests.\n- **Near overlap:** n-gram or embedding-similarity checks catch reformatted and paraphrased leaks that\n  hashes miss.\n- **Then diff manifests:** the new snapshot digest and any changed synthetic-data generator are the\n  suspects."},{"q":"A merged model has three parents. How do you decide whether it can be deployed commercially?","a":"- **Walk the closure:** every parent's manifest, recursively, down to base models and datasets with their\n  licence identifiers.\n- **Surface, do not rank:** obligations can be different or incompatible, so the tooling lists every\n  applicable term and counsel decides; a permissive licence on the merge does not override a parent's terms."},{"q":"When is signing artifacts not worth the cost?","a":"- **Throwaway experiments** that never reach a registry can skip it; they should still write the manifest.\n- **Anything deployed or shared across trust boundaries should be signed,** because a hash alone cannot say\n  who produced the file."},{"q":"Can you reproduce a fine-tune bit for bit from its manifest?","a":"- **Usually not on GPUs:** non-deterministic kernels mean bitwise identity is not guaranteed.\n- **But you can land within known run-to-run variance** using the same base revision, snapshot, commit,\n  params and seed; a result outside that variance points at an unpinned input."}],"tradeoffs":["**Exact inputs vs immutable storage.** Content-addressed snapshots make inputs exact but require immutable copies or a lakehouse with snapshot isolation; hashing a mutable table \"as of now\" is cheap and nearly useless.","**Full digests vs scan cost.** Per-file digests for large corpora cost a scan per run. For multi-terabyte datasets, hash partition manifests the lakehouse already maintains rather than every byte again.","**Tamper evidence vs key management.** Signing adds key management and a verification step at load time; skip it only for throwaway experiments that never reach a registry.","**Rich datasheets vs staleness.** Datasheets improve downstream decisions but are written by people and go stale. Keep the machine-written manifest authoritative and the prose as commentary on it.","**Reproducibility vs automatic patches.** Pinning base model revisions means security patches to a model repo are not picked up automatically; make upgrading the revision an explicit, evaluated change."],"pitfalls":["**Referencing the base model by name without a revision.** Symptom: a reproduction attempt silently uses a newer upstream commit and lands outside the expected variance. Fix: pin the commit.","**Writing the model card from memory after the run.** Symptom: documented training data does not match the snapshot the job actually read. Fix: generate the card from the manifest.","**Hashing a dataset directory that was later edited in place.** Symptom: a manifest digest that matches nothing on disk when an audit asks for the bytes. Fix: immutable snapshots.","**Loading pickle-format checkpoints from an unverified source.** Symptom: arbitrary code execution at load time, usually discovered by a security review rather than by the model misbehaving.","**Forgetting licence propagation through fine-tunes and merges.** Symptom: a commercial deployment of a model whose ancestor forbids it. Fix: compute obligations over the full closure.","**Recording eval results but not the eval set digest.** Symptom: scores that cannot be compared across runs because the set quietly changed.","**Recording a synthetic dataset's digest but not the generator model and prompt version.** Symptom: a quality shift after regenerating \"the same\" dataset that nobody can explain."],"when":"- **Record provenance for every artifact that reaches a registry:** every fine-tune, merged model, dataset\n  snapshot used for training or evaluation, and eval set.\n- **Notebook experiments** can skip signing but should still capture the manifest, since the one that works\n  will be promoted.\n- **Measure:** coverage (fraction of registry artifacts with complete manifests), reproduction success rate\n  on a sampled run, and time to answer \"what was this trained on\".\n- **Day to day:** the manifest is what you read before deploying someone else's checkpoint.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you prove your model was not trained on the benchmark\" is answered with dataset\n  and eval digests and an overlap check, not with \"we were careful\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes the training job write the manifest, pins base model revisions and dependency digests, and adds the contamination and licence checks as pipeline gates.","architect":"Defines what a complete manifest contains, requires it for registry promotion, and sets the licence inheritance and customer-data isolation rules the manifests enforce.","fde":"Shows a customer's compliance team the manifest proving which of their datasets touched which models, and that none reached a shared model."},"check":{"q":"A customer alleges that a shared model you deploy to several clients was trained on their\nproprietary documents. How do you establish the truth, and what must have been in place beforehand?","a":"Pull the deployed model's manifest from the registry and walk its ancestry: the fine-tune's dataset\nsnapshot digests, its base model revision, and recursively the manifests of any merged or distilled\ninputs. Each dataset snapshot carries a licence or restriction tag and a lineage link to its source\nsystems. If no snapshot in the closure carries the customer's restriction tag or derives from their source\nsystems, the manifests are strong evidence for the negative across everything your own pipelines fed in\n(a third-party base model's pretraining data is covered only by its vendor's documentation); if one does, you know exactly which run introduced it and which\nother models share that ancestor. For this to be credible, the manifests must have been written by the\njobs at build time with content hashes, signed so they cannot be edited afterwards, and stored with the\nartifact so the deployed digest can be matched to its manifest. Without that, the honest answer is \"we\nbelieve not\", which does not survive a dispute."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a dataset identified by a content hash rather than by its path or name?","options":["Hashes are shorter than paths","A hash is stable across copies and changes when any byte changes, so it can be verified and cannot drift","Paths are not allowed in model registries","Hashing is required by safetensors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A name points at whatever is currently in that location, which changes silently. A digest identifies the exact bytes, so a manifest naming it can be checked later and a reader can confirm they hold the same data."},{"q":"Which input is most often missing from \"we committed the training script, so it is reproducible\"?","options":["The programming language version","The name of the engineer who ran it","The exact data snapshot and base model revision the run consumed","The number of GPUs used"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Code is one input among several. The data snapshot and base model revision usually change the output more than anything in the script, and neither is captured by a commit unless the manifest records their digests."},{"q":"Why prefer safetensors over pickle-based checkpoint formats when loading third-party models?","options":["Safetensors files are smaller","Pickle cannot store floating-point tensors","Loading a pickle executes code embedded in the file, while safetensors is a pure data format","Safetensors includes the model card automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pickle deserialization runs arbitrary Python, so an unverified checkpoint is an executable. A data-only format combined with a verified digest or signature gives you an artifact that is both authentic and inert to load."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/data-retention-and-deletion-architecture","sources":["Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), Article 17 — Right to erasure","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"- **Law and contracts bound retention.** GDPR's right to erasure, similar rights under CCPA and other\n  regimes, and most enterprise contracts require personal or customer data to be kept no longer than needed\n  and deleted on request within a bounded time. Under GDPR a response is due without undue delay and within\n  one month, extendable by two further months for complex requests.\n- **The classic model does not fit.** For a web app, data lives in a database with backups, and deletion is\n  a `DELETE` plus a backup expiry policy. An AI application breaks that model.\n- **One conversation, eight persisted copies.** The message (name, order number) goes to the application\n  database; into a prompt; to a vendor whose API logs it; into a trace with full prompt and completion; into\n  a memory vector; into a semantic-cache entry; into a weekly eval set; into a fine-tuning dataset; and\n  partially into model weights.\n- **Spread across owners and companies.** Setting aside the transient prompt, that is eight persisted\n  copies in eight systems, owned by several teams, with at least one (vendor logs) outside the company, two\n  if observability is hosted.\n- **\"Delete the primary and let the rest expire\" fails,** because several copies have no expiry (vector\n  index, fine-tuning dataset, weights), and those that do often expire too late for the request deadline.\n- **\"Never store anything\" fails,** because observability, evaluation and improvement all depend on\n  retaining interactions. Retention must be a per-copy decision, and deletion a traversal that reaches every\n  copy.","how":"### Inventory and classify every copy\n- **Map where data lands.** For each store record the data classes it holds (user content, derived content\n  such as embeddings and summaries, operational metadata, aggregate metrics), whether it is keyed by a\n  subject identifier, and who owns it.\n- **Sources of the map:** the lineage system supplies most of it for derived data; the rest (vendor logs,\n  caches, backups) is documented by hand.\n- **Why classify:** retention is set per class. An aggregate latency metric can live for years; a raw prompt\n  with a customer's address should not.\n\n### Retention classes and TTLs\n- **Each store gets a policy:** a time-to-live after which data is deleted automatically, or \"retain while\n  the relationship exists\".\n- **Derived data inherits the shortest TTL of its inputs,** *because* an embedding of a deleted message is\n  still a representation of it.\n- **Enforce TTLs mechanically:** database partitions dropped by date, object storage lifecycle rules, vector\n  collections partitioned by ingestion month, trace stores with built-in retention. Policy that depends on\n  someone remembering to run a cleanup is not a policy.\n\n### Retention minimums and legal holds\n- **Retention has a floor as well as a ceiling.** Some data must be kept for a minimum period (financial\n  records, security audit logs, records a regulator requires).\n- **Legal holds** from litigation can freeze data that would otherwise expire or be erased.\n- **Erasure has exceptions.** GDPR's erasure right excludes, among others, data needed to comply with a legal\n  obligation or to establish or defend legal claims.\n- **The deletion job consults a hold register first:** a held record is skipped and reported as held, not\n  deleted and not silently ignored.\n- **Conflicts are for counsel.** Resolving floor versus ceiling versus hold versus erasure is a policy\n  decision; the architecture makes the rule explicit and executable rather than deciding it.\n- **Vendors are a store too.** Model API providers typically retain request logs for abuse monitoring\n  unless a zero-retention arrangement is in place. Terms vary by provider and contract, so record what the\n  agreement is and whether it covers the data classes you send.\n\n### Deletion as a propagated job\nA deletion request (one subject, one tenant, or one document) becomes a job that:\n1. Resolves the subject to identifiers in every store, using lineage to find derived artifacts: the\n   document's chunks, their vectors, cache entries that embedded them, eval examples sampled from them,\n   fine-tuning rows that include them.\n2. Issues deletes to each store, idempotently, with retries, and records a per-store receipt.\n3. Verifies by re-querying each store for the identifiers.\n4. Writes a tombstone so re-ingestion from a still-live source does not resurrect the data, and so a\n   later audit can show when and where deletion happened.\n\n- **Idempotent and resumable,** *because* it fans out across many systems and some will be down.\n- **Partial completion must be visible,** not silently treated as done.\n\n### Store-specific mechanics\n| Store | Mechanism | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vector indexes | delete by metadata (`subject_id`, `document_id`), so those fields must exist on every vector | soft-delete markers until compaction, segment merge or rebuild: the vector may remain on disk after it stops being returned |\n| Caches | key entries so a subject's data can be found, or a TTL short enough that expiry alone meets deadlines | semantic and exact-match caches hold full prompts and completions |\n| Traces and logs | redact PII before the trace is written, or key by subject with a short retention | redaction at write time is the more robust choice |\n| Backups, immutable stores | short rotation plus a deletion log, or crypto-shredding | you cannot delete one row from a backup |\n| Summaries and memories | record source conversations and subjects; delete or regenerate on erasure | one summary can blend several people's data |\n| Training datasets | rows carry subject or tenant identifiers | the snapshot digest changes, which flows into provenance |\n| Model weights | keep deletable data out, time-box models, or retrain | cannot delete from weights |\n\n- **Vector indexes:** treat the index as a derived store that can be rebuilt from the source of truth, and\n  schedule rebuilds so soft-deleted vectors actually leave disk.\n- **Backups, the simpler pattern:** backups age out within a defined window, and any restore replays the\n  recorded deletions before the data goes live again.\n- **Backups, the stronger pattern (crypto-shredding):** encrypt each subject's (or tenant's) data under its\n  own key, and delete the key. The ciphertext remains but is unrecoverable, widely treated as meeting the\n  intent of erasure for backups and append-only logs. Regulators have not uniformly codified that view, so\n  confirm the approach with your privacy counsel.\n- **Summaries and memories:** an LLM-written summary or agent memory is a new document that cannot be\n  filtered row by row, so erasing one subject means deleting it or regenerating it from the remaining inputs.\n- **Model weights:** machine unlearning techniques exist but are not reliable enough to certify. The\n  architectural answers: keep deletable data out of training (redact, or train only on data with consent\n  terms that survive deletion requests); time-box models so a retrain on the cleaned dataset happens within\n  the deadline; or accept retraining as the deletion mechanism and budget for it.\n\n### Tenant-level deletion\n- **Partition every store by tenant** (separate collections, prefixes or keys). Enterprise offboarding then\n  becomes dropping a partition instead of a filtered scan, which is faster and more verifiable.\n- **Per-tenant encryption keys** make the backup problem disappear for the same reason as crypto-shredding.\n\n### Proof\n- **The job's output is a report:** request id, a keyed reference to the subject, each store touched,\n  receipts, verification results, and the tombstone id.\n- **Why the job must produce it:** regulators and customers ask for this, and the job is the only thing that\n  can produce it accurately.","example":"### A deletion orchestrator with retries, verification and receipts\nIt fans out over registered stores, retries, verifies, and produces a receipt. Stores are small stand-ins;\nthe shape of the job is what matters.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\nTOMB_KEY = b\"from-a-secret-store\"                       # tombstones must not hold the raw id\ndef tomb_id(subject): return hmac.new(TOMB_KEY, subject.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\n@dataclass\nclass Store:\n    name: str\n    rows: dict = field(default_factory=dict)           # id -> {\"subject\": ..., ...}\n    flaky: int = 0                                     # simulate transient failures\n    def find(self, subject):\n        return [i for i, r in self.rows.items() if r[\"subject\"] == subject]\n    def delete(self, ids):\n        if self.flaky: self.flaky -= 1; raise ConnectionError(self.name)\n        for i in ids: self.rows.pop(i, None)\n\nclass KeyVault(Store):                                  # crypto-shredding for backups\n    def find(self, subject): return [subject] if subject in self.rows else []\n\ndef erase(subject: str, stores: list[Store], tombstones: set, max_tries=3) -> dict:\n    receipt = {\"subject_ref\": tomb_id(subject)[:16], \"stores\": {}, \"complete\": True}\n    for s in stores:\n        ids = s.find(subject)\n        for attempt in range(1, max_tries + 1):\n            try:\n                s.delete(ids); break\n            except ConnectionError:\n                time.sleep(0)                           # backoff in real code\n        remaining = s.find(subject)\n        ok = not remaining\n        receipt[\"stores\"][s.name] = {\"found\": len(ids), \"verified\": ok, \"attempts\": attempt}\n        receipt[\"complete\"] &= ok\n    tombstones.add(tomb_id(subject))                    # blocks re-ingestion from live sources\n    receipt[\"tombstone\"] = tomb_id(subject) in tombstones\n    return receipt\n\napp_db   = Store(\"app_db\",   {\"m1\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"text\": \"order 991\"}, \"m2\": {\"subject\": \"u7\"}})\nvectors  = Store(\"vectors\",  {\"c1\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"doc\": \"m1\"}}, flaky=1)\ncache    = Store(\"sem_cache\",{\"k9\": {\"subject\": \"u42\"}})\nbackups  = KeyVault(\"backup_keys\", {\"u42\": {\"subject\": \"u42\", \"key\": \"...\"}})\ntomb = set()\nprint(erase(\"u42\", [app_db, vectors, cache, backups], tomb))\n# ingestion computes tomb_id(subject) and skips any record whose id is in `tomb`\n```\n\nThe `complete` flag is what an audit reads; a `False` means the job must be rerun or escalated, never\nquietly closed. In production the store list comes from the inventory, and the derived-artifact ids come\nfrom a forward lineage traversal rather than a per-store scan.\n\n### Worked scenario: does expiry alone meet a one-month deadline?\nAn inventory for the support assistant above, with illustrative TTLs. The deadline is the GDPR one month.\n\n| Store | TTL | Expiry meets deadline? | Action on erasure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Application database | while relationship exists | no | explicit delete |\n| Traces (PII redacted at write) | 14 days | yes, and nothing personal is stored | none needed |\n| Semantic cache | 7 days | yes | optional explicit delete |\n| Vendor API logs | per contract (zero-retention or provider default) | only if the contract says so | record terms; request deletion if retained |\n| Memory vectors | none | no | delete by `subject_id`, rebuild on schedule |\n| Weekly eval set | none | no | remove rows, new snapshot digest |\n| Fine-tuning dataset | none | no | remove rows, new snapshot digest |\n| Last month's checkpoint | until replaced | no | retrain on cleaned snapshot or retire |\n| Backups | 35-day rotation | no, 35 days exceeds one month | replay deletion log on restore, or crypto-shred |\n\n- **Reading it:** five stores need explicit action and one needs a retrain. Shortening the backup rotation\n  well under one month, or crypto-shredding, removes one more row of manual work.\n\n### Production snippet: TTL by lifecycle rule\nObject storage enforces the trace-export TTL mechanically, so nobody has to remember a cleanup job.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Rules\": [\n    {\n      \"ID\": \"expire-trace-exports\",\n      \"Filter\": { \"Prefix\": \"traces/\" },\n      \"Status\": \"Enabled\",\n      \"Expiration\": { \"Days\": 14 }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","patterns":["**Redact before the trace is written.** Use whenever full prompts reach observability. Run a PII detector (Microsoft Presidio is a common open-source choice) in the app or in an OpenTelemetry Collector processor that deletes or hashes span attributes, so the trace store holds nothing deletable. Monitor the detector's hit rate and sample traces for missed PII.","**Mechanical TTLs per store.** Use for every store with a retention class. S3-style lifecycle expiration rules for exports, time-partitioned Postgres tables dropped by date (`DROP TABLE` on the old partition), and the trace backend's own retention setting. Monitor the number of stores with no automated TTL; the target is zero outside \"retain while relationship exists\".","**Metadata-keyed vector deletes plus scheduled rebuilds.** Use for every RAG or memory index. Store `subject_id`, `tenant_id` and `document_id` on each point and delete by filter (Qdrant deletes points by payload filter; in pgvector it is a plain `DELETE ... WHERE subject_id = ...` followed by vacuum). Rebuild or compact on a schedule so soft-deleted vectors leave disk. Monitor deleted-but-not-compacted counts.","**Per-tenant envelope encryption.** Use for multi-tenant enterprise products. Each tenant's data key is wrapped by a KMS key; offboarding deletes the tenant's key, shredding backups and logs. Monitor key-to-tenant coverage and key deletion events.","**Tombstone check at ingestion.** Use wherever a crawl or sync can re-read deleted data. Ingestion computes the keyed hash of each record's subject and skips matches. Monitor tombstone hits per sync; hits mean the source still holds the data and the owner should be told.","**Quarterly deletion drill.** Use for every regulated deployment. Erase a synthetic test subject seeded into every store, and require a `complete: True` receipt within the target time. Monitor request-to-verified completion time."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Erasure request for subject\"] --> H{\"On legal hold?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| HR[\"Skip, report as held\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| L[\"Resolve ids via inventory and lineage\"]\n    L --> S1[(\"App DB and traces\")]\n    L --> S2[(\"Vectors and caches\")]\n    L --> S3[(\"Eval and training sets\")]\n    L --> S4[\"Backup keys: crypto-shred\"]\n    S1 --> V{\"Re-query: all gone?\"}\n    S2 --> V\n    S3 --> V\n    S4 --> V\n    V -->|\"no\"| RT[\"Retry or escalate, complete false\"]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Write keyed tombstone and receipt\"]","caption":"An erasure request fans out to every store found by inventory and lineage; holds are checked first, and only\nverified deletes count toward a complete receipt."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do AI applications need a dedicated deletion architecture when web apps get by with DELETE and backup expiry?","a":"- **Copies multiply:** one conversation lands in roughly eight persisted stores, several owned by other teams\n  and one outside the company.\n- **Several never expire:** vector indexes, training datasets and weights persist until something deletes\n  them, so deletion has to be a propagated, verified job."},{"q":"A user deleted last month reappears in search results. What went wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the source system still holds the record and the next crawl re-ingested it, because\n  ingestion does not check tombstones.\n- **Other suspects:** chunks deleted only from the primary, or soft-deleted vectors restored by a rebuild\n  from a stale snapshot.\n- **Fix:** keyed-hash tombstones checked at ingestion, deletion propagated via lineage, and a drill that\n  re-runs a crawl after erasure."},{"q":"Per-subject or per-tenant encryption keys for crypto-shredding: which do you choose?","a":"- **Per-tenant** is far cheaper and makes offboarding a key deletion, but cannot shred one person.\n- **Per-subject** covers individual erasure in backups but adds key management and a lookup on every read.\n- **Common compromise:** per-tenant keys plus short backup rotation and deletion-log replay for individuals."},{"q":"How do you handle an erasure request when the data is already in model weights?","a":"- **Be honest:** unlearning is not reliable enough to certify.\n- **Options:** retire the checkpoint and retrain on the cleaned snapshot within the deadline, or show that\n  training data was redacted so no personal data entered the weights.\n- **Design for it:** time-boxed models with an on-demand retrain path."},{"q":"What would you measure to prove the deletion system works?","a":"- **Request-to-verified-completion time** across all stores, against the one-month deadline.\n- **Stores with no automated TTL,** and the share of derived artifacts reachable through lineage.\n- **Quarterly drills** with a synthetic subject, requiring a complete receipt."}],"tradeoffs":["**Easy deadlines vs debugging window.** Short TTLs on traces and caches make deletion deadlines trivial but shrink the window for debugging and evaluation; the balance shifts toward longer retention once PII is redacted at write time.","**Clean backup deletion vs key management.** Crypto-shredding handles backups and immutable logs cleanly, but per-subject keys add a key-management system and a lookup on every read. Per-tenant keys are far cheaper and cover offboarding, but cannot shred one person inside a tenant, so per-subject erasure then falls back to backup expiry plus replay of the deletion log on restore.","**No unlearning problem vs useful training data.** Keeping personal data out of fine-tuning avoids the unlearning problem entirely, but some use cases need it; then retraining on a schedule is the cost of being able to honour deletions.","**Partition drops vs collection sprawl.** Partitioning every store by tenant makes offboarding a partition drop, but multiplies collections and indexes. For thousands of small tenants, logical partitioning with tenant keys plus filtered deletes is more practical.","**Evidence vs load.** Verification by re-query adds load and time to each deletion job, but a deletion without verification is an assertion, not evidence; skip it only for stores with transactional guarantees."],"pitfalls":["**Vectors stored without subject or document identifiers.** Symptom: a deletion request that requires a full index rebuild because nothing can be targeted.","**Treating \"the source document was deleted\" as \"the chunks are gone\".** Symptom: retrieval still citing a page that no longer exists. Fix: propagate via lineage.","**Relying on ANN soft deletes.** Symptom: an audit finding the bytes still on disk months after a confirmed deletion. Fix: scheduled compaction or rebuild.","**Full prompts in traces with multi-year retention.** Symptom: the observability stack becomes the largest PII store in the company and the hardest to delete from. Fix: redact at write time.","**Re-ingestion resurrecting deleted data** because the source system still has it. Symptom: a user deleted last month reappears in the index after the next crawl. Fix: tombstone check at ingestion.","**Forgetting the vendor.** Symptom: a data-processing audit asks for the provider's retention terms and nobody knows whether zero-retention was ever enabled.","**Deletion jobs that report success on partial completion.** Symptom: a later discovery in a store that was down during the run. Fix: per-store verification and a `complete` flag.","**Tombstones and receipts that store the raw email or user id.** Symptom: a deny-list that is itself a growing register of exactly the people who asked to be forgotten. Store a keyed hash instead.","**Deleting data under legal hold** because the erasure job never consults the hold register. The symptom surfaces in litigation, which is the worst place to discover it."],"when":"- **Required wherever** personal, customer-confidential or regulated data enters prompts, indexes or\n  training sets: most enterprise deployments and any consumer product.\n- **Design it before the first index exists,** because adding subject identifiers to vectors and tenant\n  partitioning to collections after the fact is a full rebuild.\n- **Measure:** time from deletion request to verified completion across all stores, number of stores with no\n  automated TTL, and percentage of derived artifacts reachable through lineage.\n- **Drill:** run deletion drills quarterly.\n- **Interviews:** \"a user invokes their right to erasure; walk me through what happens in your RAG system\"\n  should produce the store inventory, the propagated job with receipts, the tombstone, and an honest\n  statement about model weights.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds subject and tenant identifiers to every vector, cache entry and training row, builds the idempotent deletion job with receipts, and wires tombstone checks into ingestion.","architect":"Owns the store inventory and retention classes, chooses crypto-shredding and partitioning strategies, and decides whether personal data may enter training at all.","fde":"Maps a customer's data-protection requirements onto the stack, confirms the vendor's retention terms match their contract, and runs a deletion drill with their privacy officer before go-live."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant fine-tunes a model monthly on sampled conversations, and a user invokes their right to\nerasure. The conversations are already in last month's checkpoint. What do you do, and how should the\nsystem have been designed?","a":"Immediately run the propagated deletion for all deletable copies: application records, traces,\nvector memories, caches, the eval and fine-tuning datasets (producing a new dataset snapshot digest), and\nrecord a tombstone and receipt. For the checkpoint, be honest: the data cannot be surgically removed from\nweights with certifiable reliability. The options are to retire the checkpoint and retrain on the cleaned\nsnapshot within the deadline, which the monthly cadence makes feasible if the retrain can be triggered on\ndemand, or to show that the training data was redacted so no personal data was ever in the weights. The\nsystem should have been designed to make one of those true by construction: redact PII before sampling\ninto training data, carry subject identifiers on every training row, keep models time-boxed with an\non-demand retrain path, and document in the vendor agreement what the provider retains. Deletion proof is\nthe job's receipt plus the provenance manifest of the replacement model showing the cleaned snapshot."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"delete from the primary database and let everything else expire\" insufficient for an LLM application?","options":["Primary databases cannot delete rows that are referenced by foreign keys","Several derived copies, such as vectors, fine-tuning data and weights, have no expiry at all","Expiry policies are not permitted under privacy regulation","The primary database is usually not the system of record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vector indexes, training datasets and model weights persist indefinitely unless a deletion mechanism is designed for them. Expiry works only for stores that have a TTL, and even those may expire after the request deadline."},{"q":"How does crypto-shredding make deletion possible in backups and append-only logs?","options":["It compresses the backup so the deleted rows are overwritten","It rewrites the backup without the subject's rows","Data is encrypted per subject or tenant, and deleting the key makes the remaining ciphertext unrecoverable","It moves the subject's rows to a separate archive with shorter retention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Immutable stores cannot have individual rows removed. Encrypting under a per-subject key and destroying the key leaves only unreadable ciphertext without modifying the store, which is why it is the common pattern for backups. Compression or a separate archive still leaves the data readable."},{"q":"In the propagated deletion job, what is the tombstone written in the final step for?","options":["To mark the subject's rows as pending so a nightly job can remove them later","To prevent re-ingestion from resurrecting deleted data and to record that deletion occurred","To hold the subject's data for the appeal period before final deletion","To signal the model vendor to purge their logs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Source systems or crawls may still contain the data; the tombstone tells ingestion to skip it and gives auditors a record of what was deleted and when. It is a deny-list, not a holding area."}]},{"key":"data-architecture/feature-stores","sources":["Official docs: Feast"],"why":"- **Caveat: hybrid systems only.** Feature stores matter in systems that train and serve classical ML models\n  (ranking, fraud, churn, routing classifiers) alongside LLM components. Pure LLM-application roles can skim\n  this and move on.\n- **Two implementations drift (training/serving skew).** A fraud model needs \"transactions by this card in\n  the last hour\" and \"customer's average order value over 90 days\". A batch SQL job computes them for the\n  training set; a service computes them at request time.\n- **The two silently disagree.** The batch job counts refunds, the service does not; the batch job uses UTC\n  day boundaries, the service local time. The model learns one definition and is scored with another, so\n  production accuracy is lower than in evaluation for reasons nobody can see.\n- **Joins to today's values leak the future (time leakage).** Training rows must join features *as they\n  were when each label's event happened*. Join to today's values and the \"90-day average\" for a transaction\n  last March includes orders from April. Offline metrics look excellent, production is worse.\n- **Hand-rolled joins go wrong at scale.** Doing this join correctly by hand across dozens of features with\n  different update cadences is where most hand-rolled pipelines fail.\n- **A shared library plus a cache is not enough.** It fixes definition drift but not leakage, stores no\n  history, and cannot say \"which feature version did model v12 train on\". The feature store adds a historical\n  store, point-in-time joins, versioned definitions and an online serving path: the minimum set that solves\n  both problems.","how":"### Entities, features and feature views\n- **A feature** is a named, typed value attached to an **entity** (a customer id, a card id, a merchant id)\n  with an **event timestamp** saying when the value became true.\n- **A feature view** (Feast's term; other systems say feature groups or tables) is a set of features for one\n  entity, computed by one transformation from one source, with a TTL saying how long a value stays valid.\n- **The definition lives in code under version control,** *because* it must be the single source of truth\n  for both the batch and the serving path.\n\n### The offline store\n- **Historical feature values,** keyed by entity and event timestamp, in the warehouse or lakehouse.\n- **Append-only by design:** a new value is a new row with a later timestamp, not an overwrite, *because*\n  training needs every past value to reconstruct what was known at any given moment.\n\n### The point-in-time join\nGiven a label table with `(entity_id, label_timestamp, label)`, for each row and each feature view:\n1. Select the latest value with `event_timestamp <= label_timestamp`.\n2. Require `label_timestamp - event_timestamp <= ttl`.\n3. If no value qualifies, the feature is null: the honest answer, since at that time the model would not have\n   known it.\n\n- **This \"as-of\" join is the core algorithm** and the reason the offline store keeps history. Feast exposes\n  it as `get_historical_features`.\n\n### The boundary must match what serving sees\nThe `<=` in the join is a decision, not a detail.\n\n| Step | Training (inclusive join) | Serving |\n|---|---|---|\n| Feature | transactions by this card in the last hour | same |\n| Label event | a transaction at 14:00:00 | the same transaction, being scored |\n| What the count includes | the pipeline already counted it in a value stamped 14:00:00 | the scorer runs *before* the transaction is recorded, so never itself |\n| Result | count includes the very event being scored | count is exactly one lower, systematically |\n\n- **This is leakage that looks like a rounding error.** Choose strict or inclusive comparison, and any\n  allowance for ingestion delay, to reproduce what the online path can know at decision time.\n\n### Backfills\n- **A new feature is useless for training until it has history.** A *backfill* runs the transformation over\n  historical source data to populate the offline store as if the feature had always existed.\n- **Only possible with historical sources** (an event log or a snapshot table).\n- **If the source is a mutable table keeping only current state,** history cannot be reconstructed. The\n  honest options: start logging the feature now and wait for enough labelled history, or accept a training\n  set where the feature is null before its launch date.\n- **So feature sources should be append-only events** wherever possible.\n\n### The online store\n- **A low-latency key-value store** (Redis, DynamoDB, Bigtable and similar) holding only the latest value per\n  entity per feature view. Serving reads it with a single lookup per entity.\n- **Populated by materialization:** a scheduled job copies the latest values from the offline store into the\n  online store, so both paths see the same computed numbers.\n- **A streaming path** writes to both stores from the event stream for features that must be fresher than the\n  materialization cadence (counts in the last minute).\n\n### Why one definition serves both paths\n- **Written once, executed twice:** in the batch engine for history, and in the materializer or stream\n  processor for serving. Skew is prevented structurally rather than by code review, *because* there is no\n  second implementation to drift.\n- **Request-payload features have two options.** Run the same transformation code at request time and at\n  training time (Feast's on-demand feature views work this way), or log the values actually served and reuse\n  them as training features later, closing the loop.\n- **Logging is safer** when the request-time inputs cannot be reconstructed from history.\n\n### Versioning and lineage\n- **Feature view definitions are versioned,** and a training run records which versions and which offline\n  snapshot it used, which plugs directly into provenance.\n- **Changing semantics means a new version;** both versions can be served during a model migration.\n- **Statistics computed at materialization** (null rates, distributions) are what drift monitoring compares\n  against.\n\n### Where it sits in a hybrid AI system\n- **Typical shapes:** a ranking or routing model decides which documents or which LLM an agent should use; a\n  fraud or risk score gates whether an LLM-generated action executes; a churn model decides which users get\n  an LLM-drafted outreach.\n- **The LLM gets the score or the decision,** not the features, which come from the feature store.\n- **Embeddings as features:** a user or item embedding fed to a ranker makes the embedding model's version\n  part of the feature view's definition. Changing it is a new feature version and a retrain, for the same\n  reason it forces a re-index in retrieval.\n- **No such models?** Then the analogous concerns (consistent metadata between ingestion and query time,\n  versioned transformations) are handled by the ingestion contract and embedding versioning, not a feature\n  store.\n\n### Products\n- **Feast** is the common open-source option (definitions in Python, pluggable offline and online stores).\n- **Managed or commercial:** Tecton, the Databricks feature store, Amazon SageMaker Feature Store, Vertex AI\n  Feature Store and Hopsworks. This market consolidates and renames products often, so check current\n  offerings.\n- **Differences** are mostly in streaming support, transformation execution and platform integration; the\n  concepts above are shared.","example":"### Point-in-time join versus the leaky latest-value join\nPure Python, contrasted with the \"join to the latest value\" that hand-rolled pipelines usually do. Feature\nrows are `(entity, event_time, value)`; labels are `(entity, label_time)`.\n\n```python\nfrom bisect import bisect_right\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# avg_order_value_90d for customer c1, appended as it changes (offline store rows)\nfeatures = [(\"c1\", \"2024-03-01\", 40.0), (\"c1\", \"2024-04-15\", 55.0), (\"c1\", \"2024-05-20\", 90.0)]\nlabels   = [(\"c1\", \"2024-03-10\"), (\"c1\", \"2024-04-20\"), (\"c1\", \"2024-06-01\")]\nTTL_DAYS = 120\n\nby_entity = defaultdict(list)\nfor ent, ts, val in sorted(features, key=lambda r: r[1]):\n    by_entity[ent].append((ts, val))\n\ndef days_between(a, b):                     # ISO dates, good enough for the demo\n    from datetime import date\n    return (date.fromisoformat(b) - date.fromisoformat(a)).days\n\ndef point_in_time(ent, label_ts):\n    rows = by_entity[ent]\n    i = bisect_right([ts for ts, _ in rows], label_ts) - 1      # latest event_time <= label_ts\n    if i < 0 or days_between(rows[i][0], label_ts) > TTL_DAYS:\n        return None                                              # unknown at that time\n    return rows[i][1]\n\ndef leaky_latest(ent, _label_ts):\n    return by_entity[ent][-1][1]                                 # what a naive join does\n\nfor ent, lts in labels:\n    print(lts, \"correct:\", point_in_time(ent, lts), \"| leaky:\", leaky_latest(ent, lts))\n# 2024-03-10 correct: 40.0 | leaky: 90.0\n# 2024-04-20 correct: 55.0 | leaky: 90.0\n# 2024-06-01 correct: 90.0 | leaky: 90.0\n```\n\nThe March label trained with the leaky join would see a value that only became true in May. A model fit\non such rows learns relationships that cannot exist at inference time, which is exactly why offline\nmetrics overstate production performance.\n\n### Production snippet: the same feature in Feast\nOne definition, applied with `feast apply`, then used for both a point-in-time training set and online\nserving. The entity dataframe must carry an `event_timestamp` column, which is the label time for the as-of\njoin.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom feast import Entity, FeatureStore, FeatureView, Field, FileSource\nfrom feast.types import Float32\n\ncustomer = Entity(name=\"customer\", join_keys=[\"customer_id\"])\norders = FileSource(path=\"data/customer_orders.parquet\", timestamp_field=\"event_timestamp\")\ncustomer_orders = FeatureView(\n    name=\"customer_orders\", entities=[customer], ttl=timedelta(days=120),\n    schema=[Field(name=\"avg_order_value_90d\", dtype=Float32)], source=orders,\n)\n\nstore = FeatureStore(repo_path=\".\")\nlabels = pd.DataFrame({\"customer_id\": [1001, 1001],\n                       \"event_timestamp\": pd.to_datetime([\"2024-03-10\", \"2024-04-20\"], utc=True)})\ntrain_df = store.get_historical_features(            # point-in-time correct, TTL applied\n    entity_df=labels, features=[\"customer_orders:avg_order_value_90d\"]).to_df()\n\nstore.materialize_incremental(end_date=datetime.now(timezone.utc))   # offline -> online\nonline = store.get_online_features(features=[\"customer_orders:avg_order_value_90d\"],\n                                   entity_rows=[{\"customer_id\": 1001}]).to_dict()\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the 0.95-offline, 0.80-production fraud model\nLogging served features and comparing them with batch values for the same entities and timestamps\n(illustrative findings):\n\n| Check | Finding | Class of bug | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| How the training set was built | labels joined to current values | time leakage | rebuild with a point-in-time join |\n| Refund handling | batch counts refunds, service does not | skew | one definition in the feature view |\n| Day boundaries | batch UTC, service local time | skew | one definition, UTC event time |\n| Last-hour count at the label event | logged serving values sit one below training values | inclusive-join leakage | strict comparison at the boundary |\n\n- **After the rebuild the offline score drops** toward production, which is the point: the evaluation now\n  measures the model production actually runs.","patterns":["**Feature definitions as reviewed code.** Use from the first shared feature. Keep feature views in a repo, run `feast apply` (or the managed store's equivalent) from CI, and require a new version for any semantic change. Monitor the fraction of model features with a single definition.","**Scheduled incremental materialization.** Use for every online feature view. Run `materialize_incremental` on a cadence shorter than the feature's freshness requirement. Monitor feature freshness at serve time (now minus the served value's event timestamp) and materialization job failures.","**Streaming push for second-level freshness.** Use only for features like last-minute counts. Write from the stream processor into both stores (Feast offers a push source for this). Monitor stream lag, because it becomes the feature's staleness.","**Served-feature logging and skew checks.** Use for every model in production. Log the feature vector with each prediction asynchronously (sample at high traffic), then join to batch-recomputed values for the same entity and timestamp. Monitor per-feature mismatch rate; a constant offset of one points at the join boundary.","**TTL on every feature view.** Use always. Set `ttl` to the longest staleness the model can tolerate, so dormant entities get nulls instead of year-old values. Monitor null rate per feature at training and serve time; a jump means a source stopped producing.","**Offline/online metric gap as the headline alert.** Use for every hybrid model. Track the difference between offline evaluation and production metrics per model version; skew or leakage shows up here first."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    DEF[\"Feature view definition in code\"] --> BATCH[\"Batch transform over event log\"]\n    DEF --> STREAM[\"Stream transform for fresh features\"]\n    BATCH --> OFF[(\"Offline store: full history\")]\n    STREAM --> OFF\n    STREAM --> ON[(\"Online store: latest value\")]\n    OFF -->|\"materialize latest\"| ON\n    LBL[\"Labels with timestamps\"] --> PIT[\"Point-in-time join\"]\n    OFF --> PIT\n    PIT --> TRAIN[\"Training set\"]\n    ON -->|\"one lookup per entity\"| SERVE[\"Model scoring\"]\n    SERVE -.->|\"log served values\"| OFF","caption":"One definition feeds both paths: history for point-in-time training joins, latest values for serving."},"interview":[{"q":"What is training/serving skew, and how does a feature store prevent it?","a":"- **Definition:** the model is trained on features computed one way and scored on features computed another,\n  such as refunds counted in batch but not in the service.\n- **Prevention is structural:** one feature view definition executes in the batch engine and in the\n  materializer or stream processor, so there is no second implementation to drift."},{"q":"Walk me through building a leakage-free training set for a churn model.","a":"- **Start from labels** with entity id and label timestamp.\n- **As-of join each feature view:** latest value with event time at or before the label time, within the TTL,\n  null otherwise.\n- **Check the boundary:** decide strict or inclusive comparison to match what serving can know at decision\n  time, and use event time, not ingestion time."},{"q":"Would you add a feature store to a pure RAG assistant?","a":"- **No:** with no classical model to train and serve, there is no skew or point-in-time problem to solve.\n- **Use instead** an ingestion contract for metadata consistency and embedding versioning for transformation\n  versions; a feature store would hold document metadata the ingestion pipeline already handles."},{"q":"A new feature looks valuable, but its source is a table that keeps only current state. What do you do?","a":"- **You cannot backfill it honestly,** because past values were overwritten.\n- **Options:** start logging the feature now and wait for labelled history, or train with the feature null\n  before its launch date; then move the source to append-only events."},{"q":"How do you choose a materialization cadence, and what do you monitor?","a":"- **Cadence follows the freshness the model needs:** hourly or daily for slow features; anything needing the\n  last few seconds goes through the streaming path instead.\n- **Monitor** serve-time freshness, materialization failures, null rates, and the offline-versus-online\n  metric gap per model version."}],"tradeoffs":["**Skew-proof by construction vs another platform.** A feature store removes skew and leakage by construction, but it is another platform to run and a new workflow for data scientists. Below a handful of models, a shared transformation library plus a careful as-of join in SQL is a reasonable interim.","**Millisecond lookups vs bounded freshness.** Materializing to an online store gives millisecond lookups but bounds freshness by the materialization cadence. Features that must reflect the last few seconds need the streaming path, which costs more to operate.","**Point-in-time joins vs unbounded storage.** Append-only history makes point-in-time joins possible but grows storage without bound; partition by time and expire beyond the longest TTL any model needs.","**Managed integration vs lock-in.** Managed stores integrate tightly with their platform and reduce operational work, but lock the feature layer to that platform; Feast keeps portability at the cost of assembling the pieces yourself.","**Closed loop vs write per prediction.** Logging served features closes the loop on online-only transformations, but adds a write per prediction to a read-only path; make it asynchronous and sample when traffic is high."],"pitfalls":["**Joining labels to current feature values.** Symptom: offline evaluation far better than production, with no bug anywhere in the model code. Fix: point-in-time join.","**Two implementations of the same feature for batch and serving.** Symptom: a production accuracy drop that disappears when the serving values are logged and compared against the batch ones. Fix: one definition.","**Setting no TTL on a feature view.** Symptom: a model happily consuming a value computed a year ago for a dormant entity as if it were current.","**Mixing event time and ingestion time.** Symptom: subtle leakage when late-arriving events get stamped with when they were loaded rather than when they happened.","**Adopting a feature store for an LLM-only application.** Symptom: an expensive platform holding document metadata that the ingestion pipeline already handled.","**Not recording feature view versions in the model's provenance.** Symptom: being unable to tell why model v12 and v13 disagree after a feature definition changed.","**An inclusive as-of join on a feature that counts the label event itself.** Symptom: a feature that is suspiciously predictive offline and whose logged serving values sit consistently one below the training values."],"when":"- **Use when** the system trains and serves more than a couple of classical models that share features, when\n  any feature is computed from time-varying history, or when training/serving skew has already been observed.\n- **Avoid when** the only models are LLMs consuming retrieved text; there the equivalent discipline lives in\n  ingestion contracts and embedding versioning.\n- **Measure:** the gap between offline and online metrics (skew or leakage shows up here first), feature\n  freshness at serve time, and the fraction of features with a single definition.\n- **Interviews:** for hybrid roles, expect to explain the point-in-time join and why the online and offline\n  stores must be fed from one definition; for LLM-application roles, expect at most to say why you do not\n  need one.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes feature views as code, implements the as-of join or uses the store's historical retrieval, and sets materialization schedules and TTLs per feature.","architect":"Decides whether the system is hybrid enough to justify a feature store, picks managed versus open source, and owns the freshness and consistency guarantees between offline and online paths.","fde":"Recognizes when a customer's \"AI system\" includes classical models with hand-rolled feature pipelines, and diagnoses skew and leakage before attributing problems to the LLM."},"check":{"q":"A customer's fraud model shows 0.95 AUC offline and behaves like 0.80 in production. They blame the\nmodel and want to add an LLM to \"explain\" transactions. What do you check first, and why?","a":"Check for time leakage and training/serving skew before touching the model. First, inspect how the\ntraining set was built: if labels were joined to current feature values rather than values as of each\ntransaction's timestamp, the offline score is inflated by information from the future; rebuild with a\npoint-in-time join and re-evaluate. Second, log the feature values the serving path actually sends and\ncompare them with the batch-computed values for the same entities and timestamps; any systematic\ndifference (refund handling, timezone boundaries, null defaults) is skew, and the model is being scored on\ninputs it never saw in training. Both are fixed by a single feature definition feeding an offline store\nwith history and an online store by materialization, which is what a feature store provides. An LLM\nexplanation layer would add cost and would explain decisions made on wrong inputs; it belongs, if at all,\nafter the inputs are right."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a point-in-time join guarantee when building a training set?","options":["Each label is matched to the most recently computed feature value","Each label sees only feature values whose event time is at or before the label's timestamp","Features are computed in the same timezone as the labels","Every label has a non-null value for every feature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The join reconstructs what was knowable when each label event occurred. Matching to the latest value leaks the future into training, and nulls are the correct outcome when no value existed yet or the TTL had expired."},{"q":"Why does a feature store keep separate offline and online stores rather than one database?","options":["Offline stores are cheaper, so all data should live there","Online stores can run SQL joins faster","Training needs full history for as-of joins while serving needs millisecond lookups of only the latest values","Regulations require training data to be isolated from production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The two access patterns conflict: history and heavy scans versus single-key latency. Materialization copies the latest values from the historical store to the key-value store so both paths see the same computed numbers."},{"q":"A team building a pure RAG assistant asks whether they need a feature store for document metadata. What is the right guidance?","options":["Yes, because metadata filters are features of the retrieval model","Yes, because the online store will speed up metadata lookups","No; without classical models to train and serve, the ingestion contract and embedding versioning cover their needs","Only if they use more than one embedding model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Feature stores solve training/serving skew and time leakage for models trained on tabular features. A RAG assistant has no such model; consistency between ingestion and query metadata is an ingestion contract concern, and transformation versioning is handled by the embedding recipe."}]}]});
